Smash Factory was a production team composed of [[T.I.]], and Grand Hustle in-house producers, [[1500 or Nothin'|Lamar Edwards]] (known as Mars or MyGuyMars) and [[Lil' C (record producer)|C Gutta]] (also known as Lil' C). The group has produced for the likes of [[Nelly]] and [[Young Dro]].<ref>[http://trackhustle.ning.com/m/blogpost?id=2214757%3ABlogPost%3A30340] {{Dead link|date=February 2022}}</ref>

== In-house producers ==
Grand Hustle Records has a production wing, which serves as a group of in-house producers for the label.

===Current===
* [[DJ Toomp|Aldrin "DJ Toomp" Davis]] – known for producing T.I.'s [[hit single]]s "[[24's (T.I. song)|24's]]", "[[Be Easy (T.I. song)|Be Easy]]", "[[U Don't Know Me (T.I. song)|U Don't Know Me]]" and "[[What You Know (T.I. song)|What You Know]]".
* [[Lil' C (record producer)|Cordale "Lil' C" Quinn]] – known for producing Young Dro's "[[Shoulder Lean]]" and "[[Rubberband Banks]]", [[Young Jeezy]]'s "[[My Hood]]" and T.I.'s "[[Wit Me]]".<ref name=MySpace/>
* [[Mars (record producer)|Lamar "Mars" Edwards]] – a member of production team [[1500 or Nothin']], known for producing [[Asher Roth]]'s "[[G.R.I.N.D (Get Ready It's a New Day)]]" and T.I.'s "[[Love This Life]]", among other songs from prominent artists.<ref name="smashfactory">{{Cite web |url=http://trackhustle.com/profiles/blogs/ti-explains-smash-factory |title=T.I. EXPLAINS SMASH FACTORY MUSIC PRODUCTION TEAM |date=October 18, 2011 |website=Trackhustle.com|access-date=March 20, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140702050103/http://trackhustle.com/profiles/blogs/ti-explains-smash-factory |archive-date=July 2, 2014 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Former===
* [[DJ Drama]] (2006–2011) – former resident disc jockey for the label
* [[Nard & B]] (2007–2016)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2016/12/28/nard-and-b-interview-future-beat-construction|title=Meet Nard & B, The Atlanta Producers Who Get Your Favorite Rappers In Their Feelings|website=The FADER}}</ref> – production duo composed of Bernard "Nard" Rosser and Brandon "B" Rackley. Known for producing several songs, including Yung L.A.'s "[[Futuristic Love (Elroy)]]", [[Slim Thug]]'s "[[So High (Slim Thug song)|So High]]", and co-producing [[Maino (rapper)|Maino]]'s "[[All the Above (Maino song)|All the Above]]", with high-profile producer [[Just Blaze]].<ref name="MySpace">MySpace. [http://www.myspace.com/grandhustlemusic List Of Artists] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091210043728/http://www.myspace.com/grandhustlemusic |date=2009-12-10 }} ''Grand Hustle Official MySpace''</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/454907-exclusive-interview-grand-hustle-production-duo-nard-and-b-news |title=Exclusive Interview: Grand Hustle Production Duo Nard and B |website=Hotnewhiphop.com |date=16 May 2012 |access-date=3 October 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170504114559/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/exclusive-interview-grand-hustle-production-duo-nard-and-b-news.2019.html |archive-date=4 May 2017 |url-status=live }}</ref>

