'''Fredrick Jamel Tipton''' (born June 14, 1982), better known by his stage name '''Freddie Gibbs''', is an American rapper. He signed with [[Interscope Records]] in 2006 and recorded his debut album for the label; however, its release was cancelled and he was dropped from the label due to executive shakeups. Gibbs later signed with [[Jeezy|Young Jeezy]]'s [[CTE World]] in 2011 and released a number of mixtapes through the label, including the acclaimed ''[[Baby Face Killa]]'' (2012).<ref name="Consequence of Sound">{{cite web|last=Bosman|first=Chris|url=https://consequence.net/2012/10/album-review-freddie-gibbs-baby-face-killa/|title=Album Review: Freddie Gibbs - BFK|date=October 5, 2012 |publisher=Consequence of Sound|access-date=July 20, 2013|archive-date=September 27, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130927164339/https://consequence.net/2012/10/album-review-freddie-gibbs-baby-face-killa/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="pitchfork1">{{cite web|url=http://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/17235-baby-face-killa/|title=Freddie Gibbs: BFK &#124; Album Reviews|publisher=[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]|date=July 10, 2013|access-date=July 28, 2013|archive-date=September 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929041125/https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/17235-baby-face-killa/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="pop matters">{{cite web|url=http://www.popmatters.com/review/163682-freddie-gibbs-baby-face-killa/|title=Freddie Gibbs - 'BFK' Album Review - Pop matters|publisher=Pop Matters|date=June 8, 2013|access-date=July 14, 2013|archive-date=September 28, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130928153303/http://www.popmatters.com/review/163682-freddie-gibbs-baby-face-killa/|url-status=live}}</ref>

After leaving CTE in early 2013, Gibbs formed the record label ESGN to release his [[ESGN|debut studio album of the same name]] in June of that year. His second and third albums, ''[[Shadow of a Doubt (album)|Shadow of a Doubt]]'' (2015) and ''[[You Only Live 2wice]]'' (2017), were both met with positive critical and lukewarm commercial reception—the latter contained no guest appearances. His commercial [[mixtape]], ''[[Freddie (Freddie Gibbs album)|Freddie]]'' (2018), was met with critical acclaim and led him to sign with [[Warner Records]] in 2020; his fourth album and [[major label]] debut, ''[[Soul Sold Separately]]'' (2022) peaked at number 11 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]]. His fifth studio album, ''[[You Only Die 1nce]]'' (2024), narrowly entered the chart. In addition, he has released five collaborative albums; two with [[Madlib]]: ''[[Piñata (Freddie Gibbs and Madlib album)|Piñata]]'' (2014) and ''[[Bandana (album)|Bandana]]'' (2019), and three with [[The Alchemist (musician)|the Alchemist]]: ''[[Fetti]]'' (2018, also with [[Currensy]]), ''[[Alfredo (album)|Alfredo]]'' (2020), and ''[[Alfredo 2]]'' (2025). ''Alfredo'' was nominated for [[Grammy Award for Best Rap Album|Best Rap Album]] at the [[63rd Annual Grammy Awards]].<ref name="Grammy">{{cite web|url=https://www.revolt.tv/news/2020/11/24/21642263/freddie-gibbs-reacts-to-grammy-nomination|title=Freddie Gibbs reacts to Grammy nomination: "I told yall niggaz"|last=Thorpe|first=Isha|work=[[Revolt (TV network)|Revolt]]|date=November 24, 2020|access-date=December 2, 2020}}</ref>

His guest appearance on [[¥$]] ([[Kanye West]] and [[Ty Dolla Sign]])'s 2024 song, "[[Back to Me (¥$ song)|Back to Me]]", peaked at number 26 on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] and became his first entry on the chart, though he remains uncredited on the official version.

==Early life==
Fredrick Tipton was born and raised in [[Gary, Indiana]], and grew up at 17th and Virginia St. on the city's east side.<ref>{{cite news|last=Weiss|first=Jeff|title=The Miseducation of Freddie Gibbs|work=LA Weekly|url=http://www.laweekly.com/2009-12-03/music/the-miseducation-of-freddie-gibbs/|date=December 2, 2009|access-date=July 31, 2012|archive-date=October 15, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121015014828/http://www.laweekly.com/2009-12-03/music/the-miseducation-of-freddie-gibbs/|url-status=live}}</ref> He played [[American football|football]] at [[Ball State University]] on an [[athletic scholarship]] but was eventually expelled from college.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://imposemagazine.com/features/freddie-gibbs-2|title=Freddie Gibbs - IMPOSE Magazine|last=Gillespie|first=Blake|work=Impose Magazine|access-date=December 2, 2020}}</ref> At the age of 19, he was enlisted into the [[United States Army|U.S. Army]] as part of a [[pre-trial diversion]] program when he was on trial for theft and gun charges, but was [[dishonorably discharged]] only eight months later after being caught smoking marijuana.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://hiphopdx.com/editorials/id.1899/title.souljas-story-10-hip-hop-artists-who-served-their-country#|title=Soulja's Story: 10 Hip Hop Artists Who Served Their Country|last=Arnold|first=Paul|work=HipHopDX|date=May 28, 2020|access-date=December 2, 2020|archive-date=November 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108090759/https://hiphopdx.com/editorials/id.1899/title.souljas-story-10-hip-hop-artists-who-served-their-country|url-status=live}}</ref>

He then began working at a Gary mall, where he met local hip-hop producer Finger Roll, who, according to Gibbs, was "the only guy [in Gary] that had a studio." Gibbs then joined Finger Roll's No Tamin Entertainment crew and began pursuing a rap career.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2019/05/freddie-gibbs-bandana-interview|title=Freddie Gibbs Survived His Darkest Moments With Madlib Beats in His Head|last=Setaro|first=Shawn|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|date=May 10, 2019|access-date=December 2, 2020|archive-date=November 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201124014501/https://www.complex.com/music/2019/05/freddie-gibbs-bandana-interview|url-status=live}}</ref>

On a December 8, 2022, the ''All The Smoke'' podcast with [[Matt Barnes]] and [[Stephen Jackson]], Tipton revealed that his father, [[Warren Tipton]], was a member of the [[The Chi-Lites|Chi-Lites]], having joined the group in 2018 as a singer.<ref>{{Citation |title=Freddie Gibbs {{!}} Ep 163 {{!}} ALL THE SMOKE Full Episode {{!}} SHOWTIME Basketball | date=December 8, 2022 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DFO9FfgdzrA |language=en |access-date=2022-12-28}}</ref>

==Career==
===2004–2011: Career beginnings and CTE World===
Gibbs's rap career began in 2004, self-releasing his first full-length project, a [[mixtape]] titled ''Full Metal Jackit'', which he followed up with a sequel that same year.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2010/aug/26/new-band-freddie-gibbs|title=New band of the day – No 855: Freddie Gibbs|last=Lester|first=Paul|work=[[The Guardian]]|date=August 26, 2010|access-date=November 1, 2020|archive-date=November 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108083255/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2010/aug/26/new-band-freddie-gibbs|url-status=live}}</ref> Around that time, he was signed to [[Interscope Records]] and began working on his debut album, but was dropped from the label in late 2006, when the man that signed him, Joe Weinberger, left the label.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/news/36567-rising-freddie-gibbs/|title=Rising: Freddie Gibbs|last=Breihan|first=Tom|work=[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]|date=October 1, 2009|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028222700/https://pitchfork.com/news/36567-rising-freddie-gibbs/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2009/10/26/wrapping-up|title=Wrapping Up: A genre ages out.|last=Frere-Jones|first=Sasha|magazine=[[The New Yorker]]|date=October 26, 2009|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201024205743/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2009/10/26/wrapping-up|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2009, Gibbs released the mixtapes ''[[The Miseducation of Freddie Gibbs]]'' and ''[[Midwestgangstaboxframecadillacmuzik]]'', followed by his first extended play ''[[Str8 Killa]]'' in August 2010.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Breihan |first=Tom |title=Freddie Gibbs: The Miseducation of Freddie Gibbs / Midwestgangstaboxframecadillacmuzik |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/13466-the-miseducation-of-freddie-gibbs-midwestgangstaboxframecadillacmuzik/ |access-date=December 1, 2025|website=Pitchfork |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.xxlmag.com/freddie-gibbs-teams-with-the-alchemist-for-new-ep/|title=Freddie Gibbs Teams Up With Alchemist For New EP|work=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]]|date=March 25, 2010|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028205034/https://www.xxlmag.com/freddie-gibbs-teams-with-the-alchemist-for-new-ep/|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Young-jeezy-freddie-gibbs.jpg|thumb|right|Gibbs (right) with Young Jeezy in August 2011]]
{{Infobox musical artist
| name              = P.O.C.
| alias             = Pulled Over by the Cops
| years_active      = 2011–present
| genre             = [[Alternative hip-hop]]
| past_members      = Freddie Gibbs<br />[[Chip tha Ripper]]<br />[[The Cool Kids (duo)|The Cool Kids]]
}}

{{anchor|P.O.C.}}He would continue self-releasing mixtapes until 2011, when he signed to [[Young Jeezy]]'s record label [[CTE World]]. In February that year, Gibbs announced on his website that he had joined up with fellow rappers [[King Chip]] and [[The Cool Kids (duo)|The Cool Kids]] to form the supergroup Pulled Over by the Cops (P.O.C.).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.freddiegibbs.com/blog/2011/2/18/poc-pulled-over-by-the-cops-authority.html|title=Freddie Gibbs - BLOG - P.O.C. Pulled Over by the Cops "Authority"|publisher=freddiegibbs.com|author=Freddie Gibbs|access-date=February 6, 2012|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120115014651/http://www.freddiegibbs.com/blog/2011/2/18/poc-pulled-over-by-the-cops-authority.html|archive-date=January 15, 2012}}</ref> In October, he released the mixtape ''[[Cold Day in Hell (mixtape)|Cold Day in Hell]]'', which featured guest appearances from Young Jeezy, [[Juicy J]], and [[2 Chainz]], among others.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.15061/title.freddie-gibbs-reveals-tracklist-for-cold-day-in-hell-due-october-31st|title=Freddie Gibs Reveals Tracklist for "Cold Day in Hell," Due October 31st|last=Horowitz|first=Steven|work=HipHopDX|date=October 28, 2011|access-date=October 24, 2020|archive-date=October 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027141422/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.15061/title.freddie-gibbs-reveals-tracklist-for-cold-day-in-hell-due-october-31st|url-status=live}}</ref> The mixtape received significant coverage from publications such as ''[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]'' and [[XXL (magazine)|''XXL'']], appearing on the latter's Freshman List Class of 2010.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/16024-freddie-gibbs-cold-day-in-hell/|title=Freddie Gibbs: Cold Day in Hell Album Review|last=Drake|first=David|work=[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]|date=November 15, 2011|access-date=October 24, 2020|archive-date=October 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027161149/https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/16024-freddie-gibbs-cold-day-in-hell/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.xxlmag.com/2010-xxl-freshman-class/|title=Meet the 2010 XXL Freshman Class|work=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]]|date=April 1, 2010|access-date=October 24, 2020|archive-date=October 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027050552/https://www.xxlmag.com/2010-xxl-freshman-class/|url-status=live}}</ref> Gibbs and hip-hop producer [[Madlib]] released their first and second [[extended play]]s, titled ''[[Thuggin' (EP)|Thuggin']]'' and ''[[Shame (EP)|Shame]]'', in September 2011 and June 2012, respectively.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://pitchfork.com/news/44698-freddie-gibbs-and-madlib-made-an-ep-together-and-its-here/|title=Freddie Gibbs and Madlib Made an EP Together, and It's Here {{!}} News|last=Battan|first=Carrie|work=[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]|date=November 21, 2011|access-date=October 24, 2020|archive-date=October 23, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023115630/https://pitchfork.com/news/44698-freddie-gibbs-and-madlib-made-an-ep-together-and-its-here/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Lubansky|first=Matt|title=The Shame EP: Madlib and Freddie Gibbs (Madgibbs) Does It Again|url=https://massappeal.com/the-shame-ep-madlib-and-freddie-gibbs-madgibbs-does-it-again/|work=[[Mass Appeal (media)|Mass Appeal]]|access-date=October 24, 2020|date=June 22, 2012|archive-date=March 3, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303215610/http://massappeal.com/the-shame-ep-madlib-and-freddie-gibbs-madgibbs-does-it-again/|url-status=live}}</ref> The EPs served as previews for their upcoming collaborative album, which was originally titled ''Cocaine Piñata'', and later renamed to simply ''[[Piñata (Freddie Gibbs and Madlib album)|Piñata]]''.<ref>{{cite web|last=Horowitz|first=Steven J.|title=Freddie Gibbs & Madlib To Release 'Shame' EP On July 10th|url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.19839/title.freddie-gibbs-madlib-to-release-shame-ep-on-july-10th|work=HipHopDX|date=May 22, 2012|access-date=October 24, 2020}}{{Dead link|date=February 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://hiphopdx.com/interviews/id.2075/title.freddie-gibbs-says-a-dream-inspired-his-cocaine-pinata-project-with-madlib|title=Freddie Gibbs Says a Dream Inspired His "Cocaine Pinata" Project With Madlib|last=Tardio|first=Andres|work=HipHopDX|date=April 4, 2013|access-date=October 31, 2020|archive-date=October 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028230355/https://hiphopdx.com/interviews/id.2075/title.freddie-gibbs-says-a-dream-inspired-his-cocaine-pinata-project-with-madlib|url-status=live}}</ref>

Shortly after the release of ''Cold Day in Hell'', Gibbs announced that his next mixtape would be a [[Gangsta Grillz]] mixtape titled ''[[Baby Face Killa]]''. It was released on September 25, 2012, and featured guest appearances from [[Pharrell Williams]], [[Jadakiss]], [[Jay Rock]], [[Currensy]], [[YG (rapper)|YG]], and others.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://pitchfork.com/news/47973-download-freddie-gibbs-baby-face-killa-mixtape/|title=Download Freddie Gibbs' Baby Face Killa Mixtape &#124; News|publisher=[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]|date=September 25, 2012|access-date=June 7, 2013|archive-date=June 2, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130602044040/http://pitchfork.com/news/47973-download-freddie-gibbs-baby-face-killa-mixtape/|url-status=live}}</ref> The second track from the album, "Still Livin'" was featured on the 2013 video game ''[[Grand Theft Auto V]]''{{'s}} virtual in-game radio station Radio Los Santos.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://music.apple.com/us/playlist/radio-los-santos-gtav/pl.18b77f2cf13c4453842c1722a1a3db3e|title=Radio Los Santos (GTAV) by Rockstar Games on Apple Music|via=[[Apple Music]]|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201029002601/https://music.apple.com/us/playlist/radio-los-santos-gtav/pl.18b77f2cf13c4453842c1722a1a3db3e|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2012–2018: Solo albums and ''Piñata''===
[[File:MadGibbs2014 (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|Gibbs performing with [[Madlib]] in 2014]]
On December 11, 2012, Gibbs announced that he was no longer a member of Young Jeezy's CTE label. He elaborated saying that there is no beef between the two rappers.<ref>{{cite web|last=Vasquez|first=Andres|url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.22170/title.freddie-gibbs-leaves-young-jeezys-label-says-they-move-in-different-ways|title=Freddie Gibbs Leaves Young Jeezy's Label, Says They "Move In Different Ways"|publisher=HipHop DX|date=December 11, 2012|access-date=June 7, 2013|archive-date=May 29, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130529165357/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.22170/title.freddie-gibbs-leaves-young-jeezys-label-says-they-move-in-different-ways|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 27, 2013, Freddie Gibbs announced he would be releasing his debut album ''[[ESGN]]'' (Evil Seeds Grow Naturally) on July 9, 2013, through his own record label of the same name.<ref name="songkick">{{cite web|title=Forgiato Fest 2013 > Forgiato Fest 2013 Line-up, Ticket Info|url=http://www.songkick.com/festivals/672569/id/16104889-forgiato-fest-2013|work=[[Songkick]]|publisher=songkick.com|access-date=March 22, 2013}}</ref> The album ended up being released on June 20, three weeks before the planned release date, due to a leak.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thesource.com/2013/06/20/freddie-gibbs-esgn-dropped-today-due-to-leak/|title=Freddie Gibbs 'ESGN' Dropped Today Due To Leak|last=Weber|first=John|work=[[The Source (magazine)|The Source]]|date=June 20, 2013|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028210410/https://thesource.com/2013/06/20/freddie-gibbs-esgn-dropped-today-due-to-leak/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2013, Freddie Gibbs and Madlib released ''[[Deeper (EP)|Deeper]]'', the final EP from the Piñata previews trilogy. ''[[Piñata (Freddie Gibbs and Madlib album)|Piñata]]'' itself was subsequently released on March 18, 2014. The album received universal acclaim from music critics with a score of 82 on the review site [[Metacritic]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Piñata by Madlib|website=[[Metacritic]] |url=http://www.metacritic.com/music/pioata/madlib|access-date=February 14, 2018|archive-date=May 3, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044444/http://www.metacritic.com/music/pioata/madlib|url-status=live}}</ref> It peaked at number 39 on the US [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] and number seven on the US Top Rap Albums charts. On July 1, 2014, Gibbs, [[Young Thug]], and [[ASAP Ferg]] released a single titled "Old English".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2014/07/young-thug-freddie-gibbs-aap-ferg-old-english-prod-salva-nick-hook|title=Young Thug, Freddie Gibbs & A$AP Ferg - "Old English" (Prod. by Salva & Nick Hook)|author=Khal|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|date=July 1, 2014|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201029003352/https://www.complex.com/music/2014/07/young-thug-freddie-gibbs-aap-ferg-old-english-prod-salva-nick-hook|url-status=live}}</ref> It was produced by Salva and Nick Hook and was certified [[RIAA certifications|Gold]] by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] (RIAA) on July 23, 2020, for selling over 500,000 certified units.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.instagram.com/p/CDMtePXp7e2|title=Gold English 🏆🏆🏆|author=@freddiegibbs|date=July 28, 2020|access-date=October 25, 2020}}</ref> In October 2014, Gibbs and Madlib released their fourth EP, titled ''[[Knicks (Remix)]]''.<ref>{{cite web|title=Freddie Gibbs & Madlib – Knicks Remix EP – with Bronson, Bada$$ & Ransom|url=http://www.rappcats.com/freddie-gibbs-madlib-knicks-remix/|access-date=October 22, 2014|date=October 20, 2014|archive-date=October 23, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141023060754/http://www.rappcats.com/freddie-gibbs-madlib-knicks-remix/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In March 2015, Gibbs released a three-track EP titled ''Pronto'', which was followed up by his second solo studio album, ''[[Shadow of a Doubt (album)|Shadow of a Doubt]]'', released on November 20, 2015.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/news/61853-freddie-gibbs-announces-new-release-shadow-of-a-doubt-shares-fuckin-up-the-count-video/|title=Freddie Gibbs Announces New Release Shadow of A Doubt, Shares "Fuckin' Up The Count" Video|last=Camp|first=Zoe|work=[[Pitchfork (magazine)|Pitchfork]]|date=October 29, 2015|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028204903/https://pitchfork.com/news/61853-freddie-gibbs-announces-new-release-shadow-of-a-doubt-shares-fuckin-up-the-count-video/|url-status=live}}</ref> Gibbs's third solo studio album, ''[[You Only Live 2wice]]'', was released in March 2017.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://hiphopdx.com/news/id.42629/title.freddie-gibbs-announces-you-only-live-2wice-album#|title=Freddie Gibbs Announces "You Only Live 2wice" Album|magazine=HipHopDX|date=March 8, 2017|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 31, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031122947/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.42629/title.freddie-gibbs-announces-you-only-live-2wice-album|url-status=live}}</ref> The only single, "Crushed Glass", was released simultaneously alongside a music video directed by Eric Nelson.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/03/freddie-gibbs-crushed-glass-live-twice-album/|title=Freddie Gibbs Drops New Video "Crushed Glass," Announces 'You Only Live 2wice' Project|magazine=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]]|date=March 8, 2017|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=January 5, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190105064752/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/03/freddie-gibbs-crushed-glass-live-twice-album/|url-status=live}}</ref> In June 2018, he released his first commercial mixtape, ''[[Freddie (Freddie Gibbs album)|Freddie]]'' without any prior announcements.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.factmag.com/2018/06/22/freddie-gibbs-freddie-mixtape/|title=Freddie Gibbs drops surprise mixtape, Freddie|work=[[Fact (UK magazine)|Fact]]|last=Wilson|first=Scott|date=June 22, 2018|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 25, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201025172631/https://www.factmag.com/2018/06/22/freddie-gibbs-freddie-mixtape/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2018–present: Further collaborations and increasing success===
After the success of ''Piñata'', Gibbs and Madlib announced in May 2016 that they were developing a second collaborative album. The resulting project was the album ''[[Bandana (album)|Bandana]]'', which was released in June 2019. ''Bandana'' was preceded by two singles: "Flat Tummy Tea" and "Bandana", the latter featuring the dancehall artist [[Assassin (deejay)|Assassin]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2019/03/freddie-gibbs-madlib-bandana-project-title-track|title=Freddie Gibbs and Madlib Share 'Bandana' Project's Title Track|website=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|date=March 5, 2019 |access-date=March 6, 2019|archive-date=June 16, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200616102602/https://www.complex.com/music/2019/03/freddie-gibbs-madlib-bandana-project-title-track|url-status=live}}</ref> The singles were released in February and March 2019 respectively. ''Bandana'' was released by [[Keep Cool Records]], [[RCA Records]], Madlib Invazion, and ESGN, and received critical acclaim.<ref>{{cite web|title=Bandana by Freddie Gibbs|website=[[Metacritic]] |url=https://www.metacritic.com/music/bandana/freddie-gibbs|language=en|access-date=2019-11-21|archive-date=April 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200408135147/https://www.metacritic.com/music/bandana/freddie-gibbs|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2019/02/20/freddie-gibbs-madlib-flat-tummy-tea|title=Freddie Gibbs and Madlib reunite on "Flat Tummy Tea"|website=The FADER|date=February 19, 2019 |access-date=March 1, 2019|archive-date=July 13, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190713005520/https://www.thefader.com/2019/02/20/freddie-gibbs-madlib-flat-tummy-tea|url-status=live}}</ref>

Gibbs also released multiple collaborations with producer [[The Alchemist (musician)|the Alchemist]]. Their first project together was ''[[Fetti]]'', a collaborative album between Gibbs, the Alchemist, and rapper [[Currensy]]. ''Fetti'' was released in October 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/09/freddie-gibbs-currensy-fetti-halloween/|title=Freddie Gibbs & Currensy to Drop 'Fetti' Album on Halloween|work=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]]|last=Hopkins|first=Anna|date=September 27, 2018|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=April 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407004823/https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/09/freddie-gibbs-currensy-fetti-halloween/|url-status=live}}</ref> Gibbs and the Alchemist then reunited to release a second collaborative album, titled ''[[Alfredo (album)|Alfredo]]'', in May 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2020/05/freddie-gibbs-alchemist-alfredo-interview|title=Freddie Gibbs and Alchemist Break Down New Album 'Alfredo'|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|last=Setaro|first=Shawn|date=May 29, 2020|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028204838/https://www.complex.com/music/2020/05/freddie-gibbs-alchemist-alfredo-interview|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2020/05/freddie-gibbs-alchemist-alfredo-stream|title=Stream Freddie Gibbs and Alchemist's Latest Project 'Alfredo'|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|last=Rose|first=Jordan|date=May 29, 2020|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=November 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112223757/https://www.complex.com/music/2020/05/freddie-gibbs-alchemist-alfredo-stream|url-status=live}}</ref> ''Alfredo'' was preceded a day earlier by its lead single, "1985", as well as an accompanying music video directed by Nick Walker.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/257136-freddie-gibbs-and-alchemist-lay-waste-to-1985-new-song|title=Freddie Gibbs & Alchemist Lay Waste To "1985"|website=HotNewHipHop|last=Findlay|first=Mitch|date=May 28, 2020|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=October 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201029003344/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/freddie-gibbs-and-alchemist-lay-waste-to-1985-new-song.1987369.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The album debuted at number 15 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]], making it both Gibbs's and the Alchemist's highest-charting album to date.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/chart-beat/9396483/lady-gaga-chromatica-no-1-debut-billboard-200-chart|title=Lady Gaga's 'Chromatica' on Course for No. 1 Debut on Billboard 200 Chart|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|last=Caulfield|first=Keith|date=June 4, 2020|access-date=October 25, 2020|archive-date=June 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200606063828/https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/chart-beat/9396483/lady-gaga-chromatica-no-1-debut-billboard-200-chart|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://hypebeast.com/2020/6/freddie-gibbs-the-alchemist-alfredo-album-interview|title=Freddie Gibbs, The Alchemist 'Alfredo' Interview|publisher=Hypebeast|last=Johnson|first=Patrick|date=June 19, 2020|access-date=October 25, 2020|quote=Alfredo culminated in a debut at No. 15 on the ''Billboard'' 200, the highest charting position for both Freddie and The Alchemist in their respective careers.|archive-date=October 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201017015829/https://hypebeast.com/2020/6/freddie-gibbs-the-alchemist-alfredo-album-interview|url-status=live}}</ref> It was critically praised, and was nominated for a [[Grammy Award]] for [[Grammy Award for Best Rap Album|Best Rap Album]] at the [[63rd Annual Grammy Awards]].<ref name="Grammy" />

In June 2020, Gibbs signed to [[Warner Records]].<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/articles/business/9404459/freddie-gibbs-warner-records-exclusive|title=Freddie Gibbs Signs to Warner Records: Exclusive|last=Lamarre|first=Carl|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=June 18, 2020|access-date=June 18, 2020|archive-date=June 18, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200618164233/https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/9404459/freddie-gibbs-warner-records-exclusive|url-status=live}}</ref> Throughout late 2020 and early 2021, Gibbs released three singles through the label: "4 Thangs", which features guest appearances from fellow rapper [[Big Sean]] and hip-hop producer [[Hit-Boy]]; "Gang Signs", featuring fellow rapper [[Schoolboy Q]]; and "Big Boss Rabbit".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2020/10/freddie-gibbs-big-sean-hit-boy-4-thangs-video|title=Freddie Gibbs Connects With Big Sean and Hit-Boy on "4 Thangs"|last=Espinoza|first=Joshua|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|date=October 30, 2020|access-date=November 1, 2020|archive-date=November 1, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201101070435/https://www.complex.com/music/2020/10/freddie-gibbs-big-sean-hit-boy-4-thangs-video|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2115179/freddie-gibbs-gang-signs-feat-schoolboy-q/music/|title=Freddie Gibbs & ScHoolboy Q Share New Song "Gang Signs": Watch the Video|last=Deville|first=Chris|work=[[Stereogum]]|date=February 5, 2021|access-date=February 5, 2021|archive-date=February 5, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205051710/https://www.stereogum.com/2115179/freddie-gibbs-gang-signs-feat-schoolboy-q/music/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nme.com/en_au/news/music/freddie-gibbs-flips-nas-classic-for-new-track-big-boss-rabbit-2913600|title=Freddie Gibbs flips Nas classic for new track 'Big Boss Rabbit'|last=Lavin|first=Will|work=[[NME]]|date=April 4, 2021|access-date=April 6, 2021}}</ref> Gibbs then released his first album on Warner and fifth overall solo album, ''[[Soul Sold Separately]]'', in September 2022.<ref name="IHR">{{Cite web|last=Centeno |first=Tony M. |url=https://www.iheart.com/content/2022-09-30-freddie-gibbs-drops-soul-sold-separately-lp-with-pusha-t-offset-more/ |title=Freddie Gibbs Drops 'Soul Sold Separately' LP With Pusha T, Offset & More |publisher=[[iHeartRadio]] |date=2022-09-30 |access-date=2022-10-11}}</ref>

In addition to his musical ventures, Gibbs had a cameo role in the first season of the [[Starz]] drama series ''[[Power Book IV: Force]]'' in March 2022. He portrayed the role of Cousin Buddy, a cross-eyed drug dealer.<ref>{{cite web |last1=A. |first1=Aron |title=Freddie Gibbs Debuts As Cross-Eyed Cousin Buddy In "Power Book IV" |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/411631-freddie-gibbs-debuts-as-cross-eyed-cousin-buddy-in-power-book-iv-news |website=HotNewHipHop|date=March 21, 2022 }}</ref>

On October 30, 2024, Gibbs announced the release of his sixth studio album, ''[[You Only Die 1nce]]''. The album dropped on November 1.<ref>{{cite web |last=Fisher |first=Caroline |title=Freddie Gibbs Unveils Tracklist For New Album "You Only Die 1nce" Dropping Halloween Night |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/855850-freddie-gibbs-tracklist-new-album-hip-hop-news |website=HotNewHipHop|date=2024-10-30|access-date=2024-10-31 }}</ref>

==Artistry==
Critics have recognized Gibbs's technically proficient and stylistically diverse rapping ability. Matthew Ramirez of ''[[NPR]]'' has described him as "the eternal technician";<ref>{{Cite web|last=Ramirez |first=Matthew |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/10/01/1126282898/freddie-gibbs-soul-sold-separately-review |title=With '$oul $old $eparately,' Freddie Gibbs cashes in on his cachet |publisher=[[NPR]] |date=2022-10-01 |access-date=2022-10-11}}</ref> Tshepo Mokoena of ''[[The Guardian]]'' characterized him as "a classic rapper's rapper" and stated that "when it comes to technique and versatility, he can take your breath away".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mokoena |first=Tshepo |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2022/oct/04/rapping-powerhouse-freddie-gibbs-weird-kid-drug-dealer |title=Rap powerhouse Freddie Gibbs: 'I was always the weird kid – a lot of people don't like that' |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=2022-10-04 |access-date=2022-10-11}}</ref> He was featured on the soundtracks for ''[[Max Payne 3]]'', ''[[NBA 2K12]]'',<ref>{{cite web|title=Str8 Killa EP Review|url=http://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/14499-str8-killa-ep/|publisher=Pitchfork Media|access-date=April 25, 2012|archive-date=June 3, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120603142535/http://www.pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/14499-str8-killa-ep/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Sleeping Dogs (video game)|Sleeping Dogs]], and ''[[Grand Theft Auto V]]''.<ref>{{cite web|title=Grand Theft Auto V Reveals Expanded Radio Station Tracklists for Game Relaunch|url=http://pitchfork.com/news/57496-grand-theft-auto-v-reveals-expanded-radio-station-tracklists-for-game-relaunch/|publisher=Pitchfork Media|date=November 17, 2014|access-date=December 24, 2015|archive-date=November 18, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141118012826/http://pitchfork.com/news/57496-grand-theft-auto-v-reveals-expanded-radio-station-tracklists-for-game-relaunch/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Gibbs has listed [[Scarface (rapper)|Scarface]], [[DMX (rapper)|DMX]], [[Tupac]], [[Jay-Z]], [[Nas]], [[Bone Thugs-N-Harmony]], [[Geto Boys]], [[UGK]], [[Three 6 Mafia]], [[Outkast]], [[Raekwon]], [[Eminem]], [[Twista]], [[Ice Cube]], [[Noreaga]], [[Juvenile (rapper)|Juvenile]], [[Spice 1]], [[De La Soul]], [[Kool G Rap]], [[Black Thought]], [[Mos Def]], [[Jeezy]], and [[Ol' Dirty Bastard]] as some of his influences.<ref>{{cite web|last=Burgess |first=Omar |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.21787/title.freddie-gibbs-describes-his-partnerships-with-madlib-and-young-jeezy |title=Freddie Gibbs Describes His Partnerships With Madlib And Young Jeezy |publisher=HipHop DX |date=2012-11-08 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141018141147/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.21787/title.freddie-gibbs-describes-his-partnerships-with-madlib-and-young-jeezy|archive-date=October 18, 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Personal life==
On November 4, 2014, a gunman opened fire on Gibbs, who was sitting in a car after he had finished performing at a record store in [[Williamsburg, Brooklyn]], New York. Gibbs escaped unharmed and two members of his entourage received non-life-threatening gunshot wounds.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://pitchfork.com/news/57310-freddie-gibbs-allegedly-targeted-in-shooting-outside-rough-trade-in-brooklyn/ | title=Freddie Gibbs Allegedly Targeted in Shooting Outside Rough Trade in Brooklyn | publisher=pitchfork.com | date=November 4, 2014 | access-date=November 4, 2014 | archive-date=November 6, 2014 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141106141240/http://pitchfork.com/news/57310-freddie-gibbs-allegedly-targeted-in-shooting-outside-rough-trade-in-brooklyn/ | url-status=live }}</ref>

In May 2015, Gibbs launched a [[GoFundMe]] to help School Supply Giveaway of Gary purchase school supplies for children in Gary, Indiana.<ref name=supplies>{{cite web|url=http://www.imposemagazine.com/bytes/news/freddie-gibbs-launches-gofundme-for-gary-indiana-school-supplies|title=Freddie Gibbs Launches GoFundMe for Gary, Indiana School Supplies|work=[[Impose (magazine)|Impose]]|publisher=Answer Media|date=May 14, 2015|access-date=May 29, 2018|archive-date=June 14, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614071706/http://www.imposemagazine.com/bytes/news/freddie-gibbs-launches-gofundme-for-gary-indiana-school-supplies|url-status=live}}</ref> On the site page, Gibbs explained, "Growing up in Gary there was little opportunity for a kid like me to make something of myself. Not having the basic supplies ... left a lot of us with little motivation to do well in school and turned a lot of us to darker paths. By helping these students acquire the basic supplies every student needs and deserves you will be maximizing their chances at a brighter future."<ref name=supplies/>

Gibbs has said that while he is not a fan of [[organized religion]], he most closely identifies as a [[Muslim]], calling it "a personal relationship between myself and God."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2021/03/10/975320913/freddie-gibbs-grammys-best-rap-album|title=Freddie Gibbs Might Blow Up, But He Won't Go Pop|last=Ma|first=David|work=[[NPR Music]]|date=March 10, 2021|access-date=March 13, 2021|archive-date=March 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210312122831/https://www.npr.org/2021/03/10/975320913/freddie-gibbs-grammys-best-rap-album|url-status=live}}</ref>

Gibbs is the father of three children: a daughter (born 2015) with his ex-fiancée Erica Dickerson, daughter of Hall Of Fame [[NFL]] running back [[Eric Dickerson]], and a son from a previous relationship.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Coleman |first=C. Vernon|url=https://www.xxlmag.com/freddie-gibbs-daughter/ |title=Freddie Gibbs and Fiancee Erica Dickerson Welcome Baby Daughter |work=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]] |date=2015-04-26 |access-date=2022-10-12}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Tigg |first=FNR |url=https://www.complex.com/music/2019/09/freddie-gibbs-gets-restraining-order-mother-of-his-son-vandalized-car |title=Freddie Gibbs Gets Restraining Order After Mother of His Son Allegedly Vandalized Car |work=[[Complex Networks|Complex]] |date=2019-09-26 |access-date=2022-10-12}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Centeno |first=Tony M. |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/freddie-gibbs-mother-child-kill-threats-son/ |title=Alleged Mother of One of Freddie Gibbs' Children Claims He Sent People to Kill Her and Their Son |work=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]] |date=2019-08-31 |access-date=2022-10-12}}</ref>. 

==Controversies==
Before performing a concert at Le Rex in [[Toulouse]], France, in June 2016, Gibbs was arrested on a European arrest warrant issued for a rape alleged to have taken place in Austria in 2015,<ref name="June 4th arrest">{{cite news |last=Park |first=Andrea |title=Gibbs arrested before performing at a concert |url=http://www.people.com/article/freddie-gibbs-arrested-in-france-alleged-rape-in-austria-denies-charges |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160605120121/http://www.people.com/article/freddie-gibbs-arrested-in-france-alleged-rape-in-austria-denies-charges |archive-date=June 5, 2016 |access-date=June 4, 2016 |newspaper=[[People.com]]}}</ref><ref name="While on Tour">{{cite news |last=Brennan |first=Collin |date=June 4, 2016 |title=Gibbs arrested while on tour |url=https://consequence.net/2016/06/freddie-gibbs-arrested-in-france-on-alleged-rape-charges-may-face-extradition-to-austria/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160605100933/https://consequence.net/2016/06/freddie-gibbs-arrested-in-france-on-alleged-rape-charges-may-face-extradition-to-austria/ |archive-date=June 5, 2016 |access-date=June 4, 2016 |newspaper=[[Consequence of Sound]]}}</ref> and extradited to Austria on sexual assault charges,<ref name="Extradited">{{cite news |date=June 23, 2016 |title=Gibbs Extradited to Austria |url=http://www.france24.com/en/20160623-french-court-orders-us-rapper-freddie-gibbs-extradited-austria-lawyer |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160624142527/http://www.france24.com/en/20160623-french-court-orders-us-rapper-freddie-gibbs-extradited-austria-lawyer |archive-date=June 24, 2016 |access-date=June 23, 2016 |newspaper=[[France24]]}}</ref> Gibbs said he "has no intention of escaping justice, whether French or Austrian"<ref name="Extradited" /> and was charged the following month with sexual abuse charges on two women.<ref>{{cite web |date=August 17, 2016 |title=Freddie Gibbs accused of Sexual abuse |url=http://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/rapper-freddie-gibbs-charged-with-sexual-abuse-in-austria/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818220717/http://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/rapper-freddie-gibbs-charged-with-sexual-abuse-in-austria/ |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |access-date=August 17, 2016 |agency=Associated Press}}</ref>
After being held in jail for a number of weeks he was released on bail of €50,000,<ref>{{cite web |last=Lilah |first=Rose |date=September 30, 2016 |title=Freddie Gibbs Acquitted Of Sexual Assault |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/58137-freddie-gibbs-acquitted-of-sexual-assault-news |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161001151905/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/freddie-gibbs-acquitted-of-sexual-assault-news.24448.html |archive-date=October 1, 2016 |access-date=September 30, 2016 |work=HipHopDX}}</ref> and acquitted on all charges in September 2016.<ref name="Acquitted">{{cite news |last=Howard |first=Jacinta |date=September 30, 2016 |title=Freddie Gibbs Acquitted in Austrian Sexual Abuse Trial [UPDATE] |url=http://theboombox.com/freddie-gibbs-acquitted-in-austrian-sexual-abuse-trial/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161002061614/http://theboombox.com/freddie-gibbs-acquitted-in-austrian-sexual-abuse-trial/ |archive-date=October 2, 2016 |access-date=September 30, 2016 |newspaper=[[The Boombox]]}}</ref>

Gibbs has been involved in several public feuds with other rappers. In December 2021, it was reported that Gibbs was beaten up by members of [[Jim Jones (rapper)|Jim Jones]]' entourage at a restaurant in Miami.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.hot97.com/hot-news/freddie-gibbs-reacts-to-reports-that-he-got-beat-up-by-jim-jones-at-restaurant-in-miami | title=Freddie Gibbs Reacts to Reports Claiming That He Was 'Beat Up' by Jim Jones at Restaurant in Miami | date=December 15, 2021 }}</ref> His most notable feud has been with [[Benny the Butcher]]. In August 2022, after trading insults with Benny the Butcher over social media for several months, Freddie Gibbs was physically assaulted by a large mob of people while on a date at a [[Dinosaur Bar-B-Que]] location in Buffalo, New York. [[TMZ]] acquired footage of the brawl, leading the video to go viral.<ref>{{cite web |date=May 17, 2022 |title=Freddie Gibbs Fight Video from Alleged Assault and Robbery in Buffalo |url=https://www.tmz.com/2022/05/16/freddie-gibbs-fight-video-benny-the-butcher-buffalo-brawl/ |access-date=August 30, 2026 |website=TMZ}}</ref> Benny the Butcher seemed to confirm his involvement in the incident, posting images on social media of a [[Jewellery|chain]] allegedly taken from Gibbs during the fight.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Freddie Gibbs and Benny the Butcher Trade Disses and Reference Altercation in Buffalo |url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/j-rose/freddie-gibbs-benny-the-butcher-trade-disses-buffalo-altercation |access-date=2024-10-20 |website=www.complex.com |date=August 24, 2022 |language=en}}</ref> In a January 2024 interview with ''[[The Breakfast Club (radio show)|The Breakfast Club]]<u>,</u>'' Benny stated that the beef between the two rappers was over, but that he was not interested in making amends with Gibbs.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Benny the Butcher Addresses Freddie Gibbs Beef, Says 'Hell No' When Asked About Burying the Hatchet |url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/markelibert/benny-the-butcher-addresses-freddie-gibbs-beef |access-date=2024-10-20 |website=www.complex.com |date=January 14, 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

