'''Radric Delantic Davis''' (born February 12, 1980),<ref name="allmusic">{{cite web|url={{AllMusic|class=artist|id=p703775/biography|pure_url=yes}}|title=Gucci Mane > Biography|last=Cordor|first=Cyril|year=2007|website=AllMusic|access-date=May 3, 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://mugshots.com/US-Counties/Georgia/Fulton-County-GA/Radric-Davis.70619217.html|title=Radric Davis Mugshot – Radric Davis Arrest – Fulton County, GA| website= mugshots.com| publisher= |date=December 31, 2013|access-date=June 14, 2015|archive-date=July 2, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150702160110/http://mugshots.com/US-Counties/Georgia/Fulton-County-GA/Radric-Davis.70619217.html|url-status=live}}</ref> known professionally as '''Gucci Mane''', is an American rapper and music executive. He is credited, along with fellow [[Atlanta]]-based rappers [[T.I.]] and [[Jeezy]], with pioneering the hip-hop subgenre [[trap music]] for mainstream audiences during the 2000s.<ref name="auto">{{cite news |url= https://www.theguardian.com/music/2015/aug/13/trap-kings-how-hip-hop-sub-genre-dominated-decade|title=Trap kings: how the hip-hop sub-genre dominated the decade| last=Lee|first=Christina|date=August 13, 2015|newspaper=The Guardian|access-date=January 27, 2017|archive-date=January 29, 2017|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170129133713/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2015/aug/13/trap-kings-how-hip-hop-sub-genre-dominated-decade|url-status= live}}</ref> His debut studio album, ''[[Trap House]]'' (2005), was released by the independent label [[Big Cat Records]] and entered the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]]; it was followed by ''[[Hard to Kill (Gucci Mane album)|Hard to Kill]]'' (2006), which spawned his first [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] entry with its single "[[Freaky Gurl]]". That same year, he released his third album, ''[[Trap-A-Thon]]'', before signing with [[Atlantic Records]] to release his fourth album, ''[[Back to the Trap House]]'' (2007).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ihiphop.com/gucci-mane-signs-with-atlantic-records/|title=Gucci Mane Signs with Atlantic Records|date=June 19, 2007|website=iHipHop.com}}</ref>

During this time, Davis continued [[independent music|self-releasing]] releasing mixtapes and collaborative projects. He signed with Atlantic's sister label, [[Warner Records|Warner Bros. Records]] to release his sixth album and mainstream breakthrough, ''[[The State vs. Radric Davis]]'' (2009). Preceded by the [[RIAA certification|double platinum]]-certified single "[[Lemonade (Gucci Mane song)|Lemonade]]", it peaked within the top ten of the ''Billboard'' 200, while the album's sequel, ''[[The Appeal: Georgia's Most Wanted]]'' (2010), peaked at number four. Following a two year incarceration between 2014 and 2016, he re-emerged with several commercial projects beginning with his ninth album, ''[[Everybody Looking]]'' (2016), which peaked at number two on the ''Billboard'' 200. He guest appeared on [[Rae Sremmurd]]'s 2016 single "[[Black Beatles]]", which became his first song to peak atop the ''Billboard'' Hot 100.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.rap-up.com/2019/10/17/gucci-mane-woptober-2-stream/|title=Stream Gucci Mane's Album 'Woptober II'|website=Rap-Up.com |language=en-US|access-date=October 19, 2019|archive-date=October 19, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191019022846/https://www.rap-up.com/2019/10/17/gucci-mane-woptober-2-stream/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Gucci Mane has released sixteen studio albums and seventy-one mixtapes since 2001. He founded the Atlantic Records-distributed label imprint [[1017 Records]] in 2007, which has signed artists including [[Young Thug]], [[Waka Flocka Flame]], [[Chief Keef]], and [[Pooh Shiesty]], among others. Throughout his career, he has worked with artists including [[the Weeknd]], [[Drake (musician)|Drake]], [[Lil Wayne]], [[Chris Brown]], [[Selena Gomez]], [[Mariah Carey]], [[Usher (musician)|Usher]], [[Bruno Mars]], and [[Marilyn Manson]]. His influence has earned him the titles of "[an] avatar of East Atlanta," and "the most influential underground rapper of the 2000s".{{cn|date= April 2026}} He was nominated for a [[Grammy Award]] in 2020.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2016/08/08/the-reinvention-of-gucci-mane|title=The Reinvention of Gucci Mane|last=Sanneh|first=Kelefa |magazine= The New Yorker |date=August 1, 2016|access-date=January 31, 2019|language=en|issn=0028-792X|archive-date=June 19, 2018|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180619055752/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2016/08/08/the-reinvention-of-gucci-mane|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/gucci-mane-is-the-most-influential-rapper-of-the-last-decade-noisey-atlanta/|title=History vs. Radric Davis: Why Gucci Mane Is the Most Influential Rapper of the Last Decade|last=Drake|first=David|date=March 19, 2015|website=Noisey|language=en-US|access-date=January 31, 2019|archive-date=February 1, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190201013424/https://noisey.vice.com/en_us/article/64yv89/gucci-mane-is-the-most-influential-rapper-of-the-last-decade-noisey-atlanta|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Early life==
===Growing up: 1980–1993===
Radric Delantic Davis was born on February 12, 1980, in [[Bessemer, Alabama]], to former US serviceman and power plant worker Ralph Everett Dudley (born 1955),<ref name= Auto />{{rp|3}} and social worker and teacher Vicky Jean Davis (born 1955).<ref name= Auto />{{rp|6}} Davis's family had a strong military background. His paternal grandfather, James Dudley Sr. served in the military for twelve years, including during [[World War II]], as a chef,<ref name= Auto />{{rp|4}} and his maternal grandfather, Walter Lee Davis, served in the Pacific during World War II and on the [[USS South Dakota (BB-57)|USS ''South Dakota'']].<ref name= Auto />{{rp|6}} Davis's father also served in the military, being stationed in Korea for two years.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|6}}

Davis's parents met in 1978; Davis's mother already had a son, Victor Davis, from another relationship. When Davis was born, his father was on the run from the police for dealing [[crack cocaine]] and [[heroin]],<ref name= Auto />{{rp|21}} and had fled to [[Detroit]], [[Michigan]].<ref name= Auto />{{rp|15}} Davis's father was not present to sign the birth certificate, and Davis took his mother's last name.<ref name= Auto>{{Cite book |title=The Autobiography of Gucci Mane |last=Mane |first=Gucci |year=2017}}</ref>{{rp|9}}

Growing up, Davis was raised by his paternal grandmother while his mother attended college to get her degree.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|11}} Davis's mother was a teacher who taught him to read at a young age, and spent most of her time teaching him scriptures from the [[Bible]].<ref name= Auto />{{rp|20}} Davis's first school was Jonesboro Elementary, where he attended kindergarten and the first several grades of elementary school.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|21}} His older half-brother Victor introduced Davis to [[hip-hop]] when Davis was six years old by bringing Davis to a [[Run-DMC]], [[Beastie Boys]] and [[LL Cool J]] concert.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|12}} Before moving to Atlanta with his mother, Davis had a distant relationship with his father, who would visit at irregular intervals;<ref name= Auto />{{rp|13}} the visits stopped when Davis's father had two children with another woman and began to prioritize his children in Atlanta over Davis.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|18}} Davis would later use a nickname given to his father, "the [[Gucci]] Man", as his [[stage name]].<ref name= Auto />{{rp|15}}

Davis moved with his single mother to [[Atlanta]] when he was nine years old due to family problems in Bessemer. Davis's mother had a boyfriend in Atlanta and planned to move in with him, but she decided otherwise at the last minute. They later moved in with someone Davis's mother met in church.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|15}} Davis's family was kicked out of the house and did not have a stable living environment until his father set them up in a [[Knights Inn]] motel.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|18}} Davis grew up in an area which was high in crime.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|19}}

Davis's mother moved Davis and Victor to [[East Atlanta]] and Davis attended Cedar Grove Elementary School.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|20}} He was generally good in school and considered himself athletic although he did not participate in school sports.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|21}} Davis dealt drugs, mainly selling cannabis with his older brother. Davis sold cannabis on his own and ended up using his Christmas money to acquire crack [[cocaine]] while in the eighth grade, starting his career as a drug dealer.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|26}}

===Time as a drug dealer: 1993–2001===
By the time Davis’ part-time [[drug dealer|drug-dealing]] became a career, he was a freshman at [[McNair High School (Georgia)|Ronald E. McNair High School]], where he got good grades and was popular among his peers.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|31}} He did not take drugs for the first few years that he was dealing drugs, and his first experience smoking cannabis was with a high school crush.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|32}} During this time one of Davis' friends was his later collaborator, [[OJ da Juiceman]], and the two would [[freestyle rap]] together.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hard To Kill: The Oral History Of Gucci Mane |url=https://www.thefader.com/2015/10/01/gucci-mane-oral-history-hard-to-kill |website= [[The Fader]] |language=en |access-date=2024-11-16}}</ref>

Even though Davis had been selling drugs for nearly two years by 1995, he had never encountered a violence. When he was 15, Davis was riding his push bicycle when a man stopped him and pointed a [[IMI Desert Eagle|Desert Eagle]] at his head. The man took all of Davis’ valuables.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|33}} and began to carry a .380 caliber handgun.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|34}} Davis had numerous other life-threatening situations, including a feud with a local street gang who called themselves "the East Shoals Boys" in 1997. The feud resulted in Davis's friend Javon being beaten close to death<ref name= Auto />{{rp|37}} and adult men walking into [[McNair High School (Georgia)|McNair High School]] looking for Davis.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|38}} of The feud resolved itself when Davis and several friends got into a fistfight with members of the East Shoals Boys, who left Davis alone from that point on.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|39}}

In 1998, Davis graduated from high school with a 3.0 GPA and a [[HOPE Scholarship]] to [[Georgia Perimeter College]]. He took a computer programming course, though he rarely attended, and he was expelled in 2001<ref name= Auto />{{rp|40}} after being caught in possession of crack cocaine by an undercover police officer.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|41}} Davis was sentenced to 90 days in a county jail and then probation.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|41}}

===Pursuing music: 2001–2005===
Davis enjoyed writing poetry at a young age, and began rapping at the age of 14.<ref name="allmusic" /> Following his first arrest, Davis began to take music seriously and released '' La Flare'' on Str8 Drop Records. It was pressed onto about 1000&nbsp;CDs and distributed throughout East Atlanta.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/m/index.php?s=news&id=12869| title= Early Gucci Mane Recordings Come To Album Release As 'La Flare' | website= HipHop DX |archive-date=December 23, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223151816/http://www.hiphopdx.com/m/index.php?s=news&id=12869|url-status=live |access-date=March 9, 2015}}</ref> Following the release of ''La Flare'', and being inspired by [[Master P]], Davis decided he wanted to start a music label.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|42}} Davis began to manage rapper Lil Buddy in 2001.<ref name= Auto />{{rp|42}} In 2002, Davis linked up with SYS Records as a member of the Sign Yourself Click. Davis also linked up with producer [[Zaytoven]] and made his own label, LaFlare Entertainment.<ref name="The Boombox">{{cite web|url=http://theboombox.com/gucci-mane-trap-house-album/|title=10 Years Ago: Gucci Mane Releases His Debut Album 'Trap House'|work=The Boombox|date=May 24, 2015 |access-date=June 14, 2015|archive-date=June 28, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150628203802/http://theboombox.com/gucci-mane-trap-house-album/|url-status=live}}</ref>

After heading to New York in search of a distribution deal and returning home empty, he was introduced to Big Cat, the head of Big Cat Records. Ultimately deciding to form an alliance with the label that brought [[Khia]] to prominence, Davis released the song "Black Tee", a response to the [[Dem Franchize Boyz]] hit record, "[[White Tee]]", as well as a collaboration with fellow rising local rapper [[Jeezy|Young Jeezy]] with "So Icy."<ref name="The Boombox" /> Securing a distribution deal with [[Tommy Boy Records]], Davis continued to work the underground while preparing for the release of his debut album.

==Career==

===2005–2006: ''Trap House'' and ''Hard to Kill''===
In 2005, Davis released his independent debut album entitled ''[[Trap House]]'', which featured the single "[[Icy (Gucci Mane song)|Icy]]" with [[Jeezy|Young Jeezy]]. Disputes over the rights to this single caused a rift between the two artists. ''Trap House'' was considered a success for an independent artist. The album crept into the Top 20 of the ''Billboard'' [[Top R&B/Hip-Hop Albums|R&B/Hip-Hop Albums charts]] and landed at the top spot on the [[Heatseekers charts|Billboard Heatseekers Album]] chart. Guest appearances include [[Bun B]], [[Killer Mike]], [[Lil Scrappy]], [[Jody Breeze]] and [[Khujo]] of [[Goodie Mobb]]. ''[[Hard to Kill (Gucci Mane album)|Hard to Kill]]'' followed in 2006. It included the hit single "[[Freaky Gurl]]", which peaked at number 12 on the [[Hot Rap Tracks]], number 19 on the [[Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs]], and at number 62 on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]]. He also shot videos for the tracks "Street Nigazzz"<ref>{{cite av media| url= https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QNsCxa4BZB8 |author= Gucci Mane| title= Gucci Mane- Street Nigga (Official Video)| archiveurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20180118075148/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QNsCxa4BZB8 |archivedate=January 18, 2018 |via= YouTube| date= October 30, 2009| access-date=}}</ref> and "Pillz".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mW7dJDuJQKc|title=Pillz| author= Gucci Mane |via= YouTube |access-date=July 1, 2016|archive-date=December 19, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151219040541/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mW7dJDuJQKc|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2006, Davis released the mixtape ''[[Chicken Talk]]'', beginning a series of mixtapes in the late 2000s.<ref name="Fader-HTK">{{Cite web |url= https://www.thefader.com/2015/10/01/gucci-mane-oral-history-hard-to-kill |title=Hard to Kill: The Oral History of Gucci Mane |first=Benjamin |last= Meadows-Ingram |website= The Fader |language=en |date=October 1, 2015 |access-date=October 14, 2025}}</ref> ''Chicken Talk'' has come to be recognized as one of hip-hop's best albums by outlets such as ''[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]'' and ''[[Rolling Stone|Rolling Stone Australia]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/features/lists-and-guides/best-rap-albums-all-time/ |title=The 100 Best Rap Albums of All Time |first=Lawrence |last=Burney |website=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]] |date=September 30, 2025 |access-date=October 14, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://au.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/-40803/gucci-mane-chicken-talk-2006-40823/ |title=The 200 Greatest Hip-Hop Albums of All Time |first=Joe |last=Gross |website=[[Rolling Stone|Rolling Stone Australia]] |date=June 8, 2022 |access-date=October 14, 2025}}</ref>

===2007–2010: Mixtapes, ''Back to the Trap House, The State vs. Radric Davis''===
The official remix of "Freaky Gurl" featuring [[Ludacris]] and [[Lil' Kim]] was included on his 2007 commercial debut album, ''[[Back to the Trap House]]''. Gucci Mane appeared on [[OJ da Juiceman]]'s "[[Make Tha Trap Say Aye]]" and began working on various mixtapes.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1607973/20090327/gucci_mane.jhtml|title=Gucci Mane Is Out Of Jail And Back On The Mixtape Circuit|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|date=March 30, 2009|work=Mixtape Monday|publisher=MTV News|access-date=May 3, 2009|archive-date=April 15, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090415094930/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1607973/20090327/gucci_mane.jhtml|url-status=dead}}</ref> Following the success of his mixtapes ''Bird Money'' and ''Writing on the Wall'', Gucci Mane signed with [[Warner Bros. Records]] in May 2009.<ref>{{cite web|author=Jason|title=Gucci Mane Signs New Deal With Warner|url=http://www.rapbasement.com/gucci-mane/051309-gucci-mane-signs-new-record-deal-with-warner-brothers-records-drops-two-new-songs-listen-now.html|work=Rap Basement|date=May 13, 2009|access-date=May 14, 2009|archive-date=May 14, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090514172833/http://www.rapbasement.com/gucci-mane/051309-gucci-mane-signs-new-record-deal-with-warner-brothers-records-drops-two-new-songs-listen-now.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

He appeared on remixes of the songs "[[Boom Boom Pow]]" by [[The Black Eyed Peas]], "[[Obsessed (Mariah Carey song)|Obsessed]]" by [[Mariah Carey]] and "[[5 Star (song)|5 Star Chick]]" by [[Yo Gotti]] and made a guest appearance on [[Mario (entertainer)|Mario]]'s "Break Up." He made a total of 17 guest appearances in 2009. Gucci Mane's sixth studio album, ''[[The State vs. Radric Davis]]'', was released by Warner Bros. Records on December 8, 2009. Its lead single, "[[Wasted (Gucci Mane song)|Wasted]]" featuring [[Plies (rapper)|Plies]], was originally from Gucci Mane's 2009 mixtape ''Guccimania''. It peaked at number 36 on the Hot 100, number 3 on the Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs, and number 3 on the [[Rap Songs]], making it Gucci Mane's most successful single to date. The second single was "[[Spotlight (Gucci Mane song)|Spotlight]]" featuring [[Usher (entertainer)|Usher]]. The third single was "[[Lemonade (Gucci Mane song)|Lemonade]]." The fourth single was "[[Bingo (Gucci Mane song)|Bingo]]", featuring [[Waka Flocka Flame]] and [[Soulja Boy]]. On October 2, 2009, Gucci Mane was listed at number 6 on MTV's annual [[MTV's Annual Hottest MC List|Hottest MC in the Game list]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|title=Gucci Mane Balances 'Hood, Mainstream To Be No. 6 Hottest MC In The Game|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1622767/20091001/index.jhtml|publisher=MTV News|date=October 1, 2009|access-date=October 9, 2009|archive-date=October 5, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091005084643/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1622767/20091001/index.jhtml|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===2010–2012: ''The Appeal: Georgia's Most Wanted'' and other projects===
After being released from jail,{{clarify|reason=when/why was he incarcerated|date=December 2017}} Gucci Mane stated that he would start or change the label name from So Icey Entertainment to 1017 Brick Squad Records. ''The Appeal: Georgia's Most Wanted'', was released on September 28, 2010.<ref name= "twitter2">{{cite tweet| user= gucci1017| date= 2010-08-01| number= 20105260440 |title= brrrrr da appeal drops sept 28 free concert for kids going back to school center stage i cant wait brrrrrrrr| archiveurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20140111162057/https://twitter.com/gucci1017/status/20105260440 |archivedate=January 11, 2014 | access-date= August 2, 2010}}</ref> The first single from this release was "[[Gucci Time]]",<ref name="twitter">{{cite tweet| user= gucci1017 |date= July 30, 2010| number= 19923728387 |title= Its Gucci Time! Stay tuned| archiveurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20140111195919/https://twitter.com/gucci1017/status/19923728387 |archivedate= January 11, 2014 | accessdate=}}</ref> produced by and featuring [[Swizz Beatz]]. It premiered on Gucci Mane's MySpace page on August 6<ref name="twitter3">{{cite tweet| user= gucci1017 | number= 20498377455 |title= It's #GucciTime ! @Gucci1017 & @THEREALSWIZZZ – official version premiere on Myspace – myspace.com/guccimane! RT #GucciTime| date= 2010-08-06| archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140111191728/https://twitter.com/gucci1017/status/20498377455 |archivedate= January 11, 2014 | access-date= 2026-04-13}}</ref> and was released to US urban radio stations on August 24, 2010.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allaccess.com/urban/future-releases |title= Urban/UAC Future Releases |publisher= | website= allaccess.com |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=December 28, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111228213714/http://www.allaccess.com/urban/future-releases |url-status=live}}</ref>

On March 18, 2011, Gucci Mane released his 10th EP, ''[[The Return of Mr. Zone 6]]'', mostly produced by [[Drumma Boy]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/the-return-of-mr-zone-6/424479077 |title=The Return of Mr. Zone 6 by Gucci Mane |publisher=iTunes Store |date=March 18, 2011 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=March 22, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120322184238/http://itunes.apple.com/us/album/the-return-of-mr.-zone-6/id424479077 |url-status=live}}</ref> It debuted at No. 18 on the ''Billboard'' 200, and is his highest charting EP. The album also debuted at no. 2 on the Rap albums chart and No. 8 on the R&B/Hip-Hop Albums chart. He also released a collaboration album with Waka Flocka Flame named ''[[Ferrari Boyz]]'' on August 5, Gucci Mane's first collaboration album.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/ferrari-boyz-deluxe-version/449357287 |title= Ferrari Boyz (Deluxe Version) by Gucci Mane & Waka Flocka Flame |publisher=iTunes Store |date=August 5, 2011 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=February 16, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120216052019/http://itunes.apple.com/us/album/ferrari-boyz-deluxe-version/id449357287 |url-status=live}}</ref> Its first single was "She Be Puttin On" featuring Brick Squad labelmate Slim Dunkin. ''Ferrari Boyz'' debuted at No. 21 on the ''Billboard'' 200. Gucci Mane released another collaboration album, this time with rapper [[V-Nasty]], called ''[[BAYTL]]'', on December 13.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/baytl/479744007 |title= Baytl by Gucci Mane & V-Nasty |publisher=iTunes Store |date=December 9, 2011 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=March 4, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120304113819/http://itunes.apple.com/us/album/baytl/id479744007 |url-status=live}}</ref> The album's first single was "Whip Appeal" featuring P2theLA.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/whip-appeal-feat-p2thela-single/478686629 |title= Whip Appeal (feat. P2theLA) – Single by Gucci Mane & V-Nasty |publisher=iTunes Store |date=November 18, 2011 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=December 8, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111208034605/http://itunes.apple.com/us/album/whip-appeal-feat.-p2thela/id478686629 |url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:Gucci Mane performing at the Williamsburg Waterfront 2.jpg|thumb|alt=Davis performing in [[Williamsburg, Brooklyn]], on August 29, 2010. He is rapping into his microphone.|Gucci Mane performing on August 29, 2010|238x238px]]

Three days after ''BAYTL''{{'}}s release, while traveling to the video shoot of "Push Ups", Slim Dunkin, who was featured on the song, was shot and killed after an argument at an Atlanta recording studio.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.foxnews.com/us/rapper-slim-dunkin-shot-dead-at-atlanta-music-studio/ | publisher=Fox News Channel | title=Rapper Slim Dunkin Shot Dead at Atlanta Music Studio | date=December 16, 2011 | access-date=December 18, 2011 | archive-date=December 18, 2011 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111218000430/http://www.foxnews.com/us/2011/12/16/rapper-slim-dunkin-shot-dead-at-atlanta-music-studio/ | url-status=live}}</ref> On February 5, 2012, Gucci Mane released his ''[[Trap Back]]'' mixtape. It features guest appearances from [[Yo Gotti]], [[Rocko (rapper)|Rocko]], Waka Flocka Flame, [[Jadakiss]], [[2 Chainz]], and [[Future (rapper)|Future]], the latter three appearing on the third studio album of Jeezy (who had removed the "Young" from his name) ''[[Thug Motivation 103: Hustlerz Ambition]]''. The first single was the mixtape's title track.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rapsandrings.com/2012/01/18/new-music-gucci-mane-trap-back/ |title=New Music: Gucci Mane – Trap Back &#124; Raps + Rings |publisher=Rapsandrings.com |date=January 18, 2012 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120204030034/http://www.rapsandrings.com/2012/01/18/new-music-gucci-mane-trap-back/ |archive-date=February 4, 2012}}</ref> Music videos were filmed for the songs "Quiet", "Face Card", "In Love With a White Girl" featuring Yo Gotti, "Chicken Room" featuring Rocko, the title track, and "Sometimes" featuring Future.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/07/gucci-mane-quiet-music-video/ |title=Gucci Mane: Quiet (Music Video) |date=February 7, 2012 |publisher=Ballerstatus.Com |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=February 12, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120212110328/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/07/gucci-mane-quiet-music-video/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/02/gucci-mane-face-card-music-video/ |title=Gucci Mane: Face Card (Music Video) |date=February 2, 2012 |publisher=Ballerstatus.Com |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=March 28, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120328052934/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/02/gucci-mane-face-card-music-video/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/16/gucci-mane-ft-yo-gotti-im-in-love-with-a-white-girl-music-video/ |title=Gucci Mane ft. Yo Gotti: I'm In Love With A White Girl (Music Video) |publisher=Ballerstatus.Com |date=February 16, 2012 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=February 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120219214424/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/16/gucci-mane-ft-yo-gotti-im-in-love-with-a-white-girl-music-video/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/08/gucci-mane-ft-rocko-chicken-room-music-video/ |title=Gucci Mane ft. Rocko: Chicken Room (Music Video) |date=February 8, 2012 |publisher=Ballerstatus.Com |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=February 12, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120212082818/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2012/02/08/gucci-mane-ft-rocko-chicken-room-music-video/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.killerhiphop.com/video-gucci-mane-trap-back-trailer/ |title=Video: Gucci Mane – Trap Back (Trailer) |publisher= |website= KillerHipHop.com |date=February 4, 2012 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=August 30, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130830191232/http://www.killerhiphop.com/video-gucci-mane-trap-back-trailer/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.killerhiphop.com/gucci-mane-ft-future-sometimes-video/ |title=Video: Gucci Mane ft Future – Sometimes |publisher= | website= KillerHipHop.com |date=January 15, 2012 |access-date=June 12, 2012 |archive-date=January 27, 2012 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120127044048/http://www.killerhiphop.com/gucci-mane-ft-future-sometimes-video/ |url-status=live}}</ref> ''Trap Back'' was met with positive reviews, scoring a 7.8 from [[Pitchfork Media]], a 7.5 from [[AllHipHop]], and an "L" from ''[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]]''.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/16280-gucci-mane-trap-back/ |title=Gucci Mane: Trap Back &#124; Album Reviews |work=Pitchfork Media |date=February 16, 2012 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=April 8, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120408072252/http://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/16280-gucci-mane-trap-back/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Mos |last=Jones |url=http://allhiphop.com/2012/02/07/mixtape-review-gucci-manes-trap-back/ |title=Mixtape Review: Gucci Mane's "Trap Back" |website=AllHipHop.com |date=February 7, 2012 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=April 11, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120411105220/http://allhiphop.com/2012/02/07/mixtape-review-gucci-manes-trap-back/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/reviews/2012/02/gucci-mane-trap-back-2/ |title=Gucci Mane, Trap Back |publisher= |work= [[XXL (magazine)|XXL Magazine]] |date=March 27, 2012 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=March 20, 2012 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120320174450/http://www.xxlmag.com/reviews/2012/02/gucci-mane-trap-back-2/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

On May 25, 2012, Gucci Mane released another mixtape, ''[[I'm Up (Gucci Mane mixtape)|I'm Up]]''. Music videos were released for the songs "Supa Cocky", "Kansas" featuring Jim Jones, "Wish You Would" featuring Verse Simmonds, and "Too Damn Sexy" featuring Jeremih. Gucci Mane released yet another mixtape, ''[[Trap God]]'', on October 17, 2012. The mixtape included features from Brick Squad affiliate Waka Flocka Flame and frequent collaborators Rick Ross, Future, Meek Mill and Birdman, among others. On September 13, 2012, he appeared on Waka Flocka Flame's mixtape ''Salute Me Or Shoot Me 4'' on two tracks.

===2013: ''Trap House III'' and ''The State vs. Radric Davis II: The Caged Bird Sings''===
On February 12, 2013, Gucci Mane released his mixtape ''[[Trap God 2]]''. By the end of March, Gucci Mane released three more mixtapes – ''Free Bricks 2'' with [[Young Scooter]], ''Trap Back 2'' and ''EastAtlantaMemphis'' with [[Young Dolph]]. Gucci Mane announced in February 2013 that he was releasing a new studio album entitled ''[[Trap House III]]'', the third installment of his ''Trap House'' series.<ref>{{cite web |last=Horowitz |first=Steven J. |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.23113/title.gucci-mane-trap-house-iii-cover-art-release-date |title=Gucci Mane 'Trap House III' Cover Art & Release Date |publisher=| website= HipHop DX |date=March 6, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130530022044/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.23113/title.gucci-mane-trap-house-iii-cover-art-release-date |archive-date=May 30, 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref> It was released on May 21, 2013. On May 31, 2013, Gucci Mane also announced he was releasing a new album entitled ''Mr. GuWop'' later that year, featuring appearances by personal friend and [[industrial rock]] performer [[Marilyn Manson]], who also helped him to give shape to the album's sound and musical direction.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://rapfix.mtv.com/2013/05/31/gucci-manes-mr-guwop-album-cover-dont-go-chasing-waterfalls/ |title=Gucci Mane's Mr. Guwop Album Cover: Don't Go Chasing Waterfalls? |publisher=MTV |date=May 31, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131213191714/http://rapfix.mtv.com/2013/05/31/gucci-manes-mr-guwop-album-cover-dont-go-chasing-waterfalls/ |archive-date=December 13, 2013}}</ref> On June 5, 2013, Gucci Mane announced that 1017 Brick Squad would release their first group compilation album, ''Big Money Talk'', in 2013.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/10902-gucci-mane-speaks-on-who-owns-brick-squad-and-waka-flocka-on-compilation-album-news |title=Gucci Mane Speaks On Who Owns Brick Squad, Waka Flocka & Compilation Album |publisher= | website= hotnewhiphop.com |date=June 7, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=April 16, 2014 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140416074546/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-speaks-on-who-owns-brick-squad-and-waka-flocka-on-compilation-album-news.5968.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

On September 7, 2013, various members of 1017 Brick Squad and Brick Squad Monopoly traded shots back and forth on Twitter, including Gucci Mane, Waka Flocka Flame, Frenchie, Wooh Da Kid, and OJ da Juiceman. Gucci Mane would go on to say "fuck brick squad" and accuse his former manager, Waka Flocka Flame's mother Debra Antney, of stealing money from OJ da Juiceman and [[French Montana]]. Frenchie also accused Gucci Mane of paying for Young Vito's lawyer, the accused murderer of former 1017 Brick Squad artist Slim Dunkin, which Gucci Mane denied. Frenchie would release a diss record towards Gucci Mane the following day.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-end-brick-squad-on-twitter |title=Gucci Mane, Waka Flocka Flame, End Brick Squad on Twitter |magazine=Complex |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=November 5, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131105084720/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-end-brick-squad-on-twitter |url-status=live}}</ref> During the following days, it was revealed that OJ da Juiceman, Young Dolph, Frenchie and Wooh da Kid were no longer, or in some cases had never been, signed to 1017 Brick Squad Records.{{citation needed|date=February 2015}}<!-- WP:RS needed; removed link to WP:SPS (Twitter, FB, WP)--><ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-oj-da-juiceman-brick-squad-twitter-wars |title=Gucci Mane Waka Flocka Flame Beef, OJ Da Juiceman, Brick Squad Twitter Wars |magazine=Complex |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=November 4, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131104150202/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-oj-da-juiceman-brick-squad-twitter-wars |url-status=live}}</ref> It was also revealed that 1017 Brick Squad had lost their distribution deal with Atlantic Records, and the label might be disbanded.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-is-dropped-from-atlantic-records/ |title=Gucci Mane Is Dropped From Atlantic Records |publisher= |work= XXL Magazine |date=September 9, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=December 3, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131203002431/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-is-dropped-from-atlantic-records/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="xxlmag.com">{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/timeline-gucci-manes-twitter-rant/7/ |title=A Timeline Of Gucci Mane's Twitter Rant |page= 7 |publisher= |work= XXL Magazine |date=September 9, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=December 2, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131202231055/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/timeline-gucci-manes-twitter-rant/7/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

On September 9, 2013, Gucci Mane advertised on Twitter that he sold the recording contracts of what he considered his main artists: Waka Flocka Flame, Young Scooter, and [[Young Thug]].<ref name="xxlmag.com"/> During his Twitter tirade he dissed many rappers and producers such as [[Nicki Minaj]], [[Plies (rapper)|Plies]], [[Drake (rapper)|Drake]], [[Rocko (rapper)|Rocko]], [[Polow da Don]], 2 Chainz, [[Rick Ross]], Jeezy, [[T.I.]], Yo Gotti, Frenchie, [[808 Mafia]], Waka Flocka Flame and [[Tyga]] among others.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/t-nicki-minaj-others-respond-gucci-mane/ |title=T.I., Nicki Minaj & Others Respond To Gucci Mane |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |date=September 9, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=December 2, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131202223041/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/t-nicki-minaj-others-respond-gucci-mane/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The following day he released a new single, "Stealing", featuring OJ da Juiceman, produced by [[Zaytoven]]. On the song he disses T.I., Jeezy, and Yo Gotti. That night he also released a new mixtape ''Diary of a Trap God''.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-oj-da-juiceman-stealing |title=Gucci Mane f/ OJ Da Juiceman "Stealing" |magazine=Complex |date=September 10, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921061928/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-oj-da-juiceman-stealing |url-status=live}}</ref> Following the mixtape's release his barrage of tweets stopped, and he claimed his Twitter account was hacked by his former manager Coach K for $5,000.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-claims-coach-k-hacked-his-twitter-account-for-5k/ |title=Gucci Mane Claims Coach K Hacked His Twitter Account For 5K |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |date=September 11, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=December 2, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131202225023/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-claims-coach-k-hacked-his-twitter-account-for-5k/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Subsequently, he deleted all the controversial tweets and hours later deleted his Twitter account.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-just-deleted-twitter-page/ |title=Gucci Mane Just Deleted His Twitter Page |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |date=September 11, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=December 2, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131202223246/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-just-deleted-twitter-page/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

On September 22, 2013, Gucci Mane admitted to making the tweets, and claimed he was binging on [[codeine]] and [[promethazine]] during the time. He went on to apologize to the fans, his family, and the music industry members he offended. Gucci Mane also said that he would be going to rehab while incarcerated on his gun charge.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-apologizes-behavior-lean-destroyed-heading-rehab-addiction/ |title=Gucci Mane Apologizes For Behavior "Lean Destroyed Me," Heading To Rehab For Addiction |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |date=September 22, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=December 3, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131203000849/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/09/gucci-mane-apologizes-behavior-lean-destroyed-heading-rehab-addiction/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Plans to release his album ''Mr. GuWop'' were scrapped indefinitely, and Gucci Mane would go on to release his tenth studio album, ''[[The State vs. Radric Davis II: The Caged Bird Sings]]'', on Christmas Day 2013.

===2014–2016: Incarceration; multiple project releases from prison===
Gucci Mane released his first music of 2014 on April 20, a free collaborative mixtape with Young Thug called ''Young Thugga Mane La Flare''. He later released another collaborative project with 1017 BrickSquad artists, ''Brick Factory Vol. 1'', on May 24. Then, he released three digital albums on the same day, ''The Purple Album'' (with Young Thug), ''The Green Album'' (with [[Migos]]) and ''The White Album'' (with [[Peewee Longway]]), collectively known as ''World War 3D''. His eighth digital album, ''[[Trap House 4]]'', was released on July 4, 2014. The album features guest appearances from [[Chief Keef]], Young Scooter, [[K Camp]] and [[Fredo Santana]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/trap-house-4/id894840645 |title= Trap House 4 by Gucci Mane |publisher=iTunes Store |date=July 4, 2014 |access-date=July 31, 2014 |archive-date=July 6, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140706104819/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/trap-house-4/id894840645 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

On July 17, 2014, he released a collaborative album with Young Dolph and PeeWee Longway (labeled as Felix Brothers) called ''Felix Brothers''. On July 22, 2014, Gucci Mane announced that he would release a new album, titled ''The Oddfather'', on the day he would go back to court, July 28, 2014.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/the-oddfather/id904001313 |title= The Oddfather by Gucci Mane |publisher=iTunes Store |date=February 2, 1980 |access-date=July 31, 2014 |archive-date=August 19, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140819072948/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/the-oddfather/id904001313 |url-status=dead}}</ref> On August 15, 2014, Gucci Mane released his tenth digital album, ''Gucci Vs Guwop''.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/gucci-vs-guwop/id908731863 |title= Gucci vs. Guwop by Gucci Mane |publisher= iTunes Store |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141221201112/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/gucci-vs-guwop/id908731863 |archive-date= December 21, 2014 |url-status= |access-date=}}</ref> Gucci Mane released the follow-up digital album to the mixtape ''Brick Factory Vol. 1'', ''Brick Factory Vol. 2'',<ref>{{cite web |url= https://music.apple.com/us/album/brick-factory-vol-2/915443649 |title= Brick Factory, Vol. 2 by Gucci Mane |publisher= iTunes Store |access-date=}}</ref> on September 3. On September 13, Gucci Mane released a free mixtape, ''The Return of Mr. Perfect'', the follow-up to his mixtape ''Mr. Perfect''. Following his September releases he started promoting ''Trap God 3'', which was released on October 17, 2014,<ref>{{cite web |url= https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/trap-god-3/id930110095 |title= Trap God 3 by Gucci Mane | publisher= iTunes Store |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150504185937/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/trap-god-3/id930110095 |archive-date= May 4, 2015 |url-status= |access-date=}}</ref> and became the highest-charting release during his prison sentence. On October 31, 2014, Gucci Mane and Chief Keef released a collaborative mixtape titled ''Big Gucci Sosa''.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/big-gucci-sosa/id935403183 |title= Big Gucci Sosa by Gucci Mane & Chief Keef |publisher= iTunes Store |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141106134312/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/big-gucci-sosa/id935403183 |archive-date= November 6, 2014 |url-status= |access-date=}}</ref>

On Christmas Day, Gucci Mane released his 13th mixtape, ''[[East Atlanta Santa]]'', featuring artists such as [[Raury]], [[Shawty Lo]] and OJ da Juiceman. He capped off the year by teaming up with producer Honorable C Note for a Christmas mixtape, ''C-Note Vs. Gucci'', a compilation of old collaborations between the two and new records.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.trapclassics.com/gucci-mane-east-atlanta-santa/ |title= Gucci Mane – East Atlanta Santa &#91;Full Album Stream&#93; + Download |website= trapclassics.com |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141225172720/http://www.trapclassics.com/gucci-mane-east-atlanta-santa/ |archive-date= December 25, 2014 |url-status= |access-date=}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url= http://rapxclusive.com/mixtape-gucci-mane-c-note-vs-gucci/ |title= Mixtape: Gucci Mane – C-Note Vs. Gucci |last= Igor |first= P. |website= rapxclusive.com |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141225175046/http://rapxclusive.com/mixtape-gucci-mane-c-note-vs-gucci/ |archive-date= December 25, 2014 |url-status= dead |access-date=}}</ref> In total, he released over twelve projects in 2014 and made over $1,300,000 from prison.{{citation needed|date=January 2023}}

Gucci Mane released his 14th mixtape on January 3, 2015, ''[[1017 Mafia: Incarcerated]]''.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://music.apple.com/us/album/1017-mafia/955168302 |title= iTunes Store |website= music.apple.com |access-date=}}</ref> On his birthday, February 12, 2015, he released the third installment of his Brick Factory series, ''[[Brick Factory 3]]''.<ref>{{cite tweet| user= gucci1017| number= 566046051426377728 |title= https://web.archive.org/web/20160305235546/https://twitter.com/gucci1017/status/566046051426377728| date= 2015-02-12 |url-status= live |archiveurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20160305235546/https://twitter.com/gucci1017/status/566046051426377728 |archivedate=March 5, 2016 | access-date= April 13, 2026}}</ref> Soon after, on February 18, 2015, Gucci Mane released a surprise EP, ''Views From Zone 6''. The title is a play on words on the upcoming album from [[Drake (rapper)|Drake]] ''Views From The 6''.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/30135-stream-gucci-mane-s-new-ep-views-from-zone-6-news |title= Stream Gucci Mane's New EP 'Views From Zone 6' |last= Lilah |first= Rose |website= HotNewHipHop.com |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150218185713/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/stream-gucci-mane-s-new-ep-views-from-zone-6-news.14028.html |archive-date= February 18, 2015 |url-status= |access-date=}}</ref> Gucci Mane released a triple digital album for the third time on March 20, 2015: ''Breakfast'', ''Lunch'' and ''Dinner''. It includes guest features from [[iLoveMakonnen]], Waka Flocka Flame, Chief Keef, [[Rich The Kid]], [[Andy Milonakis]] and others. Four days later, Gucci Mane released a follow-up EP entitled ''Dessert'', containing production from [[Mike Will Made It]] and Honorable C-Note.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.stereogum.com/1789333/stream-gucci-mane-dessert-ep/mp3s/album-stream/|title=Stream Gucci Mane Dessert EP|work=Stereogum|date=March 24, 2015|access-date=June 14, 2015|archive-date=July 3, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703134620/http://www.stereogum.com/1789333/stream-gucci-mane-dessert-ep/mp3s/album-stream/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On April 6, 2015, Gucci Mane released the final installment of his ''Trap House'' series, ''Trap House 5 (The Last Chapter)'', the first installment in the series to be released as a mixtape. Production was handled by Mike Will Made It, Zaytoven and Honorable C-Note, and it had guest features from Young Thug, Peewee Longway and Chief Keef.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.datpiff.com/Gucci-Mane-Trap-House-5-the-Final-Chapter-mixtape.700375.html|title=Gucci Mane – Trap House 5 (the Final Chapter)|work=DatPiff|access-date=June 14, 2015|archive-date=June 13, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150613065049/http://www.datpiff.com/Gucci-Mane-Trap-House-5-the-Final-Chapter-mixtape.700375.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 20, 2015, La Flare released his tenth project of the year, ''King Gucci'', with appearances from [[Fetty Wap]], [[Migos]], PeeWee Longway and [[RiFF RAFF]] and production from Chief Keef, TM88, [[Metro Boomin]], and Zaytoven.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rap-up.com/2015/05/20/new-mixtape-gucci-mane-king-gucci/|title=New Mixtape: Gucci Mane – 'King Gucci'|work=Rap-Up|access-date=June 14, 2015|archive-date=May 25, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525223903/http://www.rap-up.com/2015/05/20/new-mixtape-gucci-mane-king-gucci/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/news/id.33929/title.gucci-mane-king-gucci-release-date-cover-art-tracklist-download-mixtape-stream|title=Gucci Mane "King Gucci" Release Date, Cover Art, Tracklist, Download & Mixtape Stream|date=May 20, 2015|work=HipHopDX|access-date=June 14, 2015|archive-date=July 3, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703114006/http://hiphopdx.com/news/id.33929/title.gucci-mane-king-gucci-release-date-cover-art-tracklist-download-mixtape-stream|url-status=live}}</ref> On December 25, 2015, Gucci Mane released a mixtape titled ''East Atlanta Santa 2''.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/lb/album/east-atlanta-santa-2/1070990733 |title=iTunes – Music – East Atlanta Santa 2 by Gucci Mane |publisher=[[iTunes]] |date=December 25, 2014 |access-date=October 29, 2019 |archive-date=October 29, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191029103555/https://music.apple.com/lb/album/east-atlanta-santa-2/1070990733 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On April 26, 2016, Gucci Mane released a 36-track compilation album on [[iTunes]] consisting of his ''Breakfast'', ''Lunch'', ''Dinner'', and ''Dessert'' projects called ''Meal Ticket.''<ref>{{cite web |url=http://karencivil.com/2016/04/29/gucci-mane-drops-36-track-album-meal-ticket/ |title=Gucci Mane releases 36-track album |last=Holz |first=Jay |date=April 29, 2016 |website=Encyclopedia of Things |access-date=April 29, 2016 |archive-date=May 2, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160502205228/http://karencivil.com/2016/04/29/gucci-mane-drops-36-track-album-meal-ticket/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

===2016–2018: Release, ''Everybody Looking'', ''The Return of East Atlanta Santa'', ''Mr. Davis'' & ''Evil Genius''===
On May 26, 2016, Gucci Mane was released from prison five months early: it was ruled that the five months he had spent in prison waiting for his trial had not yet been deducted from his sentence when they should have been. The next day, Gucci Mane released the first single under his new deal with Atlantic Records, "[[First Day Out tha Feds]]".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/463728-gucci-mane-announces-first-day-out-tha-feds-dropping-tonight-news |title=Gucci announces FirstDayOutThaFeds |last=Smith |first=Trevor |date=May 27, 2016 |website= HotNewHipHop.com |access-date=May 27, 2016 |archive-date=May 28, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160528150645/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-announces-first-day-out-tha-feds-dropping-tonight-news.21870.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/entertainthis/2016/05/27/gucci-mane-releases-new-music-after-early-prison-release/85032334/|title=Gucci Mane returns with 'First Day Out Tha Feds,' promises new mixtape|date=May 27, 2015|work=USA Today|access-date=May 27, 2016|archive-date=May 27, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160527170537/http://www.usatoday.com/story/life/entertainthis/2016/05/27/gucci-mane-releases-new-music-after-early-prison-release/85032334/|url-status=live}}</ref> On June 3, 2016, Gucci Mane appeared on "Champions", the first single off of [[GOOD Music]]'s ''Cruel Winter'' album.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://pitchfork.com/news/64531-kanye-west-premieres-cruel-winter-first-single-featuring-big-sean-gucci-mane-travis-scott-2-chainz-more/ |title=Kanye West Premieres Cruel Winter First Single Featuring Big Sean, Gucci Mane, Travis Scott, 2 Chainz, More |date=June 3, 2016 |website=Pitchfork |access-date=June 3, 2016 |archive-date=June 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160604105402/http://pitchfork.com/news/64531-kanye-west-premieres-cruel-winter-first-single-featuring-big-sean-gucci-mane-travis-scott-2-chainz-more/ |url-status=live}}</ref> On June 17, 2016, Gucci Mane made his first performance since being released from prison at the Elan Mansion, an Atlanta club.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://pigeonsandplanes.com/2016/06/gucci-mane-welcome-home-concert-footage/ |title=Gucci Mane makes first post-prison performance |last=Siber |first=Alex |date=June 18, 2016 |website=pigeonsandplanes |access-date=June 18, 2016 |archive-date=June 21, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160621034537/http://pigeonsandplanes.com/2016/06/gucci-mane-welcome-home-concert-footage/ |url-status=live}}</ref> On June 25, 2016, he announced his ninth studio album and first since his release from prison, ''[[Everybody Looking]]'', which was released on July 22, 2016.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/53809-gucci-mane-announces-new-project-everybody-looking-reveals-artwork-and-release-date-news |title=Gucci Mane announces new album |last=Goddard |first=Kevin |date=June 25, 2016 |website= HotNewHipHop.com |access-date=June 25, 2016 |archive-date=June 26, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160626131549/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-announces-new-project-everybody-looking-reveals-artwork-and-release-date-news.22479.html |url-status=live}}</ref> One day later he headlined the "Gucci & Friends" concert at the Fox Theatre in Atlanta; the show featured Fetty Wap, 2 Chainz, Future and Drake.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2016/jul/23/gucci-mane-review-atlanta-drake-fetty-wap|title=Gucci Mane review – Drake and Fetty Wap join rapper in fresh start after prison|first=Christina|last=Lee|website=[[TheGuardian.com]]|date=July 23, 2016|access-date=December 14, 2016|archive-date=December 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161215054751/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2016/jul/23/gucci-mane-review-atlanta-drake-fetty-wap|url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2016, Gucci Mane collaborated with [[Rae Sremmurd]] on the single "[[Black Beatles]]", which in November 2016 reached number one on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100, Gucci Mane's first number-one single as a featured artist.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/hot-100-rae-sremmurd-black-beatles-number-one/|title=Rae Sremmurd's 'Black Beatles' Blasts to No. 1 on Hot 100|newspaper=Billboard|access-date=January 10, 2017|archive-date= January 4, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170104160936/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/chart-beat/7573655/hot-100-rae-sremmurd-black-beatles-number-one|url-status=live}}</ref> The song was described by ''Billboard'' as the "unexpected commercial high point for Gucci"<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/rae-sremmurd-black-beatles-mannequin-challenge-hot-100-7573403/|title=Rae Sremmurd's Weird, Wonderful 'Black Beatles' is Much More Than a Viral Hit| work= [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |access-date=January 10, 2017 |archive-date=January 11, 2017| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170111001809/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/7573403/rae-sremmurd-black-beatles-mannequin-challenge-hot-100|url-status=live}}</ref> as it topped the Billboard Hot 100 for 7 non-consecutive weeks between November 2016 and January 2017.

He released ''[[Woptober]]'' on October 14, 2016, and released a side project called ''Free Bricks 2'' with Future.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/54504-stream-gucci-mane-and-futures-free-bricks-2-zone-6-edition-project-news |title=Gucci Mane and Future team up for Free Bricks 2 mixtape |last=Goddard |first=Kevin |date=November 15, 2016 |website= HotNewHipHop.com |access-date=November 15, 2016 |archive-date=November 15, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161115193843/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/stream-gucci-mane-and-futures-free-bricks-2-zone-6-edition-project-news.25467.html |url-status=live}}</ref> On November 23, 2016, Gucci Mane and [[Lil Uzi Vert]] released a collaborative 7-track EP, "[[1017 vs. The World]]".

His tenth studio album, "[[The Return of East Atlanta Santa]]", was released on December 16, 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/return-east-atlanta-santa/1186968887|title=The Return of East Atlanta Santa|website=[[iTunes]]|date=December 16, 2016|access-date=January 29, 2017|archive-date=January 23, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170123125334/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/return-east-atlanta-santa/id1186968887|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 26, 2017, Gucci Mane released ''[[Droptopwop]]'', a collaborative mixtape with Metro Boomin. The 10-track mixtape features Rick Ross, [[Offset (rapper)|Offset of Migos]], Young Dolph and 2 Chainz.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-droptopwop-mixtape-stream-7809496/|title=Gucci Mane Releases Metro Boomin-Produced 'Droptopwop' Mixtape|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|access-date=May 28, 2017|archive-date=May 27, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170527143753/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/7809496/gucci-mane-droptopwop-mixtape-stream|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/droptopwop/1239965993 |title=Droptopwop by Gucci Mane on Apple Music |date=May 26, 2017 |publisher=iTunes |access-date=May 26, 2017 |archive-date=June 1, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170601010055/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/droptopwop/id1239965993 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In August 2017, Gucci Mane announced his eleventh studio album, ''[[Mr. Davis]]''.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.rap-up.com/2017/08/17/gucci-mane-announces-new-album-mr-davis/|title=Gucci Mane Announces New Album 'Mr. Davis'|work=Rap-Up|access-date=September 14, 2017|language=en-US|archive-date=September 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170918115357/http://www.rap-up.com/2017/08/17/gucci-mane-announces-new-album-mr-davis/|url-status=live}}</ref> The album was released on October 13, 2017.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-new-album-mr-davis-details-i-get-the-bag-video-7934032/|title=Gucci Mane Announces 'Mr. Davis' Album, Unleashes 'I Get The Bag' Visual With Migos|magazine=Billboard|access-date=September 14, 2017|archive-date=September 23, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170923024711/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/7934032/gucci-mane-new-album-mr-davis-details-i-get-the-bag-video|url-status=live}}</ref> It includes "[[I Get the Bag]]" featuring Migos, which peaked at number 11 on the ''[[Billboard Hot 100]]'', his most successful single as a solo artist to date.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.billboard.com/artist/303227/gucci-mane/chart|title=Gucci Mane Chart History|access-date=October 29, 2017|archive-date=September 28, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170928061948/http://www.billboard.com/artist/303227/gucci-mane/chart|url-status=live}}</ref>

Two months later, Gucci Mane released his twelfth studio album ''[[El Gato: The Human Glacier]]'', produced entirely by [[Southside (record producer)|Southside]]. A week after its release, in similar fashion to the announcements of previous projects, Gucci Mane announced his thirteenth album, ''[[Evil Genius (album)|Evil Genius]]'', released on December 7, 2018.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://www.complex.com/music/2017/12/gucci-mane-announces-new-album|title=Gucci Mane Announces Yet Another Album Dropping Soon|magazine=Complex|date=December 28, 2017|access-date=December 28, 2017|archive-date=December 28, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171228234544/http://www.complex.com/music/2017/12/gucci-mane-announces-new-album|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite tweet|number=946445938603773953|user=gucci1017|title=Next solo album #TheEvilGenius<!-- full text of tweet that Twitter returned to the bot (excluding links) added by TweetCiteBot. This may be better truncated or may need expanding (TW limits responses to 140 characters) or case changes. --> |accessdate=December 28, 2017|archivedate=December 29, 2017|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171229013409/https://twitter.com/gucci1017/status/946445938603773953|date=December 28, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-shares-evil-genius-album-track-list-release-date-8483598/|title=Gucci Mane Shares Release Date and Track List For 'Evil Genius'|magazine=Billboard|access-date=December 6, 2018|archive-date=December 4, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181204044847/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8483598/gucci-mane-shares-evil-genius-album-track-list-release-date|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2019–present: ''East Atlanta Santa 3'', ''So Icy Summer,'' ''So Icy Gang Vol. 1, Delusions of Grandeur, Woptober ll, Ice Daddy,'' and ''Breath of Fresh Air.''===
In 2019, he released two studio albums, including [[Delusions of Grandeur (Gucci Mane album)|''Delusions of Grandeur'']] and [[Woptober II|Woptober ll]]. On July 3, 2020, Gucci Mane released the 24-track ''So Icy Summer'' compilation album, marking his first project of 2020, following ''[[East Atlanta Santa 3]]'' in 2019.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rap-up.com/2020/07/01/gucci-mane-so-icy-summer-tracklist/ |title= Gucci Mane Reveals ‘So Icy Summer’ Tracklist | work=[[Rap Up]]|date=July 2020 |access-date=July 3, 2020|archive-date=July 2, 2020|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200702100723/https://www.rap-up.com/2020/07/01/gucci-mane-so-icy-summer-tracklist/|url-status=live}}</ref> On July 30, he collaborated with rapper [[Mulatto (rapper)|Mulatto]] for the single "[[Muwop]]", a play on his moniker "Guwop."<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/306601-gucci-mane-reacts-to-mulatto-recreating-his-classic-album-covers-news|title=Gucci Mane Reacts To Mulatto Recreating His Classic Album Covers |website= HotNewHipHop.com |last=Zidel|first=Alex|date=July 28, 2020|access-date=July 30, 2020|archive-date=July 29, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200729090318/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-reacts-to-mulatto-recreating-his-classic-album-covers-news.115094.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

On October 16, 2020, a day before October 17, Gucci Mane released another compilation album with his label [[1017 Records]], titled ''So Icy Gang Vol. 1'', and credited as Gucci Mane & The New 1017, indicating that new members of the label are featured. In 2021, he released his fifteenth studio album, ''[[Ice Daddy]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/312475-gucci-mane-announces-compilation-project-from-the-new-1017-news|title=Gucci Mane Announces Compilation Project From The New 1017|work= HotNewHipHop.com |last=A.|first=Aron|date=October 13, 2020|access-date=October 15, 2020|archive-date=November 1, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201101052058/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-announces-compilation-project-from-the-new-1017-news.119560.html?_amp|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On November 15, 2022, the rapper released the track and video "Letter to Takeoff", dedicated to the rapper [[Takeoff (rapper)|Takeoff]].<ref>{{cite web |url= https://genius.com/a/gucci-mane-remembers-takeoff-and-other-fallen-rappers-on-new-song-letter-to-takeoff |title= Gucci Mane Remembers Takeoff and Other Fallen Rappers On New Song “Letter To Takeoff” |website= genius.com |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221116190205/https://genius.com/a/gucci-mane-remembers-takeoff-and-other-fallen-rappers-on-new-song-letter-to-takeoff |archive-date= 2022-11-16 |url-status= |access-date=}}</ref> Through 2023, Gucci released about ten singles including "[[King Snipe]]", "[[06 Gucci]]", "[[Pissy (song)|Pissy]]", "[[Bluffin (Gucci Mane song)|Bluffin]]", and "[[There I Go (Gucci Mane song)|There I Go]]" which all had guest features, along with other non-guest feature songs that appeared on his sixteenth studio album, ''[[Breath of Fresh Air]]'' released on 10/17/23 and it is his largest project ever, with 24 songs and 12 music videos.

==Other ventures==
===Acting career===
Gucci Mane made his acting debut in the 2012 film ''Birds of a Feather'', co-starring with Producer Zaytoven. The same year, he was featured in ''[[Spring Breakers]]'' which also featured [[James Franco]], [[Selena Gomez]], [[Vanessa Hudgens]], [[Ashley Benson]] and [[Rachel Korine]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/12319-gucci-mane-speaks-on-spring-breakers-and-falling-asleep-for-sex-scene-news |title=Gucci Mane Speaks On 'Spring Breakers' & Falling Asleep For Sex Scene |date=March 22, 2013 |publisher= |website= hotnewhiphop.com |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=March 25, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130325100024/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-speaks-on-spring-breakers-and-falling-asleep-for-sex-scene-news.4984.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Delantic Clothing===
In May 2016, Gucci Mane began teasing images of his upcoming clothing line called Delantic, which will feature a range of apparel, from T-shirts and hoodies to underwear.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2016/06/03/gucci-mane-prepping-delantic-clothing-line/ |title=Gucci Mane Prepping Delantic Clothing Line |publisher=BallerStatus.com |date=June 3, 2016 |access-date=June 3, 2016 |archive-date=June 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160604024843/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2016/06/03/gucci-mane-prepping-delantic-clothing-line/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Autobiography===
In September 2017, Davis published his autobiography, ''The Autobiography of Gucci Mane''.<ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/29/books/review/gucci-mane-autobiography-best-seller.html | title = Gucci Mane's Inspirational Guide to Self-Improvement (in Prison) | date = September 29, 2017 | access-date = November 21, 2017 | newspaper = New York Times | first = Gregory | last = Cowel | archive-date = November 30, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171130144945/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/29/books/review/gucci-mane-autobiography-best-seller.html | url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.npr.org/2017/09/19/551787647/the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane-a-story-of-rap-and-rebirth | title = 'The Autobiography Of Gucci Mane': A Story Of Rap And Rebirth | date = September 19, 2017 | access-date = November 21, 2017 | publisher = NPR | first = Ailsa | last = Chang | author-link = Ailsa Chang | newspaper = NPR.org | archive-date = November 21, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171121125608/https://www.npr.org/2017/09/19/551787647/the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane-a-story-of-rap-and-rebirth | url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine | url = https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8031223/gucci-mane-the-weeknd-curve-video | title = Gucci Mane and The Weeknd Co-Star in New Duplicitous 'Curve' Video | date = November 10, 2017 | access-date = November 21, 2017 | magazine = Billboard | first = Megan | last = Armstrong | archive-date = November 14, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171114212929/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8031223/gucci-mane-the-weeknd-curve-video | url-status = dead}}</ref> The book was written during Davis's time in prison and was authored with Neil Martinez-Belkin, a former ''[[XXL (magazine)|XXL Magazine]]'' editor.<ref>{{cite magazine | url = http://www.newsweek.com/gucci-mane-autobiography-book-release-667301 | title = Gucci Mane Book Review: 12 Revelations From the Rapper's New Autobiography | date = September 21, 2017 | access-date = November 21, 2017 | magazine = [[Newsweek]] | first = Janice | last = Williams | archive-date = November 30, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171130113823/http://www.newsweek.com/gucci-mane-autobiography-book-release-667301 | url-status = live}}</ref> In the weeks before the publication, Davis released a trailer for the book consisting of a short montage directed by videographer [[Cam Kirk]].<ref>{{cite magazine | url = https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane-trailer-7950154/ | title = Gucci Mane Shares Trailer for 'The Autobiography of Gucci Mane' | date = September 5, 2017 | access-date = November 21, 2017 | magazine = Billboard | first = Nerisha | last = Penrose | archive-date = October 9, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171009165738/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/7950154/gucci-mane-the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane-trailer | url-status = live}}</ref> ''The Autobiography of Gucci Mane'' went on to become a ''[[New York Times]]'' bestseller.<ref>{{cite magazine | url = http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/10/gucci-mane-mr-davis-album-sold-first-week/ | title = Here's how much Gucci Mane's 'Mr. Davis' album sold first week | date = October 23, 2017 | access-date = November 21, 2017 | magazine= XXL Magazine |publisher= | first = Peter A. | last = Berry | archive-date = November 25, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171125094233/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/10/gucci-mane-mr-davis-album-sold-first-week | url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url = http://www.philly.com/philly/entertainment/music/sam-smith-gucci-mane-lee-ann-womack-reviews-20171102.html | title = Album reviews: Sam Smith is sad, Gucci Mane is back and Lee Ann Womack sounds better than ever | date = November 2, 2017 | access-date = November 22, 2017 | newspaper = Philadelphia Inquirer | first = Sam | last = Smith | archive-date = December 1, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171201031447/http://www.philly.com/philly/entertainment/music/sam-smith-gucci-mane-lee-ann-womack-reviews-20171102.html | url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.bet.com/article/2pz8jq/love-story-excerpt-the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane | title = A Love Story Excerpt From NY Times Best Seller 'The Autobiography Of Gucci Mane' | access-date = November 22, 2017 | publisher = BET | archive-date = December 1, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171201030708/https://www.bet.com/shows/the-mane-event/content/photos/10/excerpt-from-gucci-autobiography.html | url-status = live}}</ref> The ''New Yorker'' said the book "reads like a set of liner notes that try to impose a central order on his life story."<ref>{{cite magazine | url = https://www.newyorker.com/books/page-turner/the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane-and-the-struggle-to-be-seen | title = "The Autobiography of Gucci Mane" and the Struggle to Be Seen | date = November 17, 2017 | access-date = November 22, 2017 | magazine = New Yorker | first = Melvin | last = Backman | archive-date = November 20, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171120095519/https://www.newyorker.com/books/page-turner/the-autobiography-of-gucci-mane-and-the-struggle-to-be-seen | url-status = live}}</ref>

===Gucci collaboration===
In 2019, Davis started working with Italian fashion house [[Gucci]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.aol.com/article/finance/2019/10/04/luxury-brand-gucci-taps-gucci-mane-for-its-latest-campaign/23827516/|title=Luxury brand Gucci taped Gucci Mane for its latest campaign|date=October 4, 2019 |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191009043923/https://www.aol.com/article/finance/2019/10/04/luxury-brand-gucci-taps-gucci-mane-for-its-latest-campaign/23827516/|archive-date=October 9, 2019}}</ref> The brand had previously not associated itself with the rapper due to his criminal record and image. After a few years of personal development, which included Davis getting married and improving his fitness,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.highsnobiety.com/p/gucci-mane-transformation-2017/|title=A Brief History of Gucci Mane's Incredible Transformation|date=February 12, 2019|website=Highsnobiety.com|access-date=June 26, 2021}}</ref> Gucci announced he would be the face of their Gucci Cruise 2020 Campaign.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2019/10/01/gucci-mane-is-a-face-of-the-gucci-cruise-2020-campaign|title=Gucci Mane is a face of the Gucci Cruise 2020 Campaign |website= The Fader |language=en | publisher= |date=October 2019 |url-status=live|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20191009161441/https://www.thefader.com/2019/10/01/gucci-mane-is-a-face-of-the-gucci-cruise-2020-campaign| archive-date= October 9, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.essence.com/fashion/gucci-mane-stars-in-guccis-ss2020-campaign/|title=Gucci Mane stars in Gucci's SS 2020 Campaign|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191009161437/https://www.essence.com/fashion/gucci-mane-stars-in-guccis-ss2020-campaign/| archive-date=October 9, 2019}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Following his release from prison in 2016, Gucci Mane became the subject of an internet conspiracy theory that stated he was a clone of the "real" Gucci Mane, citing the rapper's new slim physique, a slight change in his voice and his shift towards a healthier lifestyle as supposed proof. Gucci Mane denied that he was a clone and poked fun at the theory in an Instagram post.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 23, 2016 |title=Gucci Mane Addressed A Conspiracy Theory That He Is A Government Clone |url=https://www.thefader.com/2016/06/21/gucci-mane-government-clone-conspiracy |website= The Fader |language=en |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190201013157/https://www.thefader.com/2016/06/21/gucci-mane-government-clone-conspiracy |archive-date=February 1, 2019 |access-date=January 31, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guan |first=Frank |date=2016-07-25 |title=The Definitive Proof That Gucci Mane Is Not a Clone |url=https://www.vulture.com/2016/07/definitive-proof-gucci-mane-is-not-a-clone.html |access-date=2024-11-10 |website= Vulture |language=en}}</ref> Davis discussed his newfound sobriety with ''[[The New York Times]]'' in 2016.<ref>{{cite news |last=Caramanica |first=Jon |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=20 July 2016 |title=Gucci Mane, Buff, Sober, Out of the Pen and Ready to Flow |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2016/07/24/arts/music/gucci-mane-everybody-looking-interview.html |access-date=12 August 2024}}</ref>

Davis married Keyshia Ka'oir, the founder of Ka'oir Cosmetics and Ka'oir Fitness, in [[Miami]] on October 17, 2017.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.41521/title.did-gucci-mane-keyshia-kaoir-already-get-married#| title=Gucci Mane & Keyshia Ka'oir Set Wedding Date |publisher=| website= HipHop DX |access-date=September 23, 2017|archive-date=September 24, 2017|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170924045252/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.41521/title.did-gucci-mane-keyshia-kaoir-already-get-married|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://people.com/music/gucci-mane-marries-keyshia-kaoir/|title=Gucci Mane Marries Keyshia Ka'oir in Lavish $1.7 Million Miami Ceremony |website= [[People (magazine)|People.com]] |access-date=December 11, 2019|archive-date=August 10, 2019|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190810000314/https://people.com/music/gucci-mane-marries-keyshia-kaoir/|url-status=live}}</ref> The wedding was paid for by [[BET]] which produced an accompanying 10-segment TV series, ''The Mane Event'', depicting wedding preparations and the ceremony.<ref>{{cite web|publisher=[[TMZ]]| title=Gucci & Keyshia The Man Event GUCCI...All on BET's Dime!!!|date=October 18, 2017|url=https://www.tmz.com/2017/10/18/gucci-mane-married-keyshia-all-white-wedding-the-mane-event/|access-date=November 11, 2018|archive-date=October 15, 2018|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181015235956/http://www.tmz.com/2017/10/18/gucci-mane-married-keyshia-all-white-wedding-the-mane-event/|url-status=live}}</ref> Davis had proposed to Ka'oir after performing at an [[Atlanta Hawks]] basketball game.<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.41386/title.gucci-mane-proposes-to-girlfriend-keyshia-kaoir-at-atlanta-hawks-game|title=Gucci Mane Proposes To Girlfriend Keyshia Ka'oir At Atlanta Hawks Game|publisher= | website= HipHop DX |access-date=September 23, 2017|archive-date=September 24, 2017|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170924045359/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.41386/title.gucci-mane-proposes-to-girlfriend-keyshia-kaoir-at-atlanta-hawks-game |url-status=live}}</ref> On December 23, 2020, Ka'oir gave birth to their son, Ice Davis.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Kaufman |first1=Gil |title=Gucci Mane and Wife Keyshia Ka'oir Welcome First Child Together: Ice Davis |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-wife-welcome-baby-ice-davis-9504529/ |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |date=December 24, 2020}}</ref> On February 8, 2023, Ka'oir gave birth to their daughter, Iceland Ka'oir Davis.{{citation needed|date=October 2025}}

Davis disclosed in his 2017 autobiography that he has a son born in 2007 whom he had not known about until the child was ten months old.<ref>{{Cite news| url= http://hiphop-n-more.com/2017/09/gucci-mane-reveals-he-has-a-son-in-autobiography/|title=Gucci Mane Reveals He Has a Son in Autobiography |date= September 23, 2017|publisher= |website= HipHop-N-More.com |access-date=September 23, 2017|archive-date=September 24, 2017|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170924092908/http://hiphop-n-more.com/2017/09/gucci-mane-reveals-he-has-a-son-in-autobiography/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Davis stated in his 2025 autobiography that he was diagnosed with [[bipolar disorder]] and [[schizophrenia]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Del Rosario |first= Alexandra |date=2025-10-21 |title=Gucci Mane has schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. Keyshia Ka'oir has been his rock through it all |url= https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/music/story/2025-10-21/gucci-mane-diagnosed-schizophrenia-bipolar-disorder-keyshia-kaoir |access-date=2025-10-22 |website=[[Los Angeles Times]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Legal issues==
===2001–2008===
In April 2001, Davis was arrested on [[cocaine]] charges and sentenced to 90 days in county [[jail]].<ref name="Murder charge">{{cite news|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1502738/20050523/gucci_mane.jhtml|title=Atlanta Rapper Gucci Mane Faces Murder Charge|last=Kaufman|first=Gil|date=May 23, 2005|publisher=MTV News|access-date=May 3, 2009|archive-date=March 15, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090315174400/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1502738/20050523/gucci_mane.jhtml|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On May 10, 2005, Davis was attacked by a group of men at a house in [[Decatur, Georgia]]. Davis and his companions shot at the group, killing one. The body of that attacker, Pookie Loc, was found later behind a nearby middle school. Davis turned himself in to police investigators on May 19, 2005, and was subsequently charged with murder. Davis claimed that the shots fired by him and his party were in self-defense.<ref name="Murder charge"/> The [[DeKalb County, Georgia|DeKalb County]] district attorney's office dropped the murder charge in January 2006 due to insufficient evidence. The previous October, in an unrelated matter, Davis had pleaded no contest to a charge of aggravated assault for assaulting a nightclub promoter the previous June; at the time the murder charge was dropped, he was serving a six-month county jail sentence for this.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1519599/20060103/gucci_mane.jhtml|title=Murder Charges Against Gucci Mane Dropped|last=Harris|first=Chris|date=January 3, 2006|publisher=MTV News|access-date=May 3, 2009|archive-date=May 18, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090518103246/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1519599/20060103/gucci_mane.jhtml|url-status=dead}}</ref> Davis was released from jail in late January 2006.<ref name="allmusic"/>

In September 2008, Davis was arrested for a probation violation for completing only 25 out of 600 community service hours following his 2005 arrest for aggravated assault. He was sentenced to a year in the county jail but was released after six months.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/online/?p=24484|title=Gucci Mane Sent To Jail For Violating Probation|date=September 15, 2008 |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |access-date=October 31, 2009|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090320115209/http://www.xxlmag.com/online/?p=24484|archive-date=March 20, 2009}}</ref> He was incarcerated in the [[Fulton County, Georgia|Fulton County]] jail for violating his probation<ref>{{cite web|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|title=Gucci Mane Says Warm Post-Prison Reception 'Strengthens' Him|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1639133/20100513/gucci_mane.jhtml|publisher=MTV News|date=May 13, 2010|access-date=May 16, 2010|archive-date=May 16, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100516140953/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1639133/20100513/gucci_mane.jhtml|url-status=dead}}</ref> and was released on May 12, 2010.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://rapradar.com/2010/05/06/gucci-mane-to-be-released-from-jail-may-12th/|title=Gucci Mane To Be Released From Jail May 12th|publisher= | website= RapRadar.com|date=May 6, 2010|access-date=May 6, 2010|archive-date=May 8, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100508161337/http://rapradar.com/2010/05/06/gucci-mane-to-be-released-from-jail-may-12th/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2010–2011===
On November 2, 2010, Gucci Mane was arrested for driving on the wrong side of the road, running a red light or stop sign, damage to government property, obstruction, driving without a license, no proof of insurance, and other traffic charges. He was taken to [[Grady Memorial Hospital]].<ref name= "allhiphop.com">{{cite web |first=Houston |last=Williams |url=http://allhiphop.com/stories/news/archive/2010/11/02/22466793.aspx |title=Breaking News: Gucci Mane Arrested |website=AllHipHop.com |date=November 2, 2010 |access-date=March 31, 2012 |archive-date=December 23, 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20101223111037/http://allhiphop.com/stories/news/archive/2010/11/02/22466793.aspx |url-status=live}}</ref>

On January 4, 2011, a judge in the Superior Court of Georgia's Fulton County ordered Davis to a psychiatric hospital, according to court documents. The documents reveal that his lawyers filed a Special Plea of Mental Incompetency on December 27, arguing that he was unable "to go forward and/or intelligently participate in the probation revocation hearing."<ref>{{cite news|url=http://music-mix.ew.com/2011/01/04/gucci-mane-court-hearing/| title= Gucci Mane sent to psychiatric facility by judge|newspaper= [[Atlanta Journal-Constitution]] |date=April 1, 2011|access-date=April 1, 2011 |archive-date= February 26, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110226234158/http://music-mix.ew.com/2011/01/04/gucci-mane-court-hearing/| url-status=live}}</ref>

Davis was arrested twice in April 2011 in DeKalb County, on April 13 on one charge of battery,<ref>{{cite web| url= http://georgia.arrests.org/Arrests/Radric_Davis_7071797/|title=April 8, 2011 Dekalb County|publisher= |website= georgia.arrests.org|date=April 8, 2011 |access-date= April 8, 2011|archive-date=March 5, 2016|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160305121304/http://georgia.arrests.org/Arrests/Radric_Davis_7071797/|url-status=dead}}</ref> and on April 20 on two charges of aggravated assault with a deadly weapon.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://georgia.arrests.org/Arrests/Radric_Davis_6466689/|title=April 20, 2011 Dekalb County| publisher= |website= georgia.arrests.org |date=April 20, 2011|access-date=April 20, 2011|archive-date=March 5, 2016|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160305162942/http://georgia.arrests.org/Arrests/Radric_Davis_6466689/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On September 13, 2011, Davis was given a six-month county jail sentence after pleading guilty to two counts of battery, two counts of reckless conduct and one count of disorderly conduct.<ref>{{cite news|title=Gucci Mane Sentenced To Six Months In Jail.|url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2011/09/13/gucci-mane-sentenced-to-jail_n_960263.html|work=The Huffington Post|first=Kelly|last=Fisher|date=September 13, 2011|access-date=November 21, 2011|archive-date=April 22, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120422215340/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2011/09/13/gucci-mane-sentenced-to-jail_n_960263.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He was released on December 11, 2011.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/gucci-mane-is-out-of-jail-before-expected-due-to-good-behavior-rep-464752/|title=Gucci Mane is Out of Jail Before Expected Due to 'Good Behavior;' Rep Tells The Juice|magazine=Billboard|access-date=December 11, 2019|archive-date=December 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191212002711/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/the-juice/464752/gucci-mane-is-out-of-jail-before-expected-due-to-good-behavior-rep|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2013–2016===
On March 22, 2013, the [[Atlanta Police Department]] issued a warrant for the arrest of Davis after he allegedly attacked a fan who was trying to take a photo with him. A soldier, "James," claimed that Gucci Mane hit him with a bottle in the head while he was talking to a security guard about getting a photo with the rapper. He was treated at [[Grady Memorial Hospital]] and received 10 stitches.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Gucci Mane Wanted By Atlanta Police for Flexing with deadly intentions|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/03/gucci-mane-wanted-by-atlanta-police-for-alleged-assault-on-soldier/|magazine= XXL Magazine |publisher= |access-date=March 23, 2013|first=Eric|last=Diep|date=March 22, 2013|archive-date=March 24, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130324092831/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/03/gucci-mane-wanted-by-atlanta-police-for-alleged-assault-on-soldier/|url-status=live}}</ref> Four days later, a second man going by the initials T.J. claimed Gucci Mane punched him in the face when T.J. tried to shake his hand after a concert performance at Club Onyx in [[Philadelphia]].<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Gucci Mane Faces Another Assault Claim| url= http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/03/gucci-mane-faces-another-assault-claim/|magazine= XXL Magazine |publisher= |access-date=March 26, 2013|archive-date= April 3, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130403070209/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/03/gucci-mane-faces-another-assault-claim/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the early morning of March 27, Davis turned himself in on the aggravated assault charges. He was denied bond and was incarcerated at [[Fulton County Jail]]. His lawyer said that witnesses claim Gucci Mane had nothing to do with the assault. He appeared in court on April 9, 2013, and was indicted on one count of aggravated assault.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Isenberg |first=Daniel |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/03/gucci-mane-denied-bond-after-turning-himself-in |title=Gucci Mane Denied Bond After Turning Himself In |magazine=Complex |date=March 27, 2013 |access-date=July 31, 2014 |archive-date=October 21, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021030306/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/03/gucci-mane-denied-bond-after-turning-himself-in |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Fitzgerald |first=Trent |title=Gucci Mane Indicted on Assault Charge in Club Incident |url=https://thedrop.fm/gucci-mane-indicted-on-assault-charge-in-club-incident/ |access-date=2023-03-27 |website=TheDrop.fm |date=April 10, 2013 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Gucci Mane -- Pleads NOT GUILTY to Assault Charges |url=https://www.tmz.com/2013/05/13/gucci-mane-pleads-not-guilty-in-atl-night-club-incident/ |access-date=2023-03-27 |website=TMZ |date=May 13, 2013}}</ref> Days later, he posted $75,000 bail, and the following day, April 14, he was arrested again for a parole violation.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/04/gucci-mane-got-arrested-for-violating-parole |title=Gucci Mane Got Arrested For Violating Parole |magazine=Complex |date=April 13, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=April 16, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130416014658/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/04/gucci-mane-got-arrested-for-violating-parole |url-status=live}}</ref> He was released three weeks later, on May 2, 2013.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/05/gucci-mane-freed-from-jail |title=Gucci Mane Freed From Jail |magazine=Complex |date=May 2, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=May 5, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130505115448/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/05/gucci-mane-freed-from-jail |url-status=live}}</ref>

On September 13, 2013, Gucci Mane was hanging out with a friend and was behaving "erratically." The friend decided to call the police to address the situation. When the police arrived, Gucci Mane began cursing and threatening them. Authorities took him into custody at 12:05&nbsp;a.m., and found marijuana and a handgun on him. He was booked on charges of carrying a concealed weapon, possession of marijuana, and disorderly conduct. He was reportedly hospitalized following the arrest.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-arrested-gun-possession |title=Gucci Mane Arrested Gun Possession |magazine=Complex |date=September 14, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=September 17, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130917002021/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/gucci-mane-arrested-gun-possession |url-status=live}}</ref> On September 30, 2013, it was revealed that Gucci Mane would serve 183 days in jail on charges of firearm possession by a convicted felon, disorderly conduct, carrying a concealed weapon, and marijuana possession, among others.<ref>{{cite web |last=Harling |first=Danielle |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.25607/title.gucci-mane-sentenced-to-183-days-in-jail-on-weapons-drugs-charges |title=Gucci Mane Sentenced To 183 Days In Jail On Weapons & Drugs Charges &#124; Get The Latest Hip Hop News, Rap News & Hip Hop Album Sales |publisher= | website= HipHop DX |date= September 30, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130930221856/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.25607/title.gucci-mane-sentenced-to-183-days-in-jail-on-weapons-drugs-charges |archive-date=September 30, 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

On December 3, 2013, Gucci Mane was charged in federal court with two counts of possessing a firearm as a felon. According to the federal prosecutor, Gucci Mane was in possession of two different loaded guns between September 12 and 14, 2013, and was facing up to 20 years in prison.<ref>{{cite web|title=Rapper Gucci Mane faces up to 20 years on federal gun charges, prosecutor says|date=December 3, 2013 |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2013/12/03/showbiz/gucci-mane-federal-weapons-charges|publisher=CNN|access-date=December 3, 2013|archive-date=December 6, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131206064215/http://edition.cnn.com/2013/12/03/showbiz/gucci-mane-federal-weapons-charges|url-status=live}}</ref>

On May 13, 2014, Gucci Mane pleaded guilty to possession of a firearm by a convicted felon. He agreed to a plea deal that would result in him being in prison until late 2016.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2014/05/gucci-mane-wont-getting-jail-late-2016/ |title=Gucci Mane Won't Be Getting Out Of Jail Until Late 2016 |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |date=May 13, 2014 |access-date=July 31, 2014 |archive-date=March 3, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303224605/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2014/05/gucci-mane-wont-getting-jail-late-2016/ |url-status=live}}</ref> According to the [[Federal Bureau of Prisons]], his release date would be September 20, 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bop.gov/mobile/find_inmate/byname.jsp#inmate_results|title=BOP: Federal Inmates By Name|website=www.bop.gov|access-date=April 21, 2016|archive-date=June 23, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160623184137/https://www.bop.gov/mobile/find_inmate/byname.jsp#inmate_results|url-status=live}}</ref> He served his sentence in the [[United States Penitentiary, Terre Haute|United States Penitentiary in Terre Haute]], Indiana.<ref name="boplocator">{{cite web |url= https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/ |title= Inmate locator |date= January 6, 2016 |website= bop.gov |publisher= Bureau of Prisons | location= US, No. 65556-019| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180531184722/https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/ |archive-date= 2018-05-31 |url-status= dead |access-date=}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nuvo.net/MusicBlog/archives/2014/12/08/write-to-gucci-mane-in-prison-in-terre-haute|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141213184904/http://www.nuvo.net/MusicBlog/archives/2014/12/08/write-to-gucci-mane-in-prison-in-terre-haute|url-status= dead| archive-date=December 13, 2014|title=Write to Gucci Mane in prison in Terre Haute|work= nuvo.net |access-date=June 14, 2015}}</ref> On May 26, 2016, Gucci Mane was released from prison ahead of his scheduled September date because he was only belatedly credited with the time he served while waiting for his court date.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2016/05/gucci-mane-released-from-prison |title=Finally Home: Gucci Mane Released From Prison |magazine=Complex |date=May 26, 2016 |access-date=May 26, 2016 |archive-date=May 26, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160526220231/http://www.complex.com/music/2016/05/gucci-mane-released-from-prison |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Feuds ==
===Young Jeezy===
In May 2005, Gucci Mane released his first single "[[Icy (Gucci Mane song)|Icy]]", which featured rapper Young Jeezy (now simply known as [[Jeezy]]), at the time an upcoming artist as well. Tensions grew when "Icy" was believed to be put on Young Jeezy's debut album ''[[Let's Get It: Thug Motivation 101]]'', although it never was. It was put on Gucci Mane's debut album ''Trap House''. Young Jeezy claimed he was never paid his royalties for the song.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/jeezy-and-gucci-feud-over-icy |title=Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane Feud Over "Icy" – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |date=October 15, 2012 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921061944/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/jeezy-and-gucci-feud-over-icy |url-status=live}}</ref> On May 9, 2005, Young Jeezy released the controversial song "Stay Strapped," placing a $10,000 bounty on Gucci Mane's chain. Gucci Mane responded by stating, "That nigga Young Jeezy, man. That nigga fake."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/bounty-on-gucci-chain |title=Young Jeezy Puts a Bounty on Gucci's Mane's Chain – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921062005/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/bounty-on-gucci-chain |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/round-one |title=Gucci Mane Responds With 'Round One' – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |date=October 15, 2012 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921061931/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/round-one |url-status=live}}</ref>

On May 19, 2005, tensions were soaring between Gucci Mane and Young Jeezy when four men set up Gucci Mane at a stripper's home and attempted to rob him. Gucci Mane grabbed his pistol and shot one of the assailants. The assailant, Pookie Loc, a CTE affiliate of Young Jeezy, was later found buried behind Columbia Middle School in [[Decatur, GA|Decatur, Georgia]]. An official warrant was put out for Gucci Mane's arrest. Days later he turned himself in to police after hearing about the warrant.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/charged-with-murder |title=Gucci Mane Charged With the Murder of a Young Jeezy Associate – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921061950/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/charged-with-murder |url-status=live}}</ref>

During a phone conversation, Gucci Mane stated, "I just want to let everyone know I'm not a murderer. I was upset. I was scared a little bit, but I had to do what I had to do. You gotta be a man about it. I'm not a bad person. I have remorse for everything that happened." Young Jeezy responded to Gucci Mane and the allegations of hiring Pookie Loc to rob Gucci Mane by saying that Gucci Mane was "trying to turn a bad situation into good publicity and sell his record."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/denies-involvement |title=Gucci Mane Says "I'm Not A Murderer," Young Jeezy Denies Involvement – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921061939/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/denies-involvement |url-status=live}}</ref>

On January 17, 2006, prosecutors [[Nolle prosequi|dropped]] the murder charge against Gucci Mane as he maintained he was acting out of self-defense.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/acquitted-of-murder |title=Gucci Mane Is Acquitted of Murder – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=December 30, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121230173044/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/acquitted-of-murder |url-status=live}}</ref> On June 15, 2006, after tensions had cooled, Gucci Mane came at Young Jeezy on the song "745" rapping, "Do I smell pussy? Nah, that's Jeezy. You ain't a snowman, you more like a snowflake, cupcake, corn flake. Nigga, you too fake."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/snowflake-cupcake |title=Gucci Mane Lashes Out at Jeezy Again on "745" – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921063527/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/snowflake-cupcake |url-status=live}}</ref>

On June 15, 2009, Young Jeezy came at Gucci Mane on the song "24, 23," rapping, "I'm on some Louie shit today, fuck some Gucci, man. These niggas still on my dick, they like some groupies, man. Can't keep they lips closed, they worse than '[[Coochee|Coochie]]s Mane'."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/24-23 |title=Young Jeezy Takes Aim at Gucci Again on "24, 23" – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921062019/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/24-23 |url-status=live}}</ref> In light of the renewed tensions with the [[Crips|Crip]]-affiliated Young Jeezy, Gucci Mane started associating himself with the [[Bloods]] street gang, known rivals of the Crips, with Gucci Mane going on to use Blood imagery in his music videos as well as associating himself with known Blood figures in hip-hop, such as [[The Game (rapper)|The Game]].<ref>{{cite web |url= https://theurbandaily.cassiuslife.com/445327/the-game-says-he-turned-gucci-mane-lil-wayne-and-jim-jones-into-bloods/ |title= The Game Claims To Have Turned Gucci Mane & Others Into Bloods |website= theurbandaily.cassiuslife.com |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181219051322/https://theurbandaily.cassiuslife.com/445327/the-game-says-he-turned-gucci-mane-lil-wayne-and-jim-jones-into-bloods/ |archive-date= December 19, 2018 |url-status= dead |access-date=}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/15624-waka-flocka-says-gucci-mane-lost-his-noodles-discusses-his-relationship-with-gucci-news |title= Waka Flocka Dismisses Gucci Mane's Blood Ties |website= hotnewhiphop.com |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181219044514/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/waka-flocka-says-gucci-mane-lost-his-noodles-discusses-his-relationship-with-gucci-news.7259.html?_amp |archive-date= December 19, 2018 |url-status= live |access-date=}}</ref> On December 4, 2009, during a radio interview with [[DJ Drama]] and Jeezy, Gucci Mane called in to finally announce a truce between the two, saying, "I'd just like to say, it's way bigger than all of us. I feel like the way the city's been supporting all three of us, they deserve this. It's about that time, man. We're getting older, growing, so let's do it for the city."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/truce-with-drama |title=Young Jeezy Calls a Truce With Gucci and Drama – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921062012/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/truce-with-drama |url-status=live}}</ref>

On March 24, 2010, members of Gucci Mane's crew [[1017 Brick Squad Records|1017 Brick Squad]] and members of Jeezy's crew [[Corporate Thugz]] got into an altercation outside an Atlanta clothing store. Neither Gucci Mane nor Jeezy were present during the altercation.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/entourages-brawl |title=Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Entourages Brawl – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921062016/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/entourages-brawl |url-status=live}}</ref> On December 2, 2011, Jeezy explained how the beef originated, stating that Gucci Mane got mad at him because he would not perform "Icy" together; Jeezy said he could not perform the song because he was going through major throat surgery.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/beef-was-misunderstanding |title=Young Jeezy Claims the Entire Beef Was a Misunderstanding – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921063532/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/beef-was-misunderstanding |url-status=live}}</ref>

On October 10, 2012, during a radio interview, Gucci Mane announced he had no more love for Jeezy due to his controversial fight with rapper Rick Ross. During a radio interview, Jeezy responded by stating, "Man everybody knows that boy is retarded. Ain't nobody taking him seriously. He has an ice cream cone on his face, let's be for real. Given that said, I'm not going back, I'm going forward."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/no-respect |title=Gucci Mane Says He Has 'No Respect' for Jeezy – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=December 27, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121227083639/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/no-respect |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/retarded-interview |title=Young Jeezy Calls Gucci Mane "Retarded" – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=October 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121019082642/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/retarded-interview |url-status=live}}</ref> On October 15, 2012, Gucci Mane came at Jeezy and other artists on the song "Truth" by rapping, "A ten thousand dollar bounty put on my neck. I hope you didn't pay them cause they didn't have no success" and, "Go dig your partner up, nigga, bet he can't say shit," referring to Pookie Loc.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/responds-with-truth |title=Gucci Mane Responds With Diss Track "Truth" – A History of Young Jeezy and Gucci Mane's Beef |magazine=Complex |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921063529/http://www.complex.com/music/2012/10/a-history-of-young-jeezy-and-gucci-manes-beef/responds-with-truth |url-status=live}}</ref>

Over 15 years after their feud had begun, Jeezy and Gucci Mane both appeared in a [[Verzuz|Verzuz Battle]] on November 19, 2020. Despite a tense atmosphere, including Gucci Mane dissing Pookie Loc after performing "Truth",<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gucci Mane Says He Regrets Pookie Loc Jab During 'Verzuz'... |url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/cmplxjoshua-espinoza/gucci-mane-says-he-regrets-pookie-loc-comments-jeezy-verzuz |access-date=2024-11-15 |website=Complex |language=en}}</ref> the battle ended with the two performing "Icy" together for the first time since 2005, followed Gucci Mane telling Jeezy "it's all love" and that he "respected him" for proposing the idea.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Vera|first=Amir|date=November 20, 2020|title=Rappers Gucci Mane and Jeezy Verzuz battle ends peacefully, despite some jabs, drawing at least 1.8M people|url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/11/19/entertainment/gucci-mane-jeezy-verzuz-trnd/index.html|access-date=December 8, 2020|website=CNN}}</ref>

Gucci Mane would name-drop Pookie Loc again in 2022, rapping, "D.A. dropped my murder, didn’t have evidence to prove it / I think my house is haunted, by who? The ghost of Pookie" on his song "[[Rumors (Gucci Mane song)|Rumors]]" featuring [[Lil Durk]].<ref>{{cite journal |title=Yeah, Yeah, Oh-Yeah, Just Dropped in to See What Condition My Condition Was In |date=2012-12-06 |work=Bisexuality and Transgenderism |pages=71–115 |url= http://dx.doi.org/10.4324/9780203728444-6 |access-date=2024-11-15 |publisher=Routledge |doi=10.4324/9780203728444-6 |isbn=978-0-203-72844-4|url-access= subscription}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Allah |first=Sha Be |date=2022-01-26 |title=Gucci Mane Namedrops Jeezy's Dead Friend Pookie on 'Rumors' |url= https://thesource.com/2022/01/26/watch-gucci-mane-namedrops-jeezys-dead-friend-pookie-on-rumors/ |website= [[The Source (magazine)|The Source]] |publisher= |language=en-US |access-date=2024-11-15}}</ref>

===Waka Flocka Flame===
On March 15, 2013, Gucci Mane announced that frequent collaborator and close friend Waka Flocka Flame was "dropped" from 1017 Brick Squad Records. The two rappers proceeded to throw insults back and forth on [[Twitter]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rap-up.com/2013/03/15/gucci-mane-drops-waka-flocka-flame-from-brick-squad/ |title=Gucci Mane Drops Waka Flocka Flame From Brick Squad |publisher= |website= Rap-Up.com |date=March 15, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=May 30, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130530160757/http://www.rap-up.com/2013/03/15/gucci-mane-drops-waka-flocka-flame-from-brick-squad/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://rapfix.mtv.com/?p=75166 |title=Gucci Mane Says Twitter Was Hacked, Waka Flocka Isn't Buying It |publisher= |website= MTV.com |date=March 18, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=June 13, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130613223938/http://rapfix.mtv.com/?p=75166 |url-status=dead}}</ref> On March 27, 2013, during an ''[[MTV Jams]]'' interview with [[Sway Calloway|Sway]], Waka Flocka Flame explained that he would never do music nor business with Gucci Mane ever again. Neither of the rappers have explained where the controversy originated from. Waka Flocka Flame has stated, "I guess we both be at the finish line we just going our own routes. That's all I can say. What's the reason? Sometimes it's none of your fuckin' business what's the reason. Just understand two men went they own ways but it's no problem."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1704479/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-music-fallout.jhtml |title=Gucci Mane And Waka Flocka Will 'Never' Do Music Again |publisher= |website= MTV.com |date=March 27, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |archive-date=June 4, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130604041054/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1704479/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-music-fallout.jhtml |url-status= dead}}</ref>

On November 19, 2013, it was revealed that Gucci Mane had filed a lawsuit against Waka Flocka Flame, Waka Flocka Flame's mother Debra Antney, OJ da Juiceman, rapper Khia Stone and producer Zaytoven. The lawsuit accuses the parties of fraud, racketeering, and breach of contract. According to Gucci Mane, Antney took control of 1017 Brick Squad Records, LLC., without permission, and used it to create three separate offshoot labels. "Gucci is also accusing the parties in the lawsuit of withholding royalties and inflating the cost of label expenditures" and claimed that Antney seized his assets and stole a ring and a necklace.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/11/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-mom-lawsuit |title=Gucci Mane, Waka Flocka Flame Mom Lawsuit |magazine=Complex |date=November 19, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-date=November 30, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131130030354/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/11/gucci-mane-waka-flocka-flame-mom-lawsuit |url-status=live}}</ref> In his lawsuit, Gucci Mane also says that Antney took more than the typical 20% management fee. Gucci Mane also alleged that Antney's actions led to his having money and tax problems.<ref>{{cite web |last=Ju |first=Shirley |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.26338/title.waka-flocka-flame-s-mother-debra-antney-says-drugs-greed-women-ruined-gucci-mane |title=Waka Flocka Flame's Mother Debra Antney Says Drugs, Greed & Women Ruined Gucci Mane |publisher=| website= HipHop DX |date=November 20, 2013 |access-date=December 13, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131124211635/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.26338/title.waka-flocka-flame-s-mother-debra-antney-says-drugs-greed-women-ruined-gucci-mane |archive-date=November 24, 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

In early 2017, issues between the two resurfaced when Waka Flocka Flame released the song "Was My Dawg", which many believed referred to Gucci Mane.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/waka-flocka-flame-was-my-dawg-7685929/|title=Waka Flocka Flame Fires Off Subliminals on Alleged Gucci Mane Diss 'Was My Dawg': Listen|magazine=Billboard|access-date=January 8, 2018|archive-date=November 19, 2017|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20171119045207/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/7685929/waka-flocka-flame-was-my-dawg|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Others===
In 2012, Gucci Mane dissed fellow Atlanta rapper [[Yung Joc|Young Joc]] in a song from his [[Trap God]] mixtape, rapping, "I got all eyes on me like Pac did, but I ain't tryin' to go broke like Joc did." During a radio conversation, Joc reacted to the song by stating "Gucci is a master at getting his name in other people's mouths, I ain't trippin', I know what my financial situation is."<ref>{{cite web |date=October 22, 2012 |title=Yung Joc Responds to Gucci Mane Diss |url=https://www.bet.com/article/b2cb89/yung-joc-responds-to-gucci-mane-diss |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150107222214/http://www.bet.com/news/music/2012/10/22/yung-joc-responds-to-gucci-mane-diss.html |archive-date=January 7, 2015 |access-date=March 11, 2015 |work=BET.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web  |date=2012-10-19 |title=Gucci Mane Disses Young Jeezy, Yung Joc In New Songs |url= https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.21590/title.gucci-mane-disses-young-jeezy-yung-joc-in-new-songs |website=HipHopDX |language=en |access-date=2024-11-15}}</ref>

On March 16, 2012, [[R&B]] singer [[Keyshia Cole]] responded to Gucci Mane's mention of her on his Jeezy diss song "Truth", during which he had rapped, "I did a song with Keyshia Cole and I know you still miss her. But Puff was fucking her while you was falling in love with her," implying that, during the time of her rumored relationship with Young Jeezy, Cole was cheating on him with her "[[Last Night (Diddy song)|Last Night]]" collaborator [[Diddy (entertainer)|Diddy]]{{efn|Also known as Puff, Puff Daddy or Puffy.}}. Cole stated on the radio, "How you gonna be a [[Gangster|G]], and you spreading lies, to sell mixtapes! Where your talent at. Dude ain't have to lie though! Could've kept me out of it."<ref>{{cite web |last=Loren |first=Arielle |title= Keyshia Cole: Gucci Mane Lied About My Love Life |url=https://www.bet.com/article/xsduow/keyshia-cole-gucci-mane-lied-about-my-love-life |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130607054433/http://www.bet.com/news/music/2012/10/16/keyshia-cole-gucci-mane-lied-about-my-love-life.html |archive-date=June 7, 2013 |access-date=May 26, 2013 |publisher=BET}}</ref>

In September 2013, Gucci Mane sent a series of derogatory tweets to various figures in the hip-hop industry. In the tweets he claimed to have had sexual relations with rappers' girlfriends.<ref>{{Cite web |first= Emmanuel |last= C. M. |date=2013-09-09 |title=A Timeline Of Gucci Mane's Twitter Rant| url= https://www.xxlmag.com/timeline-gucci-manes-twitter-rant/ |work= XXL Magazine |publisher= |language=en |access-date=2024-11-15}}</ref> A couple of days later, Gucci Mane stated that his Twitter account had been hacked.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/14365-gucci-mane-continues-twitter-war-puts-nicki-minaj-drake-and-more-on-blast-news|title=Gucci Mane Continues Twitter War, Puts Nicki Minaj, Drake & More On Blast [Update: Nicki, T.I. & Tyga Respond] |first=Trevor|last=Smith|work= HotNewHipHop.com |date=September 9, 2013|access-date=March 9, 2015|archive-date=March 28, 2015|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150328212644/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/gucci-mane-continues-twitter-war-puts-nicki-minaj-drake-and-more-on-blast-news.7173.html |url-status=live}}</ref> However, he later admitted in his autobiography that he did author the tweets.<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Watch Gucci Mane Discuss His Toxic Past, Protege Young Thug and More in XXL Cover Story Interview |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2016/10/gucci-mane-xxl-cover-story-interview-video/ |magazine= XXL Magazine |publisher= |language=en |archive-date=March 21, 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190321044951/https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2016/10/gucci-mane-xxl-cover-story-interview-video/ |url-status=live |access-date=March 21, 2019}}</ref>

In a 2016 interview with the hip-hop radio program ''[[The Breakfast Club (radio show)|The Breakfast Club]]'', Gucci Mane alleged that host [[Angela Yee]] had attempted to sleep with him, saying "she done tried ... she was on my dick back then", which Yee strongly denied. Four years later, in October 2019, Gucci Mane claimed that he was banned from ''The Breakfast Club'' over these comments, which the hosts discussed and denied on-air. In response, Gucci Mane called Angela Yee a "punk ass bitch" and threatened to "step to" and [[Slapping (strike)|slap]] host [[DJ Envy]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gucci Mane's Beef With 'The Breakfast Club,' Explained |url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/j-mckinney/gucci-mane-breakfast-club-beef-explained |access-date=2024-11-14 |website=Complex |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Saponara |first=Michael |date=2019-10-21 |title=Gucci Mane Blasts DJ Envy & Angela Yee Over Rumored 'The Breakfast Club' Ban, Envy Responds |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-dj-envy-angela-yee-breakfast-club-ban-8533679/ |access-date=2024-11-14 |magazine=Billboard |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Gucci Mane Slams DJ Envy in Response to Claims He's Banned from The Breakfast Club |url=https://people.com/music/gucci-mane-slams-dj-envy-angela-yee-addresses-claim-banned-from-breakfast-club/ |access-date=2024-11-14 |website=People.com |language=en}}</ref>

In November 2024, Gucci Mane's ex-girlfriend and former label signee, Mac Bre-Z, went public against the rapper, accusing him of domestic violence, verbal abuse, and unpaid writing credits during the time of their involvement.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-07 |title=Gucci Mane's Former Artist Accuses Him Of Domestic Violence And Verbal Abuse |url=https://www.yahoo.com/entertainment/gucci-mane-former-artist-accuses-213917711.html |access-date=2024-11-14 |website= Yahoo Entertainment |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Gucci Mane Ex-Girlfriend Threatens to 'Flatline' Him Amid... |url= https://www.complex.com/music/a/markelibert/gucci-mane-ex-girlfriend-flatline-domestic-violence-unpaid-wages |access-date=2024-11-14 |website=Complex |language= en}}</ref>

