'''Terrence LeVarr Thornton''' (born May 13, 1977),<ref>{{cite tweet |user=PUSHA_T |number=466584650714865664 |date=May 14, 2014 |title="@HotNewHipHop: Wish @PUSHA_T a happy birthday, y'all." *5/13 is my bday...mark your calendars for next year but thanx...* }}</ref> better known by his stage name '''Pusha T''',<ref>{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-details-new-kanye-produced-album-daytona/|title=Pusha-T Details New Kanye-Produced Album DAYTONA|work=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]|last1=Wicks|first1=Amanda|last2=Strauss|first2=Matthew|date=May 23, 2018|access-date=May 24, 2018|archive-date=May 24, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180524001514/https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-details-new-kanye-produced-album-daytona/|url-status=live}} A representative for Pusha-T confirmed to Pitchfork that the hyphen has, indeed, returned.</ref> is an American rapper. He rose to prominence as one half of the Virginia-based hip hop duo [[Clipse]], which he formed with his older brother [[No Malice|Malice]]. Established in 1994, the duo were discovered by [[Pharrell Williams]] and signed with his record label [[Star Trak Entertainment]], an imprint of [[Arista Records]], in 2001. They initially released three studio albums—''[[Lord Willin']]'' (2002), ''[[Hell Hath No Fury (Clipse album)|Hell Hath No Fury]]'' (2006) and ''[[Til the Casket Drops (Clipse album)|Til the Casket Drops]]'' (2009)—to mild commercial success; the former spawned the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]]-top 40 singles "[[Grindin']]" and "[[When the Last Time]]", for which they became best known.

After the duo disbanded in 2010, Thornton began pursuing a solo career. He signed with [[Kanye West]]'s record label [[GOOD Music]], an imprint of [[Def Jam Recordings]] following his guest appearance on West's 2010 single "[[Runaway (Kanye West song)|Runaway]]", which peaked at number 12 on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100. In 2011, he released two solo projects—''Fear of God'' and ''[[Fear of God II: Let Us Pray]]''—before releasing his debut studio album, ''[[My Name Is My Name]]'' (2013). A critical and commercial success, it peaked at number 4 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] and was followed by his second and third albums ''[[King Push – Darkest Before Dawn: The Prelude]]'' (2015) and ''[[Daytona (album)|Daytona]]'' (2018), which peaked at numbers 20 and three on the chart, respectively. His fourth album, ''[[It's Almost Dry]]'' (2022), became his first to debut atop the ''Billboard'' 200.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ebony.com/pusha-t-on-generational-wealth-and-how-the-dmv-culture-influenced-his-next-album/|title=Pusha T on Generational Wealth and How The DMV Culture Influenced His Next Album|website=ebony.com|access-date=February 21, 2024|archive-date=February 21, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240221041601/https://www.ebony.com/pusha-t-on-generational-wealth-and-how-the-dmv-culture-influenced-his-next-album/|url-status=live}}</ref> Clipse briefly reunited to self-release their fourth album, ''[[Let God Sort Em Out]]'' (2025), which peaked at number 4 on the ''Billboard'' 200.

West appointed Thornton as president of GOOD Music in 2015, where he remained one of the label's flagship artists until his departure in 2022. Three years later, he parted ways with his remaining label, Def Jam Recordings, due to creative disagreements.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-says-dispute-over-kendrick-lamar-verse-led-to-expensive-def-jam-exit/|title=Pusha T Says Dispute Over Kendrick Lamar Verse Led to Expensive Def Jam Exit}}></ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Caulfield |first=Keith |date=2022-05-01 |title=Pusha T Scores First No. 1 Album on Billboard 200 Chart With 'It's Almost Dry' |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/pusha-t-its-almost-dry-billboard-200-chart-debut-1235065350/ |access-date=2022-07-03 |magazine=Billboard |language=en-US |archive-date=May 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220508051611/https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/pusha-t-its-almost-dry-billboard-200-chart-debut-1235065350/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2020, he launched his record label, Heir Wave Music Group, as a platform for Virginia-based artists.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wtkr.com/news/only-on-3-pusha-t-talks-new-record-label-and-giving-back-to-the-757|title=Pusha T talks New Record Label and Giving Back to the 757|website=WTKR.com|date=August 28, 2020|access-date=October 11, 2023|archive-date=October 13, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231013170323/https://www.wtkr.com/news/only-on-3-pusha-t-talks-new-record-label-and-giving-back-to-the-757|url-status=live}}</ref> Thornton has won a [[Grammy Award]] from 11 nominations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pusha T {{!}} Artist {{!}} GRAMMY.com |url=https://grammy.com/artists/terrence-thornton/5687 |access-date=2026-03-09 |website=grammy.com}}</ref>

== Early life ==
Pusha T was born Terrence LeVarr Thornton on May 13, 1977, in [[the Bronx]] borough of [[New York City]] to Gene and Mildred Thornton, though the family soon relocated to [[Virginia Beach, Virginia]], where he and his brother, [[Malice (rapper)|Gene Thornton Jr.]], grew up.<ref name="vv">{{cite web |last=Mlynar |first=Phillip |url=http://blogs.villagevoice.com/music/2010/05/pusha_t_present.php |title=Pusha T Presents: A Clipse Guide to New York City - New York - Music - Sound of the City |publisher=Blogs.villagevoice.com |date=May 5, 2010 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=January 4, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150104051821/http://blogs.villagevoice.com/music/2010/05/pusha_t_present.php |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="pitchfork">{{cite web |url=https://pitchfork.com/features/5-10-15-20/8449-5-10-15-20-pusha-t/ |title=5-10-15-20: Pusha-T &#124; Features |publisher=Pitchfork |date=July 14, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=November 25, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121125073045/http://pitchfork.com/features/5-10-15-20/8449-5-10-15-20-pusha-t/ |url-status=live }}</ref> As teenagers, the brothers both sold drugs, with Gene eventually being kicked out of their parents' house after they discovered what he was doing.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://hiphopwired.com/258482/12-rappers-who-actually-sold-drugs-for-real-photos/|title=12 Rappers Who Actually Sold Drugs, For Real [PHOTOS]|website=Hiphopwired.com|date=October 2, 2013|access-date=July 5, 2018|archive-date=July 5, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180705233634/https://hiphopwired.com/258482/12-rappers-who-actually-sold-drugs-for-real-photos/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.25800/title.pusha-t-details-when-his-parents-found-out-he-sold-drugs|title=Pusha T Details When His Parents Found Out He Sold Drugs|date=October 14, 2013|website=HipHopDX.com|access-date=April 14, 2020|archive-date=June 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200608050636/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.25800/title.pusha-t-details-when-his-parents-found-out-he-sold-drugs|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Career ==
===1993–2010: Clipse===
{{Main|Clipse}}
[[File:Pusha T in Cambridge 3.jpg|thumb|left|Pusha T performing at [[The Middle East (nightclub)|The Middle East]] in 2007 as part of [[Clipse]]]]
In 1992, alongside his brother, Thornton began to pursue a career in [[hip hop music|hip hop]], forming a group known as [[Clipse]]. His older brother Gene was known for most of the group's duration as Malice (the two were originally known as '''Terrar''' and Malicious, respectively).

Shortly after forming Clipse, the brothers were introduced to record producer and fellow Virginian [[Pharrell Williams]] of [[The Neptunes]], who helped them secure a recording contract with [[Elektra Records]] in 1997.<ref name="pitchfork1">{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/features/interviews/6551-clipse/|title=Interviews: Clipse|last=Maher|first=Dave|date=February 26, 2007|website=Pitchfork.com|access-date=July 30, 2013|archive-date=March 5, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305091555/http://pitchfork.com/features/interviews/6551-clipse/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Terrence initially took the stage name '''Terrar'''. After being signed to Elektra, they began working on their debut album, ''[[Exclusive Audio Footage]]''. After recording the entire album, they released one single, "The Funeral", which was commercially unsuccessful and eventually led to the release of the album being cancelled and Clipse being dropped from the label shortly after. After being dropped, Terrar changed his name to '''Pusha T''', and despite not being signed to a major label, he made numerous appearances on other artists' songs, appearing on [[Kelis]]' 1999 single "[[Good Stuff (Kelis song)|Good Stuff]]" and [[Nivea (singer)|Nivea]]'s 2001 single "[[Run Away (I Wanna Be with U)]]", respectively, thanks to his relationship with The Neptunes, who produced both songs.

In early 2001, Pharrell Williams signed the duo to [[Arista Records]] through his recently established [[Star Trak Entertainment|Star Trak]] imprint. Clipse began working on their major label debut, executively produced by The Neptunes. Their debut single, ''[[Grindin']]'' was released on May 14, 2002, and became a summer Top 40 hit, reaching number 30 on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] on July 30, 2002. Similar success followed with their second single, ''[[When the Last Time]]'', which peaked at No. 19. Fueled by two successful singles, Clipse released their commercial debut album ''[[Lord Willin']]'' on August 20, 2002, debuting at No. 1 on Billboard's Top R&B/Hip-hop Album chart and No. 4 on the [[Billboard 200]], eventually being certified gold by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] (RIAA) on October 1, 2002.

In late 2003, Clipse began recording material for their second album, ''[[Hell Hath No Fury (Clipse album)|Hell Hath No Fury]]''.<ref name="pitchfork1"/> However, further work on the album ground to a halt in 2004, when [[Arista Records]]'s urban artists were absorbed into its sister label [[Jive Records]] as part of a larger merger between [[Sony Music Entertainment]] and [[Bertelsmann Music Group|BMG]].<ref name="pitchfork1"/> Due to contractual requirements, Clipse was forced to stay on Jive, while Star Trak and the rest of its roster moved to their new home at [[Interscope Records]].<ref name="pitchfork1"/> While Clipse resumed work on the album, and eventually finished recording it, the duo became increasingly frustrated with Jive, as the label was notorious for overlooking various hip-hop/R&B artists in favor of more pop-oriented acts on its roster. The label's musical strategy expansion caused numerous delays in the release of ''Hell Hath No Fury''.<ref name="pitchfork1"/> As delays continued, the group asked for a formal release from its contract. When Jive refused to grant this request, the duo sued the label.<ref name="pitchfork1"/> In response to this, in 2004, the brothers launched their own record label imprint, Re-Up Records, and formed the hip hop group [[Re-Up Gang]], along with fellow rappers Ab-Liva and Sandman.

On May 9, 2006, Clipse finally reached an agreement with Jive to release the album through both Re-Up and Jive.<ref name="Reference 2">{{cite web|url=http://allhiphop.com/hiphopnews/?ID=5650|title=Clipse Tour With Ice Cube; Album Release Date Set|format=Online news article|work=AllHipHop.com|access-date=June 11, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060521025035/http://www.allhiphop.com/hiphopnews/?ID=5650 <!-- Bot retrieved archive -->|archive-date=May 21, 2006}}</ref> ''Hell Hath No Fury'' was finally released on November 28, 2006. Once again produced by [[The Neptunes]], it debuted at No. 14 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] with over 78,000 copies sold in the 1st week. While the album received a great deal of critical acclaim, its sales were less successful than Lord Willin'.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/bloggers/2006/12/clipse-catch-a-brick/ |title=Clipse catch a brick |date=December 6, 2006 |publisher=Xxlmag.Com |access-date=February 21, 2012 |archive-date=October 10, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121010155849/http://www.xxlmag.com/bloggers/2006/12/clipse-catch-a-brick/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="pitchfork1"/> It spawned two moderately successful singles: "[[Mr. Me Too]]" with [[Pharrell Williams]] and "[[Wamp Wamp (What It Do)]]" with [[Slim Thug]].<ref name="pitchfork1"/> Clipse's frustrations with Jive were a constant theme of the album, with Pusha T saying ''"I'm sorry to the fans, but them crackers weren't playing fair at Jive"'' on the track "Mr. Me Too:.<ref name="pitchfork1"/> The [[hip hop music|hip hop]] magazine ''[[XXL magazine|XXL]]'' gave the album a "XXL" rating, marking it as a five-star album. At the time only five albums had previously received that honor.<ref name="pitchfork1"/>  [[File:Pusha T.jpg|thumb|upright|Pusha T performing at the 2007 [[Pitchfork Music Festival|''Pitchfork'' Music Festival]]]]

In a May 19, 2007, interview with ''[[Eye Weekly]]'', Clipse revealed that they had been officially released from any contractual obligations with Jive.<ref name="Reference 3">{{cite web|url=http://www.eyeweekly.com/eye/issue/issue_05.17.07/music/feature.php|title=Keys Open Doors|format=Print interview|work=Eye Weekly|access-date=May 22, 2007|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927000821/http://www.eyeweekly.com/eye/issue/issue_05.17.07/music/feature.php|archive-date=September 27, 2007}}</ref> After this, the duo began discussions with several record labels, eventually signing with [[Columbia Records]] on October 26, 2007.<ref name="Reference 5">{{cite web|url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/1047652/exclusive-the-clipse-signs-with-columbia|title=The Clipse Signs With Columbia|format=Online news article|work=Billboard|access-date=October 26, 2007|archive-date=May 28, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130528050026/http://www.billboard.com/articles/news/1047652/exclusive-the-clipse-signs-with-columbia|url-status=live}}</ref> The follow-up to ''Hell Hath No Fury'', titled ''[[Til the Casket Drops (Clipse album)|Til the Casket Drops]]'', was released on December 8, 2009, via Columbia Records.<ref name="Release Date">{{cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/online/?p=52393|title=CLIPSE to drop casket in October|work=xxlmag.com|access-date=July 24, 2009|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090723092557/http://www.xxlmag.com/online/?p=52393|archive-date=July 23, 2009}}</ref> In a departure from the group's previous works, which only featured production from the Neptunes, the album features production from [[Sean "Diddy" Combs]]' production team [[The Hitmen (production team)|The Hitmen]], and [[DJ Khalil]] among others. The album peaked at No. 41 on the ''Billboard'' 200.<ref name="pitchfork1"/>

===2010–2011: Solo career beginnings===
After releasing their third album, Pusha T and Malice announced Clipse would be going on indefinite hiatus so the brothers could focus on their solo careers. Shortly after this, Pusha T was signed to [[Kanye West]]'s [[GOOD Music]] label in September 2010, and made his first appearance as a member of the GOOD roster on Kanye West's ''[[My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy]]'', appearing on his hit "[[Runaway (Kanye West song)|Runaway]]" and premiering the song with him at the [[2010 MTV Video Music Awards]] on September 12, 2010. He was also featured on several tracks from West's [[GOOD Fridays]] series and made other appearances on [[Swizz Beatz]]'s Monster Mondays series, [[Lloyd Banks]]' ''[[H.F.M. 2 (Hunger for More 2)]]'' and [[Tabi Bonney]]'s ''Fresh'', respectively. On the week of December 16, 2010, Pusha T signed an exclusive management deal with [[NUE Agency]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://encore.celebrityaccess.com/?encoreId=281&articleId=37618 |title=ENCORE: The Standard in Live Entertainment News |publisher=Encore.celebrityaccess.com |date=December 16, 2010 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=March 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304115441/http://encore.celebrityaccess.com/?encoreId=281&articleId=37618 |url-status=live }}</ref> On February 11, 2011, [[Funkmaster Flex]] debuted Pusha T's debut solo single on [[New York City]]'s [[WQHT|Hot 97]].<ref>[http://nahright.com/news/2011/02/12/pusha-t-my-god-prod-by-hit-boy-no-tags/ Pusha T – My God (Prod. by Hit-Boy) (No Tags)] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151118015434/http://nahright.com/news/2011/02/12/pusha-t-my-god-prod-by-hit-boy-no-tags/ |date=November 18, 2015 }} Nah Right</ref> The track, titled "My God", was produced by [[Hit-Boy]] and was generally well received by the public. Soon after, the song leaked onto the internet, however was not officially released through iTunes and Amazon until August 24, 2011. On August 31, 2011, it was announced Pusha had signed a solo record deal with [[Def Jam]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://rapdose.com/2011/08/31/pusha-t-signs-solo-deal-with-def-jam |title=Pusha T Signs Solo Deal With Def Jam |publisher=Rap Dose |date=August 31, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=May 29, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160529032053/http://rapdose.com/2011/08/31/pusha-t-signs-solo-deal-with-def-jam/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On March 21, 2011, Pusha T released his solo debut, a [[mixtape]] titled ''Fear of God'', which included "My God" and featured both [[Freestyle rap|freestyles]] and original songs, and included features from [[Kanye West]], [[50 Cent]], [[Rick Ross (rapper)|Rick Ross]], [[Pharrell Williams]] and [[Kevin Cossom]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/mixtape/id.1386/title.pusha-t-fear-of-god|title=Pusha T – Fear of God mixtape|publisher=HipHopDX|date=March 21, 2011|access-date=May 29, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110530125341/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/mixtape/id.1386/title.pusha-t-fear-of-god|archive-date=May 30, 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rapbasement.com/clipse/091310-pusha-t-of-the-rapping-group-the-clipse-confirms-signing-with-kanye-wests-good-music.html |title=Pusha T Signs With Kanye's GOOD Music |publisher=Rap Basement |date=September 13, 2010 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=August 20, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130820054743/http://www.rapbasement.com/clipse/091310-pusha-t-of-the-rapping-group-the-clipse-confirms-signing-with-kanye-wests-good-music.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://rapradar.com/2011/03/21/new-mixtape-pusha-t-fear-of-god/ |title=New Mixtape: Pusha T Fear Of God |publisher=Rap Radar |date=March 21, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=August 6, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806224957/http://rapradar.com/2011/03/21/new-mixtape-pusha-t-fear-of-god/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The mixtape was well received, and soon after he began working on his debut [[extended play]] (EP) ''[[Fear of God II: Let Us Pray]]'', which initially included four songs from the mixtape and five new songs. The EP was originally set to be released on June 21, 2011;<ref name="mtv">{{cite web |last=Blanco |first=Alvin |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1662245/pusha-t-fear-of-god-2-june.jhtml |title=Pusha T Set To Drop Fear of God 2 In June News |publisher=MTV.com |date=April 19, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=April 24, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140424031912/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1662245/pusha-t-fear-of-god-2-june.jhtml |url-status=dead }}</ref> however, it was pushed back to August 23, and for a while September 27 was believed to be the date, but after it came and went, on October 6, 2011, Pusha T announced the EP was set to be released on November 8, 2011. In addition, it was revealed he had added four more songs to the EP, bringing it to 12 tracks in total.<ref>{{cite web |title=Pusha T Announces November Release For Solo Debut, 'Fear of God II' |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2011/10/06/pusha-t-announces-november-release-for-solo-debut-fear-of-god-ii/ |publisher=Ballerstatus |date=October 6, 2011 |access-date=October 7, 2011 |archive-date=April 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160422073904/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2011/10/06/pusha-t-announces-november-release-for-solo-debut-fear-of-god-ii/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Considered a re-release of the initial ''Fear of God'', it was released on November 8, 2011, and was well received by critics.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.metacritic.com/music/fear-of-god-ii-let-us-pray-ep|title=Fear of God II: Let Us Pray [EP] Reviews, Ratings, Credits, and More at Metacritic|work=[[Metacritic]]|publisher=[[CBS Interactive]]|access-date=December 1, 2011|archive-date=November 22, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111122222657/http://www.metacritic.com/music/fear-of-god-ii-let-us-pray-ep|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Christgau">{{cite web|last=Christgau|first=Robert|author-link=Robert Christgau|url=http://social.entertainment.msn.com/music/blogs/expert-witness-blogpost.aspx?post=c2981ce0-d004-4c7d-bc83-0efec79f284a|title=Tom Waits/Pusha T|work=[[MSN Music]]|publisher=[[Microsoft]]|date=November 29, 2011|access-date=December 1, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111204052024/http://social.entertainment.msn.com/music/blogs/expert-witness-blogpost.aspx?post=c2981ce0-d004-4c7d-bc83-0efec79f284a|archive-date=December 4, 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[File:Kanye at Lollapalooza Santiago 2011 9.jpg|thumb|left|Pusha T (left) performing "[[Runaway (Kanye West song)|Runaway]]" with Kanye West at [[Lollapalooza Chile]] in [[Santiago]], 2011]]
The first single from ''Fear of God II: Let Us Pray'' was leaked onto the Internet on July 8, 2011. The song, titled "[[Trouble on My Mind]]", features [[Odd Future]] frontman [[Tyler, The Creator]]. The EP's second single, "Amen", was released September 13, 2011. The song, originally meant for [[Young Jeezy]], was produced by [[Shawty Redd]] and features verses from Kanye West and Young Jeezy, respectively. After the EP's release on November 8, the project debuted at number 66 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] with 8,900 copies sold in its first week released.<ref>{{cite web |last=Jacobs |first=Allen |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.17630/title.hip-hop-album-sales-the-week-ending-11-13-2011 |title=Hip Hop Album Sales: The Week Ending 11/13/2011 |publisher=HipHop DX |date=November 16, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130601054555/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.17630/title.hip-hop-album-sales-the-week-ending-11-13-2011 |archive-date=June 1, 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="Billboard">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/artist/1531316/pusha+t/chart?f=305 |title=Pusha T - Chart history |magazine=Billboard |date=October 26, 2013 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=May 14, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190514164543/https://www.billboard.com/artist/1531316/pusha+t/chart?f=305 |url-status=live }}</ref> It also entered at number ten on ''[[Billboard charts|Billboard]]''{{'}}s [[R&B/Hip-Hop Albums]], at number eight on ''[[Billboard charts|Billboard]]''{{'}}s [[Top Rap Albums]], and at number 25 on its [[Top Digital Albums|Digital Albums]] chart.<ref name="R&B">[https://www.billboard.com/charts/2011-11-26/r-b-hip-hop-albums R&B/Hip-Hop Albums – Week of November 26, 2011] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140718091453/http://www.billboard.com/charts/2011-11-26/r-b-hip-hop-albums |date=July 18, 2014 }}. ''Billboard''. Retrieved on November 19, 2011.</ref><ref name="Rap">[https://www.billboard.com/charts/2011-11-26/rap-albums Rap Albums – Week of November 26, 2011] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140718111651/http://www.billboard.com/charts/2011-11-26/rap-albums |date=July 18, 2014 }}. ''Billboard''. Retrieved on November 19, 2011.</ref>

In 2011, Pusha T appeared on the second season of the [[HBO]] [[Serial (radio and television)|series]] ''[[How to Make It in America]]''. He played the role of a henchman for Urban Caribbean League.<ref>[http://www.theboombox.com/2011/05/27/pusha-t-lands-new-role-on-hbos-how-to-make-it-in-america/ Pusha T Lands New Role On HBO's 'How To Make It In America'] "The Boom Box". May 27, 2011.</ref> After the release of his EP, Pusha T started working on his solo debut studio album, which would be executively produced by Kanye West.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.vibe.com/posts/bangladesh-talks-producing-pusha-ts-solo-album-future-good-collaborations |title=Bangladesh Talks Producing For Pusha T's Solo Album, Future G.O.O.D. Music Collaborations |publisher=Vibe |date=December 29, 2010 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=May 1, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110501214404/http://www.vibe.com/posts/bangladesh-talks-producing-pusha-ts-solo-album-future-good-collaborations |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |author=SOHH for Alex Da Kid |url=http://www.sohh.com/2011/01/i_really_feel_like_pusha_t_is_the_most_u.html |title=He Could Be Even Bigger Than He Is Right Now |publisher=Sohh.Com |date=January 11, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140424014525/http://www.sohh.com/2011/01/i_really_feel_like_pusha_t_is_the_most_u.html |archive-date=April 24, 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://pusha-t.com/2011/03/06/studio-with-ryan-leslie/ |title=Pusha T & Ryan Leslie In The Studio &#124; Pusha-T.com &#124; Official Website of G.O.O.D Music Artist Pusha T |publisher=Pusha-T.com |date=March 6, 2011 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110926153410/http://pusha-t.com/2011/03/06/studio-with-ryan-leslie/ |archive-date=September 26, 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Pusha also said that after the release of his solo debut album and the [[Re-Up Gang]] mixtape, ''Long Live the Cane'', [[Clipse]] would also reunite and release another album.<ref name="mtv"/> In late 2011, Pusha made a guest appearance on English [[Pop music|pop]] singer [[Pixie Lott]]'s "[[What Do You Take Me For?]]", the second single from her second studio album ''[[Young Foolish Happy]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/music/news/a342027/pixie-lott-confirms-new-single-what-do-you-take-me-for.html|title=Pixie Lott confirms new single|website=[[Digital Spy]]|date=September 23, 2011|access-date=July 1, 2016|archive-date=September 24, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924144957/http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/music/news/a342027/pixie-lott-confirms-new-single-what-do-you-take-me-for.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The song was a [[hit single]] in the UK, peaking at number ten on the [[UK Top 40]]. Clipse then went on a full hiatus as his brother renamed himself "No Malice" and pursued a brief Christian hip-hop career.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gee |first=Andre |date=2024-09-04 |title=How Pusha T and Malice Resurrected the Clipse After 14 Years |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/clipse-pusha-t-malice-new-album-interview-1235076985/ |access-date=2025-03-21 |website=Rolling Stone |language=en-US}}</ref>

===2012–2014: ''My Name Is My Name''===
{{main|My Name Is My Name}}
On October 19, 2011, via [[Twitter]], Kanye West announced plans for a Spring 2012 [[GOOD Music]] album, featuring all artists from the label as well as numerous guest appearances. On April 6, 2012, "[[Mercy (Kanye West song)|Mercy]]", the lead single from the GOOD Music compilation album ''[[Cruel Summer (GOOD Music album)|Cruel Summer]]'' was released. The song, produced by newly signed in-house producer Lifted, features Pusha T along with Kanye West, [[Big Sean]] and [[Southern hip hop|southern rapper]] [[2 Chainz]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.djbooth.net/index/tracks/review/good-music-mercy/ |title=G.O.O.D Music - Mercy |newspaper=Djbooth |publisher=DJBooth.net |date=April 6, 2012 |access-date=April 9, 2012 |archive-date=July 5, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150705163428/http://www.djbooth.net/index/tracks/review/good-music-mercy/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On May 24, 2012, Pusha T released a song titled "[[Exodus 23:1]]" featuring [[The-Dream]]. It is speculated that the song is a [[diss track]] aimed at [[Young Money]] rappers [[Drake (entertainer)|Drake]] and [[Lil Wayne]].<ref name="youngmoney">{{cite web |last=Markman |first=Rob |url=http://rapfix.mtv.com/2012/05/31/pusha-t-exodus-diss-lil-wayne-not-mentioned-in-recording/ |title=Lil Wayne Wasn't Mentioned When Pusha T Recorded 'Exodus 23:1' |publisher=MTV |date=May 31, 2012 |access-date=June 20, 2012 |archive-date=July 5, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120705153538/http://rapfix.mtv.com/2012/05/31/pusha-t-exodus-diss-lil-wayne-not-mentioned-in-recording |url-status=dead }}</ref> This resulted in a diss-song by Lil Wayne, which Pusha T did not directly respond to, but branded it as "horrible" in an interview.<ref>{{cite web|last= Langhorne|first= Cyrus|url= http://www.sohh.com/2012/06/pusha_t_calls_lil_waynes_diss_track_weak.html|title= Pusha T Calls Lil Wayne's Diss Track Weak, "It Was Horrible. It Was Trash. It Wasn't Good" [Audio]|publisher= Sohh.com|date= June 21, 2012|access-date= June 21, 2012|archive-date= June 23, 2012|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120623200831/http://www.sohh.com/2012/06/pusha_t_calls_lil_waynes_diss_track_weak.html|url-status= dead}}</ref> He was featured alongside West on the third single for ''Cruel Summer'' - "[[New God Flow]]". In October 2012, Pusha T made an appearance on the [[The Man with the Iron Fists (soundtrack)|soundtrack to the film]] ''[[The Man with the Iron Fists]]'' on a track titled "Tick Tock" alongside fellow American rappers [[Raekwon]], [[Joell Ortiz]] and [[Danny Brown (rapper)|Danny Brown]].<ref>{{cite web | author=Jeffries, David | url=https://www.allmusic.com/album/the-man-with-the-iron-fists-original-motion-picture-soundtrack-mw0002419973 | title=The Man with the Iron Fists <nowiki>[Original Motion Picture Soundtrack]</nowiki> – Original Soundtrack | publisher=[[Rovi Corporation]] | work=[[AllMusic]] | access-date=October 28, 2012 | archive-date=October 26, 2012 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121026024034/http://www.allmusic.com/album/the-man-with-the-iron-fists-original-motion-picture-soundtrack-mw0002419973 | url-status=live }}</ref>

On October 8, 2012, Pusha T released "[[Pain (Pusha T song)|Pain]]" featuring southern rapper [[Future (rapper)|Future]], the first single from his debut album. That same day [[Spin (magazine)|''Spin'' magazine]] reported that Pusha T's debut album had been pushed back until 2013.<ref>{{cite web |last=Martins |first=Chris |url=https://www.spin.com/2012/10/pusha-t-pain-trayvon-martin-the-wire-kanye-future/ |title=Pusha T Ties Trayvon Martin To 'The Wire' In Kanye-Produced 'Pain' &#124; SPIN &#124; SPIN Mix &#124; Songs |publisher=SPIN |date=October 9, 2012 |access-date=November 30, 2012 |archive-date=July 16, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150716115332/http://www.spin.com/2012/10/pusha-t-pain-trayvon-martin-the-wire-kanye-future/ |url-status=live }}</ref> While performing in [[Vancouver]], Canada, it was reported that Pusha T announced he would be releasing a mixtape entitled ''Wrath of Caine'' preceding the release of his debut studio album.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2012/11/11/pusha-t-announces-wrath-of-caine-mixtape/ |title=Pusha T Performs In Vancouver, Announces Wrath of Caine Mixtape |publisher=2DopeBoyz.com |date=November 11, 2012 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=April 1, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160401191635/http://2dopeboyz.com/2012/11/11/pusha-t-announces-wrath-of-caine-mixtape/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="www.hiphopdx.com">{{cite web | last = Vasquez | first = Andres | title = Pusha T To Release "Wrath Of Caine" Mixtape Before His Album | url = http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.21847/title.pusha-t-to-release-wrath-of-caine-mixtape-before-his-album | publisher = Cheri Media Group | date = November 12, 2012 | access-date = November 13, 2012 | archive-date = March 19, 2015 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150319064455/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.21847/title.pusha-t-to-release-wrath-of-caine-mixtape-before-his-album | url-status = live }}</ref> Later in November, Thornton announced the title of his debut studio album to be ''[[My Name Is My Name]]''.<ref>{{cite web |last=Kuperstein |first=Slava |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.22048/title.pusha-t-announces-album-title-release-date |title=Pusha T Announces Album Title & Release Date |publisher=HipHop DX |date=November 25, 2012 |access-date=November 30, 2012 |archive-date=December 6, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121206020258/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.22048/title.pusha-t-announces-album-title-release-date |url-status=live }}</ref> On December 5, 2012, Pusha T released "Blocka" the first single from his ''Wrath of Caine'' mixtape. The song features [[Travis Scott]] and [[Popcaan]].<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Greenwald |first=David |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/pusha-t-drops-reggae-inspired-blocka-listen-473838/ |title=Pusha T Drops Reggae-Inspired 'Blocka' |magazine=Billboard |date=December 5, 2012 |access-date=December 6, 2012 |archive-date=March 17, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130317025740/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/the-juice/473838/pusha-t-drops-reggae-inspired-blocka-listen |url-status=live }}</ref> The music video for the song was released on December 11, 2012.<ref>{{cite web |author=Jake Crates |url=http://allhiphop.com/videos/pusha-t-feat-popcaan-and-travis-scott-blocka/ |title=Pusha T feat Popcaan and Travis Scott – Blocka |publisher=AllHipHop.com |date=December 11, 2012 |access-date=March 30, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170225082412/http://allhiphop.com/videos/pusha-t-feat-popcaan-and-travis-scott-blocka/ |archive-date=February 25, 2017 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The mixtape was released on January 28, 2013, along with the announcement of his album coming at the end of the first quarter of 2013. In January 2013 it was announced that Pusha would be performing at the 2013 [[Coachella Valley Music and Arts Festival]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/01/check-out-the-2013-coachella-lineup |title=Check Out the 2013 Coachella Lineup |publisher=Complex |date=January 25, 2013 |access-date=March 30, 2014 |archive-date=December 28, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161228015532/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/01/check-out-the-2013-coachella-lineup |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Pusha T 2013.jpg|thumb|right|upright|Pusha T performing in 2013]]
''My Name Is My Name'' was scheduled to be released on July 16, 2013, but was pushed back to October. Pusha T's debut studio album was released on October 8, 2013, featuring guest appearances from [[Rick Ross]], [[Young Jeezy]], [[2 Chainz]], [[Big Sean]], [[Future (rapper)|Future]], [[Pharrell Williams]], [[Chris Brown]] and [[Kendrick Lamar]], among others. The album was met with acclaim from music critics and was positioned high on many "Best Albums of 2013" lists by major publications. The album also fared well commercially, debuting at number 4 on the US [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]], selling 74,000 copies in its first week of release. As of July 8, 2017, the album has sold 182,000 copies in the United States. The album was promoted by five singles: "[[Pain (Pusha T song)|Pain]]" featuring Future, "Numbers on the Boards", "[[Sweet Serenade]]" featuring Chris Brown, "Let Me Love You" featuring [[Kelly Rowland]], and "[[Nosetalgia]]" featuring Kendrick Lamar, along with the promotional single "Who I Am" featuring 2 Chainz and Big Sean.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.metacritic.com/music/my-name-is-my-name/pusha-t |title=My Name Is My Name Reviews |publisher=Metacritic.com |access-date=October 12, 2013 |archive-date=October 10, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131010084412/http://www.metacritic.com/music/my-name-is-my-name/pusha-t |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/10/miley-cyrus-pusha-t-danny-brown-first-week-sales |title=Miley Cyrus, Pusha T & Danny Brown First Week Sales |publisher=Complex |access-date=October 16, 2013 |archive-date=February 3, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160203013620/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/10/miley-cyrus-pusha-t-danny-brown-first-week-sales |url-status=live }}</ref> The album includes production from Kanye West, [[The Neptunes]], [[The-Dream]], [[Just Blaze]], [[No I.D.]], [[Nottz]], [[Don Cannon]] and [[Swizz Beatz]], among others. In an April 2013 interview with ''[[AskMen]]'', Pusha revealed that shortly after his debut album was released, he would finish working on his second album, titled ''King Push''.<ref name=SecondLP>{{cite news|last=Paine|first=Jake|title=Pusha T Reveals "Ni**as In Paris" Beat Was Offered To Him, Sophomore LP Title|url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.23762/title.pusha-t-reveals-ni-as-in-paris-beat-was-offered-to-him-sophomore-lp-title|access-date=April 30, 2013|newspaper=HipHopDX|date=April 30, 2013|archive-date=May 2, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130502005851/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.23762/title.pusha-t-reveals-ni-as-in-paris-beat-was-offered-to-him-sophomore-lp-title/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Pusha T's 'King Push' Has A Release Date|url=http://www.fashionably-early.com/2015/05/11/pusha-ts-king-push-has-a-release-date/|website=Fashionably-Early|date=May 11, 2015|access-date=May 11, 2015}}</ref> In 2013, Pusha T provided guest vocals over a Vice remix of "[[I'll Be Gone (Linkin Park song)|I’LL BE GONE]]" for the remix album, ''[[Recharged (Linkin Park album)|Recharged]]'', by American rock band [[Linkin Park]].

===2014–2017: ''King Push – Darkest Before Dawn''===
{{main|King Push – Darkest Before Dawn: The Prelude}}

On November 19, 2014, Pusha released a standalone single titled "[[Lunch Money (song)|Lunch Money]]," produced by Kanye West.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://pitchfork.com/news/57513-pusha-t-shares-lunch-money-produced-by-kanye/ |title=Pusha T Shares "Lunch Money", Produced by Kanye &#124; News |publisher=Pitchfork |date=November 18, 2014 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=January 11, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160111081213/http://pitchfork.com/news/57513-pusha-t-shares-lunch-money-produced-by-kanye/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In December 2014, Pusha T ''King Push'' for a Spring 2015 release.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/457952-pusha-t-says-king-push-is-dropping-spring-2015-news |title=Pusha T Says "King Push" Is Dropping Spring 2015 |website=Hotnewhiphop.com |date=December 22, 2014 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=November 28, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151128053934/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/pusha-t-says-king-push-is-dropping-spring-2015-news.13342.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Vozick |first=Simon |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/pusha-t-promises-amazing-new-album-in-spring-2015-20141222 |title=Pusha T Promises 'Amazing' New Album in 2015 |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=December 22, 2014 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=May 13, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170513142639/http://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/pusha-t-promises-amazing-new-album-in-spring-2015-20141222 |url-status=live }}</ref> In May 2015, he announced the album would be pushed back to June.<ref>{{cite web |last=Balfour |first=Jay |url=http://hiphopdx.com/news/id.31853/title.pusha-t-planning-spring-2015-release-for-king-push |title=Pusha T Announces "King Push" Release Date |website=HipHopDX.com |date=December 9, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=May 13, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170513080356/http://hiphopdx.com/news/id.31853/title.pusha-t-planning-spring-2015-release-for-king-push |url-status=live }}</ref> On November 9, 2015, Pusha T revealed to ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' that he had been appointed the new president of GOOD Music.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.vogue.com/13370864/pusha-t-kanye-west-rappers/ |title=G.O.O.D. Music's New President, Pusha T, on His Work Uniform |work=Vogue |date=November 13, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=March 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304112524/http://www.vogue.com/13370864/pusha-t-kanye-west-rappers/ |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |author=Adelle Platon |url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/6753076/pusha-t-president-good-music-exclusive |title=Pusha T Named President of G.O.O.D. Music: Exclusive |magazine=Billboard |date=November 9, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=November 12, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151112001322/http://www.billboard.com/articles/news/6753076/pusha-t-president-good-music-exclusive |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Pusha T (21898809281).jpg|thumb|left|upright|Pusha T performing at the 2015 [[Hopscotch Music Festival]]]]
On November 12, 2015, Pusha T released a single titled "Untouchable", produced by [[Timbaland]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.stereogum.com/1843541/pusha-t-untouchable-prod-timbaland/mp3s/ |title=Pusha T – "Untouchable" (Prod. Timbaland) |website=Stereogum.com |date=November 12, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160101082247/http://www.stereogum.com/1843541/pusha-t-untouchable-prod-timbaland/mp3s/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On November 23, 2015, it was announced that Pusha T would release a new album, ''[[King Push – Darkest Before Dawn: The Prelude]]'' before the aforementioned ''King Push'' album, serving as a prelude to ''King Push''. It was released on December 18, 2015, and received favorable reception from critics, and reached at number 20 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]]. The album was [[executive producer|executive produced]] by one of Pusha T's inspirations, [[Sean Combs|Puff Daddy]]<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/jay-balfour/pusha-t-defends-puff-daddy-as-a-producer |title=Pusha T Defends Puff Daddy as a Producer: "Puff Surprised Me The Most" |website=complex.com |date=2015-12-23 |access-date=2023-10-12 |archive-date=October 13, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231013170323/https://www.complex.com/music/a/jay-balfour/pusha-t-defends-puff-daddy-as-a-producer |url-status=live }}</ref> as well as manager [[Steven Victor]].<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Kreps |first=Daniel |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/pusha-t-sets-date-for-king-push-prelude-darkest-before-dawn-20151123 |title=Pusha T Sets Date for 'King Push' Prelude 'Darkest Before Dawn' |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=November 23, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=April 10, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170410220337/http://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/pusha-t-sets-date-for-king-push-prelude-darkest-before-dawn-20151123 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="metacritic">{{cite web|url=http://www.metacritic.com/music/king-push---darkest-before-dawn-the-prelude/pusha-t|title=Reviews for King Push – Darkest Before Dawn: The Prelude by Pusha T|publisher=[[Metacritic]]|access-date=January 5, 2016|archive-date=May 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170518183859/http://www.metacritic.com/music/king-push---darkest-before-dawn-the-prelude/pusha-t|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.allmusic.com/album/king-push-darkest-before-dawn-the-prelude-mw0002898501 |title=King Push – Darkest Before Dawn: The Prelude – Pusha T |publisher=[[AllMusic]] |access-date=January 5, 2016 |last=Jeffries |first=David |archive-date=January 8, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160108022416/http://www.allmusic.com/album/king-push-darkest-before-dawn-the-prelude-mw0002898501 |url-status=live }}</ref> ''Darkest Before Dawn'' reached over 17 million streams in its first week. On May 31, 2016, Pusha T released the lead single from his upcoming ''King Push'' album, "[[Drug Dealers Anonymous]]" featuring [[Jay-Z]], initially exclusively through Jay-Z's streaming service [[Tidal (service)|Tidal]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/584297-pusha-t-drug-dealers-anonymous-feat-jay-z-prod-by-dj-dahi-new-song|title=Pusha T - Drug Dealers Anonymous Feat. Jay Z (Prod. By DJ Dahi) {{!}} Stream [New Song]|website=HotNewHipHop|date=June 2016|access-date=June 7, 2016|archive-date=June 4, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160604062843/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/pusha-t-drug-dealers-anonymous-feat-jay-z-prod-by-dj-dahi-new-song.1970008.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2017, he collaborated once again with Linkin Park on the song "[[Good Goodbye (Linkin Park song)|Good Goodbye]]" along with British rapper [[Stormzy]] on the band's seventh studio album ''[[One More Light]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.list.co.uk/article/90251-linkin-park-team-up-with-stormzy-and-pusha-t/|title=Linkin Park team up with Stormzy and Pusha T|date=April 10, 2017|access-date=April 11, 2017|archive-date=April 12, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170412062605/https://www.list.co.uk/article/90251-linkin-park-team-up-with-stormzy-and-pusha-t/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the same year, Pusha T collaborated with the virtual band [[Gorillaz]] on the song "[[Let Me Out (song)|Let Me Out]]" from their studio album ''[[Humanz]]''.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/allsongs/2017/04/06/522890321/pusha-t-and-mavis-staples-join-gorillaz-for-let-me-out|title=Pusha T And Mavis Staples Join Gorillaz For 'Let Me Out'|newspaper=NPR|date=April 6, 2017|last1=Gotrich|first1=Lars|access-date=April 5, 2018|archive-date=May 14, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180514141926/https://www.npr.org/sections/allsongs/2017/04/06/522890321/pusha-t-and-mavis-staples-join-gorillaz-for-let-me-out|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2018–2022: ''Daytona'' and ''It's Almost Dry'' ===
{{main|Daytona (album)|It's Almost Dry}}
On April 19, 2018, Kanye West announced on his Twitter page that Pusha T's ''King Push'' album would be released on May 25, 2018.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Daramola|first1=Israel|title=Kanye West Announces New Pusha T Album Release Date|url=https://www.spin.com/2018/04/pusha-t-king-push-release-date-kanye-west/|website=SPIN|access-date=May 15, 2018|date=April 19, 2018|archive-date=May 15, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180515184644/https://www.spin.com/2018/04/pusha-t-king-push-release-date-kanye-west/|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 23, 2018, Pusha T announced on his Twitter page that he changed the album title from ''King Push'' to [[Daytona (album)|''Daytona'']].<ref>{{cite magazine|last1=Penrose|first1=Nerisha|title=Pusha T Changes Album Title From 'King Push' to 'Daytona,' Reveals Track List|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/pusha-t-changes-album-title-king-push-daytona-track-list-8457628/|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 24, 2018|date=May 23, 2018|archive-date=May 24, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180524032339/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8457628/pusha-t-changes-album-title-king-push-daytona-track-list|url-status=live}}</ref> The album garnered controversy when West spent $85,000 to acquire the cover art: a photo of [[Whitney Houston]]'s drug-filled bathroom, taken in her [[Atlanta, Georgia]] residence in 2006. Not only did the content of the album cover cause outside controversy, but Pusha T claims to not have agreed with the price West paid for the photo.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Lenker|first1=Maureen Lee|title=Kanye West Paid $85,000 For a Photo of Whitney Houston's Bathroom for Pusha T's New Album|url=https://time.com/5291212/kanye-west-pusha-t-daytona-whitney-houston/|website=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|access-date=May 25, 2018|date=May 25, 2018|archive-date=May 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526042041/http://time.com/5291212/kanye-west-pusha-t-daytona-whitney-houston/|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 25, 2018, ''Daytona'' released to critical acclaim. It received an 86 on Metacritic, indicating "universal acclaim".<ref>{{Citation|title=DAYTONA by Pusha T|url=https://www.metacritic.com/music/daytona/pusha-t|language=en|access-date=December 6, 2018|archive-date=December 5, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181205144230/https://www.metacritic.com/music/daytona/pusha-t|url-status=live}}</ref> Subsequently, he was named the "Best Rapper Alive" by [[Complex (magazine)|Complex]] for 2018 for his work on ''Daytona'' and his diss track "[[The Story of Adidon]]".<ref>{{cite web|title=The Best Rapper Alive, Every Year Since 1979|url=https://www.complex.com/music/the-best-rapper-alive-every-year-since-1979|website=Complex|access-date=March 11, 2019|date=September 2, 2019|archive-date=February 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180203161718/http://www.complex.com/music/the-best-rapper-alive-every-year-since-1979|url-status=live}}</ref> The album received praise from hip-hop veterans Nas and P. Diddy, calling it "a classic album" and "a modern day masterpiece".<ref>{{Cite magazine|date=May 25, 2018|title=Diddy Calls Pusha T's 'Daytona' Album 'A Modern Day Masterpiece'|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/diddy-pusha-t-daytona-album-8457941/|access-date=June 7, 2020|magazine=Billboard|archive-date=June 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200607133232/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8457941/diddy-pusha-t-daytona-album|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=May 25, 2018|title=Diddy, Nas, Jermaine Dupri & More Praise Pusha T's "DAYTONA"|url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.46998/title.diddy-nas-jermaine-dupri-more-praise-pusha-ts-daytona|access-date=June 7, 2020|website=HipHopDX|archive-date=June 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200607133229/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.46998/title.diddy-nas-jermaine-dupri-more-praise-pusha-ts-daytona|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2024, [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] named it as the 2nd 'Most Scathing Hip-Hop Diss of All Time'.<ref name=":0">{{Cite magazine |last=Scott |first=Damlen |title=The 15 Most Scathing Hip-Hop Diss Songs of All Time |url=https://www.billboard.com/lists/diss-songs-rap/t-i-whats-up-what-happenin/ |magazine=Billboard}}</ref>

In February 2020, Pusha T started a new record label, Heir Wave Media Group, to sign and develop artists from the [[Virginia]] area. He remained the president of GOOD Music while focusing on building a roster of artists from his home state. In creating the label, Pusha cited his desire to recruit "album artists" who craft narratives and larger stories throughout their projects.<ref name=Complex1>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2020/03/pusha-t-new-label-heir-wave-music-group-interview|first=Jessica|last=McKinney|title=Why Pusha-T Is Starting a New Record Label|work=Complex|date=March 5, 2020|access-date=November 9, 2021|archive-date=November 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211109025650/https://www.complex.com/music/2020/03/pusha-t-new-label-heir-wave-music-group-interview|url-status=live}}</ref> Local artists were encouraged to submit music to the Heir Wave Music YouTube channel and a weekly radio show on a local station, [[WOWI|103 Jamz FM]].<ref name=Pilot>{{cite web|url=https://www.pilotonline.com/entertainment/music/vp-db-pusha-t-heir-wave-music-group-20201014-ei2cv27vjzbjppjc4sjcn226ke-story.html|title=Pusha T talks love and strategy for helping his hometown become a music hotbed|date=October 14, 2020|access-date=November 8, 2021|first=Amy|last=Poulter|work=The Virginian Pilot}}</ref> In the label's first year, Heir Wave Music signed Kahri 1k, Shaolinn, Leeto, and WhyNotDuce.<ref name=Complex1 /><ref name=DMVRevamped>{{cite web|url=https://allhiphop.com/news/pusha-ts-hwmg-label-to-host-dmv-revamped-charity-twitch-concert/|title=Pusha T's HWMG Label To Host "DMV Revamped" Charity Twitch Concert|first=Yohance|last=Kyles|date=February 19, 2021|access-date=November 8, 2021|work=AllHipHop|archive-date=November 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211109025652/https://allhiphop.com/news/pusha-ts-hwmg-label-to-host-dmv-revamped-charity-twitch-concert/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On February 7, 2022, Pusha T released "[[Diet Coke (song)|Diet Coke]]", the lead single from his fourth studio album ''[[It's Almost Dry]]'', which was produced by [[Kanye West]] and [[88-Keys]]. The music video for the single was released the next day, directed by Omar Jones, and features Pusha T and West moving around over a white backdrop, which pays direct homage to [[Craig Mack]]'s "[[Flava in Ya Ear#Remix|Flava in Ya Ear (Remix)]]" music video.<ref name=Okayplayer>{{cite web|url=https://www.okayplayer.com/music/pusha-t-diet-coke-video.html|first=Jaelani|last=Turner-Williams|title=Watch Pusha-T & Kanye West Channel Biggie & Diddy in "Diet Coke" Video|work=[[Okayplayer]]|date=February 8, 2022|access-date=February 26, 2022|archive-date=February 26, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220226190508/https://www.okayplayer.com/music/pusha-t-diet-coke-video.html|url-status=live}}</ref> 88-Keys later revealed that the beat for the single was made 18 years before the song was made, on a beat tape named ''The Makings of Crack Cocaine''.<ref name=HipHopDX>{{cite web|url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.67892/title.producer-88-keys-reveals-18-year-old-origin-of-pusha-ts-diet-coke-beat|first=Michael|last=Saponara|title=Producer 88-Keys Reveals 18-Year-Old Origin of Pusha T's 'Diet Coke' Beat|work=[[HipHopDX]]|date=February 8, 2022|access-date=February 26, 2022|archive-date=February 26, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220226190508/https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.67892/title.producer-88-keys-reveals-18-year-old-origin-of-pusha-ts-diet-coke-beat|url-status=live}}</ref>

On March 4, Pusha T and [[Nigo]] released the single "Hear Me Clearly" for Nigo's album ''[[I Know Nigo!]]'', the song is also set to appear on ''It's Almost Dry''. He also appeared alongside his brother (once again credited as Malice) on the Clipse song "Punch Bowl" on the ''I Know Nigo!'' album as well. The music video also for "Hear Me Clearly" released on March 30.<ref name=Hypebeast>{{cite web|url=https://hypebeast.com/2022/3/pusha-t-nigo-hear-me-clearly-music-video|first=Dylan|last=Kelly|title=Pusha T and NIGO Drop Chaotic "Hear Me Clearly" Music Video|work=[[Hypebeast (website)|Hypebeast]]|date=April 19, 2022|access-date=April 19, 2022|archive-date=April 19, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220419043809/https://hypebeast.com/2022/3/pusha-t-nigo-hear-me-clearly-music-video|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gee |first=Andre |date=2024-09-04 |title=How Pusha T and Malice Resurrected the Clipse After 14 Years |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/clipse-pusha-t-malice-new-album-interview-1235076985/ |access-date=2025-03-21 |website=Rolling Stone |language=en-US}}</ref>

On April 6, Pusha T released the third single for ''It's Almost Dry'', "[[Neck & Wrist]]" featuring rapper [[Jay-Z]] and Pharrell, and would later appear in an interview with [[Charlamagne tha God]], which released on the 11th.<ref name=Complex>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/watch-pusha-t-new-interview-with-charlamagne-tha-god|first=Trace|last=Cowen|title=Watch Pusha T's New Interview With Charlemagne Tha God|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|date=April 11, 2022|access-date=April 19, 2022|archive-date=April 19, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220419193950/https://www.complex.com/music/watch-pusha-t-new-interview-with-charlamagne-tha-god|url-status=live}}</ref> On the 18th, Pusha T announced the release date of ''It's Almost Dry'' as April 22,<ref name=Billboardb>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/pusha-t-new-album-its-almost-dry-1235060464/|first=Fred|last=Sahai|title=Pusha T New Album 'It's Almost Dry' Release Date|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=April 18, 2022|access-date=April 19, 2022|archive-date=April 19, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220419110021/https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/pusha-t-new-album-its-almost-dry-1235060464/|url-status=live}}</ref> and also announced a listening party in New York City named "Cokechella".<ref name=Fader>{{cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2022/04/18/pusha-t-announces-cokechella-an-album-listening-event-for-its-almost-dry|first=Jordan|last=Darville|title=Pusha T announces "Cokechella," an album listening event for ''It's Almost Dry''.|work=[[Fader (magazine)|Fader]]|date=April 18, 2022|access-date=April 19, 2022|archive-date=April 18, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220418151836/https://www.thefader.com/2022/04/18/pusha-t-announces-cokechella-an-album-listening-event-for-its-almost-dry|url-status=live}}</ref>

The much-delayed album, primarily produced by frequent collaborators [[Pharrell Williams]] and [[Kanye West]], was released on April 22 to widespread critical acclaim.<ref name="Pitchfork">{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-releases-new-album-its-almost-dry-listen/|first=Matthew|last=Strauss|title=Pusha T Releases New Album It's Almost Dry: Listen and Read the Full Credits|work=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]|date=April 22, 2022|access-date=April 23, 2022|archive-date=April 22, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422122602/https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-releases-new-album-its-almost-dry-listen/|url-status=live}}</ref> On the week dated May 7, 2022, it debuted atop the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] with a total of 55,000 album equivalent units, becoming Pusha T's first number-one album on the chart.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Caulfield |first=Keith |date=2022-05-01 |title=Pusha T Scores First No. 1 Album on Billboard 200 Chart With 'It's Almost Dry' |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/pusha-t-its-almost-dry-billboard-200-chart-debut-1235065350/ |access-date=2022-05-08 |magazine=Billboard |language=en-US |archive-date=May 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220508051611/https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/pusha-t-its-almost-dry-billboard-200-chart-debut-1235065350/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Here Are the First Week Numbers for Pusha-T's Chart-Topping Album 'It's Almost Dry' |url=https://www.complex.com/music/pusha-t-its-almost-dry-first-week-numbers |access-date=2022-05-08 |website=Complex |language=en |archive-date=May 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220508150645/https://www.complex.com/music/pusha-t-its-almost-dry-first-week-numbers |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== 2022–2023: Heir Wave Music Group and departure from GOOD Music ===
In December 2022, Pusha T announced his departure from GOOD Music after founder Kanye West's [[Antisemitism|antisemitic]] and [[White Lives Matter|pro-race]] remarks. Speaking on West's support for "White Lives Matter", his [[Kanye West 2024 presidential campaign|2024 presidential campaign]] and other public outbursts, Pusha T stated that they were nothing to "tap dance around".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Garcia |first=Thania |date=2022-12-20 |title=Pusha T Confirms He's Cut All Ties With Kanye West's G.O.O.D. Music |url=https://variety.com/2022/music/news/pusha-t-good-music-kanye-west-1235466370/ |access-date=2023-02-07 |website=Variety |language=en-US |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207043710/https://variety.com/2022/music/news/pusha-t-good-music-kanye-west-1235466370/ |url-status=live }}</ref> His departure also ended his tenure as the label's president. Shortly after, he changed his focus on his own label, Heir Wave Music Group. During an interview with ''[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]'', Pusha T stated: "I have a particular need to build out Heir Wave as a platform and build out a circuit for artists within this region, simply because I feel like it's something that we've never had." He further added: "[the label] was made in the spirit of an “album artist”—artists who have a story to tell. They have a background for the fan who wants to be fully invested in that particular artist, their whole movement, and everything about them. That's how we’re picking artists. It's about authenticity. It's about pure music, whatever type of music it is. It must be pure."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/j-mckinney/pusha-t-new-label-heir-wave-music-group-interview|title=Why Pusha-T Is Starting a New Record Label|last=McKinney|first=Jessica|website=[[Complex Networks]]|date=March 5, 2020|access-date=October 11, 2023|archive-date=October 13, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231013170322/https://www.complex.com/music/a/j-mckinney/pusha-t-new-label-heir-wave-music-group-interview|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2023–present: Clipse reunion ===
To promote the film, ''[[Cocaine Bear]]'', which was released on February 24, 2023, Pusha T remade the [[Grandmaster Flash]] and [[Melle Mel]] 1983 classic, "[[White Lines (Don't Don't Do It)]]", keeping the original chorus, but replacing the original verses with his own.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Blistein |first=Jon |date=2023-02-24 |title='Cocaine Bear' Needed a Song. Obviously They Got Pusha T |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/pusha-t-remix-melle-mel-white-lines-cocaine-bear-1234685924/ |access-date=2024-03-19 |magazine=Rolling Stone |language=en-US |archive-date=February 10, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240210111122/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/pusha-t-remix-melle-mel-white-lines-cocaine-bear-1234685924/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-02-24 |title=Pusha T Shares New Song "White Lines (Cocaine Bear Remix)" |url=https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-shares-new-song-white-lines-cocaine-bear-remix-listen/ |access-date=2024-03-19 |website=Pitchfork |language=en-US |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319052237/https://pitchfork.com/news/pusha-t-shares-new-song-white-lines-cocaine-bear-remix-listen/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He later performed at the [[Coachella 2023|22nd Coachella Valley Music and Arts Festival]] in April 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-04-15 |title=Pusha T Welcomes Fans To 'Cokechella' A Week After Jim Jones Slander |url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/pusha-t-cokechella-jim-jones |access-date=2023-04-26 |website=HipHopDX |language=en |archive-date=April 26, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230426161540/https://hiphopdx.com/news/pusha-t-cokechella-jim-jones |url-status=live }}</ref>

In October 2024, he headlined the Indian music festival Gully Fest alongside Indian rapper [[Divine (rapper)|Divine.]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tagat |first=Anurag |date=2024-09-13 |title=Pusha T, DIVINE Announced for Gully Fest 2024 in Mumbai |url=https://rollingstoneindia.com/gully-fest-2024-lineup-pusha-t-divine-prabh-deep/ |access-date=2026-08-17 |website=[[Rolling Stone India]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Junaini |first=Hidzir |date=2024-09-13 |title=Pusha T & DIVINE headline Gully Fest 2024, India’s premier hip-hop festival |url=https://www.bandwagon.asia/articles/pusha-t-divine-more-headline-the-2nd-edition-of-india-s-premier-hip-hop-music-festival-gully-fest |access-date=2026-08-17 |website=Bandwagon Asia |language=en}}</ref> In November 13, 2024, Thornton announced Grindin Coffee, which takes its name from the 2002 Clipse hit "Grindin'", which will launch nationwide in 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-13 |title=If You Know You Know: Pusha T Pays Homage To A Classic & Announces Grindin Coffee |url=https://globalgrind.com/playlist/push-grindin |access-date=2025-03-26 |website=GlobalGrind |language=en }}</ref>

Ahead of [[Clipse]]'s reunion and fourth studio album after fifteen years, ''[[Let God Sort Em Out]]'', Pusha T and his brother [[No Malice]] premiered an interview with ''[[GQ]]'', where the duo revealed that they were dropped from the Def Jam label as well as Pusha T as a solo artist, and also confirmed Kendrick Lamar's appearance in their upcoming album.<ref name=":0" /> The next day, Pusha T's longtime manager [[Steven Victor]] revealed during an interview with ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' that the rapper actually had to pay an undisclosed seven-figure sum to be dropped from the Def Jam label, in order to be released from both Clipse's and his own contractual obligations, elaborating further how the label has been suppressing Pusha T's collaborations with other artists and even his own music releases since [[Pusha T#Drake|his feud]] with [[Canada|Canadian]] rapper [[Drake (musician)|Drake]] in 2018.<ref>{{cite magazine | url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/pusha-t-clipse-leave-def-jam-insane-amount-money/ | title=Steven Victor on the 'Insane Amount of Money' It Took for Pusha T, Clipse to Exit Def Jam | magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] }}</ref>

== Feuds ==
===Consequence===
After Pusha T released "My God", the first single from his solo mixtape, his former [[GOOD Music]] label-mate [[Consequence (rapper)|Consequence]] took to [[Twitter]] and claimed Pusha T stole his flow and lyrics from a song of his that Pusha T was supposed to appear on, titled "The Last Supper".<ref>[http://www.complex.com/music/2011/02/consequence-claims-pusha-t-stole-lines-on-my-god Consequence Claims Pusha T Stole Lines on "My God"] Complex.com</ref> On July 22, 2011, Consequence released a song titled "The Plagiarist Society" where he takes shots and questions Pusha T's [[Mafioso rap|coke-dealing raps]]. Towards the end of the record, the Queens native promises to take aim at Kanye West next saying "You're nothing but a body shield, for that coward from the mid-west/ so yes you can bet, that your boss is next".

Pusha T responded to the diss track when he appeared on [[WGCI-FM|Chicago's 107.5 WGCI's The Morning Riot]] and said nobody is looking for Consequence, as for if he would respond on a record, Pusha T went on to say "At the end of the day I am an artist and this whole rap thing is fun to me, so I can't say you won't hear nothing, but to go back and forth with him, it's not right, I won't get anything out of it."<ref>[http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2011/07/28/pusha-t-responds-to-consequence/ Pusha T Responds To Consequence] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120930011939/http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2011/07/28/pusha-t-responds-to-consequence/ |date=September 30, 2012 }} 2dopeboyz.com</ref> Later that day, Consequence called into Chicago's WGCI's The Morning Riot in retaliation to the remarks Pusha T made about him. He also premiered a song titled "Everybody Told Me 2 (Straighten It Out)" where he disses West, Pusha T and GOOD Music in general.<ref>[http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2011/07/29/consequence-responds-to-pusha-t/ Consequence Responds To Pusha T, Drops G.O.O.D. Music Diss] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120930012028/http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2011/07/29/consequence-responds-to-pusha-t/ |date=September 30, 2012 }} 2dopeboyz.com</ref><ref>[http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2011/07/29/consequence-%E2%80%93-everybody-told-me-2-straighten-it-out/ Consequence – Everybody Told Me 2 (Pusha T, Kanye West, G.O.O.D. Music Diss)] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120930012136/http://www.2dopeboyz.com/2011/07/29/consequence-%E2%80%93-everybody-told-me-2-straighten-it-out/ |date=September 30, 2012 }} 2dopeboyz.com</ref> Since then Pusha T has responded to Consequence on a record. The song was the remix to [[Ace Hood]]'s "[[Go 'N' Get It]]" which also featured [[Beanie Sigel]], [[Busta Rhymes]] and [[Styles P]]. In the song Pusha raps "Consequence, nigga, talk is cheap You don't want a problem? Off the beef Before I off his ass with his awful teeth". On August 19, Consequence released ''Movies on Demand 3'' and continued to [[Diss track|diss]] his former label and Pusha T on songs such as "Career Killer" and "Mr.RapFix (Hot Water)".

On September 15, 2011, Consequence appeared on [[MTV]]'s Rap Fix and announced his feud with Kanye West and GOOD Music was over.<ref>[http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.16874/title.consequence-says-feud-is-over-with-kanye-west-continues-feud-with-q-tip Consequence Says "Feud Is Over With Kanye West," Continues Feud With Q-Tip] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111115140037/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.16874/title.consequence-says-feud-is-over-with-kanye-west-continues-feud-with-q-tip |date=November 15, 2011 }} HipHopDX.com</ref> While out in Chicago in October 2011, Pusha T appeared on WGCI radio for a second time, and confirmed that the "beef" was indeed over, that it had died out and they have moved on. In January 2015, the two officially ended the feud when Consequence took to his Instagram page to let the people know: "All G.O.O.D. in 2015, Pusha [T] and I deaded everything and we creatively vibed with Kanye for this [[The Life of Pablo|new LP]]."<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/30381-pusha-t-and-consequence-end-beef-to-work-on-kanye-west-s-new-album-news |title=Pusha T & Consequence End Beef To Work On Kanye West's New Album |website=Hotnewhiphop.com |date=January 29, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=November 18, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151118113821/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/pusha-t-and-consequence-end-beef-to-work-on-kanye-west-s-new-album-news.13802.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/everything-we-know-about-kanye-wests-swish-news.17030.html?image=0&gallery=2 |title=Everything We Know About Kanye West's "SWISH" |website=Hotnewhiphop.com |date=August 13, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |archive-date=November 18, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151118115506/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/everything-we-know-about-kanye-wests-swish-news.17030.html?image=0&gallery=2 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In an August 15, 2025, interview on [[WQHT|Hot 97]], Consequence criticized Pusha T and referenced messages from a group chat among collaborators during the recording of Kanye West’s ''[[Donda]]'' (2021), alleging that Pusha T had apologized to West for “dragging [him] into the beef with Drake.” Consequence also raised additional grievances, including comments he attributed to Pusha T and criticisms of his music, before walking out of the interview.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Catlin |first=Caleb |date=2025-08-16 |title=Consequence Slams Pusha T and Defends Ye in Lengthy Hot 97 Rant |url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/consequence-slams-pusha-t-and-defends-ye-in-lengthy-hot-97-rant/ |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=VICE |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Diaz |first=Angel |date=2025-08-15 |title=Consequence Walks Out of Hot 97 Interview After Calling Out Pusha T Over Ye Beef: 'I'm Outside' |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/consequence-leaves-interview-pusha-t-kanye-west-ye-beef-1236023202/ |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=Billboard |language=en-US}}</ref>  Similar sentiments were shared across Consequence's social media.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=T. M. Z. |date=2025-08-21 |title=Consequence Declares Lyrical War on Pusha T, Clowns 'Yellow Diamond' Lyrics |url=https://www.tmz.com/2025/08/21/consequence-lyrical-war-pusha-t/ |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=TMZ |language=en}}</ref>

===Lil Wayne===
The tension between Pusha T and rapper [[Lil Wayne]] had been going on for years, beginning soon after [[Clipse]] and [[Birdman (entertainer)|Birdman]] worked on "What Happened to That Boy", the latter's 2002 single. In 2006, Wayne felt the Clipse song "[[Mr. Me Too]]" was directed at him,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.missinfo.tv/index.php/history-of-lil-wayne-pusha-t-beef/ |title=A History of Lil Wayne vs. Pusha-T Beef: From "Mr. Me Too" to the "F–k Pusha" Tweet |publisher=MissInfo.tv |date=May 24, 2012 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=January 16, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170116152533/http://www.missinfo.tv/index.php/history-of-lil-wayne-pusha-t-beef/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and in 2012 Pusha T's "Exodus 23:1" song caused Lil Wayne to vent on [[Twitter]], later releasing a diss track titled "Ghoulish", in which he says "Fuck Pusha T and anybody that love him / His head up his ass, I'mma have to head-butt him".<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dHl7TzHHOuI DJ MoonDawg & Pusha T talk Drake, BET Cypher, 2012 Good Music Album on 107.5 WGCI] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160417213722/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dHl7TzHHOuI |date=April 17, 2016 }} YouTube.com</ref> Pusha T has called Wayne's diss track "horrible" and said he felt it did not deserve a response. Both men have downplayed the feud, with Wayne saying he is over it.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://hiphopwired.com/2012/10/16/exclusive-pusha-t-admits-new-god-flow-was-directed-at-birdman-details-issues-with-lil-wayne-drake/ |title=Exclusive: Pusha T Admits "New God Flow" Was Directed At Birdman; Details Issues With Lil Wayne & Drake |publisher=Hip-Hop Wired |date=October 16, 2012 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=November 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121119051207/http://hiphopwired.com/2012/10/16/exclusive-pusha-t-admits-new-god-flow-was-directed-at-birdman-details-issues-with-lil-wayne-drake/? |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Alexis |first=Nadeska |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1695657/pusha-t-new-god-flow-birdman-response.jhtml |title=Pusha T's 'New God Flow' Lyrics Aimed At Birdman |publisher=MTV.com |date=October 16, 2012 |access-date=November 12, 2012 |archive-date=October 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121019021503/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1695657/pusha-t-new-god-flow-birdman-response.jhtml |url-status=dead }}</ref> However, in September 2012, Pusha T appeared on the remix of [[Chief Keef]]'s "[[I Don't Like]]", where he accused Wayne of using [[ghostwriter]]s saying, "My pen's better, you don't write, trendsetter, you clone-like", he has since confirmed that the line was a Wayne diss.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://genius.com/757403|title=Kanye West - Don't Like.1 Lyrics|website=[[Genius (website)|Genius]]|access-date=September 2, 2019}}</ref> In late November 2012, Pusha T once again dissed Wayne and [[Birdman (rapper)|Birdman]] on a [[Ludacris]] song titled "Mad Fo" from his ''#IDGAF'' mixtape. Pusha T later revealed his motivation for the verse: "Oh because it's a [[Swizz Beatz|Swizz]] beat. You got to blame Swizz. See, when Wayne got a Swizz beat{{nbsp}}... with the 'Ghoulish' track; he got busy, well he tried to."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.vibe.com/article/pusha-t-confirms-dissing-lil-wayne-and-birdman-new-ludacris-song |title=Pusha T Confirms Dissing Lil Wayne and Birdman on New Ludacris Song |publisher=Vibe |date=November 30, 2012 |access-date=March 30, 2014 |archive-date=April 1, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140401154953/http://www.vibe.com/article/pusha-t-confirms-dissing-lil-wayne-and-birdman-new-ludacris-song |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Drake===
{{see also|The Story of Adidon}}
After the release of Pusha T's song "Exodus 23:1" in 2012, [[Drake (rapper)|Drake]] seemingly responded to Pusha on the intro to his 2013 album ''[[Nothing Was the Same]]'', calling Pusha T a "bench player acting like a starter". The two directed subliminal disses towards each other between 2012 and 2017 with Pusha T rapping on the song "H.G.T.V", "It's too far gone when the realest ain't real / I walk amongst the clouds, so your ceilings ain't real/ These niggas Call of Duty 'cause they killings ain't real / With a questionable pen so the feeling ain't real,"<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/pusha-t-8-1188741|title=Hear Pusha T diss Drake in new song 'HGTV'|date=October 13, 2016|work=NME|access-date=May 30, 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=June 12, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612113132/http://www.nme.com/news/music/pusha-t-8-1188741|url-status=live}}</ref> with Drake later responding on the diss track "[[Two Birds, One Stone]]" questioning Pusha T's drug dealing past.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/thepitch/1337-the-drake-vs-pusha-t-beef-are-we-really-doing-this/|title=The Drake vs. Pusha T Beef: Are We Really Doing This? {{!}} Pitchfork|website=pitchfork.com|date=October 24, 2016|language=en|access-date=May 30, 2018|archive-date=May 27, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180527163534/https://pitchfork.com/thepitch/1337-the-drake-vs-pusha-t-beef-are-we-really-doing-this/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Pusha T further escalated the feud on the song "Infrared" from his 2018 album ''[[Daytona (album)|Daytona]]'', where he accused Drake of using a [[ghostwriter]]. Drake responded to the song less than one day later, releasing a track titled "[[Duppy Freestyle]]" where he insults both Pusha T and [[Kanye West]], accusing Pusha of being a fake drug dealer and claiming that he would boost sales of Pusha T's album just by dissing him.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://consequence.net/2018/05/pusha-t-daytona-drake/|title=Pusha-T reignites feud with Drake on Daytona track "Infrared"|date=May 25, 2018|access-date=May 27, 2018|archive-date=May 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526185828/https://consequenceofsound.net/2018/05/pusha-t-daytona-drake/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/news/drake-shares-new-song-duppy-freestyle-listen/|title=Drake Disses Pusha-T and Kanye on New Song "Duppy Freestyle": Listen - Pitchfork|website=pitchfork.com|date=May 25, 2018|access-date=May 27, 2018|archive-date=May 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526035917/https://pitchfork.com/news/drake-shares-new-song-duppy-freestyle-listen/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/141153-pusha-t-vs-drake-a-complete-history-news|title=Pusha T Vs. Drake: A Complete History|website=HNHH|date=May 24, 2018|access-date=May 27, 2018|archive-date=May 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526112747/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/pusha-t-vs-drake-a-complete-history-news.51021.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Pusha T responded to Duppy on May 29, 2018, with the track "[[The Story of Adidon]]" which featured Pusha responding to Drake over the instrumental of Jay-Z's 2017 single "[[The Story of O.J.|The Story of O.J]]."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.vulture.com/2018/05/pusha-t-drops-retaliatory-drake-diss-the-story-of-adidon.html|title=Pusha T Drops Retaliatory Drake Diss Track 'The Story of Adidon'|last=Crucchiola|first=Jordan|work=Vulture|access-date=May 30, 2018|language=en|archive-date=May 30, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180530050640/http://www.vulture.com/2018/05/pusha-t-drops-retaliatory-drake-diss-the-story-of-adidon.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The song debuted on Hot 97 and has lines directed towards rumors Drake has an illegitimate child<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://uproxx.com/hiphop/pusha-t-drake-diss-response-story-of-adidon/|title=Pusha T Shocks With Drake Diss Track Response, 'The Story Of Adidon'|date=May 29, 2018|work=[[Uproxx]]|access-date=May 29, 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=May 30, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180530065157/https://uproxx.com/hiphop/pusha-t-drake-diss-response-story-of-adidon/|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[40 (record producer)|his producer]]'s multiple sclerosis.<ref>{{Cite book|title=People with Multiple Sclerosis|publisher=Palgrave Macmillan|isbn=9781137457066|doi = 10.1057/9781137457066.0007|chapter = Multiple Sclerosis|year = 2015}}</ref> The song additionally referenced Pusha's ongoing beef with Lil Wayne and Birdman. The song's cover art also attracted controversy, showing an unedited picture of Drake wearing a [[Jim Crow]] t-shirt and hoodie and [[blackface]] makeup.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/150021-instagram-removes-pusha-t-s-photo-of-drake-s-blackface-news|title=Instagram Removes Pusha T's Photo Of Drake's Blackface|website=HNHH|date=May 30, 2018|access-date=May 30, 2018|archive-date=May 30, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180530080139/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/instagram-removes-pusha-t-s-photo-of-drake-s-blackface-news.51392.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Drake eventually admitted to losing the feud in an interview with RapRadar, saying it was his "first loss in the competitive sport of rapping."<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Penrose |first=Nerisha |date=July 7, 2020 |title=A Timeline of Drake & Pusha T's Feud |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/drake-pusha-t-beef-timeline-8458323/ |access-date=March 24, 2022 |magazine=Billboard |language=en-US |archive-date=March 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220323112425/https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/drake-pusha-t-beef-timeline-8458323/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== McDonald's ===
In a June 2016 interview, former record executive [[Steve Stoute]] claimed that Pusha T had allegedly written the [[McDonald's advertising|McDonald's "I'm lovin' it" jingle]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Guess Which Rapper Wrote The McDonalds 'I'm Lovin' It' Jingle? |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/2888277/pusha-t-mcdonalds-jingle/ |website=[[MTV]] |access-date=September 24, 2016 |archive-date=September 24, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160924175655/http://www.mtv.com/news/2888277/pusha-t-mcdonalds-jingle/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Thornton later confirmed the news on Twitter.<ref>{{Cite news |date=June 2, 2016 |title=King Push on Twitter |url=https://twitter.com/PUSHA_T/status/738498169147957250 |access-date=December 4, 2016}}</ref> However, this claim has been disputed by advertising executives who created the campaign, and a McDonald's marketing executive. Neither Stoute nor Pusha T have commented on the dispute.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hogan |first=Marc |author-link=Marc Hogan |date=July 14, 2016 |title=The Contentious Tale of the McDonald's "I'm Lovin' It" Jingle |url=https://pitchfork.com/thepitch/1227-the-contentious-tale-of-the-mcdonalds-im-lovin-it-jingle/ |access-date=December 4, 2016 |website=Pitchfork |archive-date=December 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161204170607/http://pitchfork.com/thepitch/1227-the-contentious-tale-of-the-mcdonalds-im-lovin-it-jingle/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On March 21, 2022, Pusha T recorded a freestyle for a commercial presented by fast food chain [[Arby's]], naming it "Spicy Fish Diss", aimed at rival fast food chain [[McDonald's]]. On the track, he states that their Filet-O-Fish sandwich was "disgusting", and states that Arby's Fish Sandwich is better.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-03-21 |title=Pusha T Drops Diss Track Aimed at McDonald's for Arby's Spicy Fish Commercial |url=https://hypebeast.com/2022/3/pusha-t-arbys-spicy-fish-diss-track-mcdonalds |access-date=2023-02-07 |website=Hypebeast |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207043653/https://hypebeast.com/2022/3/pusha-t-arbys-spicy-fish-diss-track-mcdonalds |url-status=live }}</ref> This was revenge toward the company for not crediting him for helping popularize the "I'm lovin' it" advertising campaign.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Newman |first=Jason |date=2022-03-21 |title=Pusha T's New Diss Track Is an Arby's Ad. Here's Why He's Laughing All the Way to the Bank |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/pusha-t-arbys-mcdonalds-diss-1323869/ |access-date=2023-02-07 |magazine=Rolling Stone |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Jim Jones ===
Pusha T entered into a dispute with [[Jim Jones (rapper)|Jim Jones]], which began in May 2023 after the latter dismissed the former on the [[The Breakfast Club (radio show)|''Breakfast Club'']], when he claimed that he "couldn't name any top five Pusha T songs". A month later, on June 25, a snippet of an unnamed diss track by Pusha T was released.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-06-21 |title=Pusha T and Malice Reunite at Pharrell's Louis Vuitton Show With New Song and a Response to Jim Jones |url=https://www.gq.com/story/pusha-t-jim-jones-beef-explained-malice-clipse-new-song-louis-vuitton-spring-summer-2024-show |access-date=2024-03-19 |website=GQ |language=en-US |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319052237/https://www.gq.com/story/pusha-t-jim-jones-beef-explained-malice-clipse-new-song-louis-vuitton-spring-summer-2024-show |url-status=live }}</ref> The following week, on July 6, Jim Jones responded with "Summer Collection".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jim Jones Fires More Shots At Pusha T & No Malice, Prompting Jadakiss to Share Concerns About the Feud - Okayplayer |url=https://www.okayplayer.com/jim-jones-fires-more-shots-at-pusha-t-no-malice |access-date=2024-03-19 |website=www.okayplayer.com |language=en |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319052237/https://www.okayplayer.com/jim-jones-fires-more-shots-at-pusha-t-no-malice |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Jim Jones Heats Up Pusha T Beef With "Summer Collection" Diss Track - Okayplayer |url=https://www.okayplayer.com/jim-jones-heats-up-pusha-t-beef-with-summer-collection-diss-track |access-date=2024-03-19 |website=www.okayplayer.com |language=en |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319052237/https://www.okayplayer.com/jim-jones-heats-up-pusha-t-beef-with-summer-collection-diss-track |url-status=live }}</ref> Podcaster [[Joe Budden]] also defended "Summer Collection", while calling Pusha T a "sore loser".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-07-24 |title=Joe Budden Argues Pusha T 'Lost' Jim Jones Battle |url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/joe-budden-pusha-t-lost-jim-jones-battle |access-date=2024-03-19 |website=HipHopDX |language=en |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319052237/https://hiphopdx.com/news/joe-budden-pusha-t-lost-jim-jones-battle |url-status=live }}</ref> Jones' former collaborator and friend, [[Cam'ron]], also sided with him in the midst of the falling out.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Elibert |first=Mark |title=Cam'ron Tells Pusha T to Not Use 'Old Problems' He Had With Jim Jones to 'Try and Win a Battle' |url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/markelibert/camron-sides-with-jim-jones-in-pusha-t-beef |access-date=2024-03-19 |website=Complex |language=en-us |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319052237/https://www.complex.com/music/a/markelibert/camron-sides-with-jim-jones-in-pusha-t-beef |url-status=live }}</ref> Pusha's diss track titled "Chains & Whips" eventually released on July 10 exclusively on Apple Music, ahead of its release as a part of the Clipse album ''Let God Sort Em Out''.<ref>{{cite web |last=Paul |first=Bryson "Boom" |date=2025-07-10 |title=The Clipse & Apple Music Release Kendrick Lamar-Featured "Chains & Whips" Ahead Of "Let God Sort Em Out" Album |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/928018-clipse-apple-kendrick-lamar-chains-whips-let-god-sort-em-out-album-hip-hop-news |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250711001124/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/928018-clipse-apple-kendrick-lamar-chains-whips-let-god-sort-em-out-album-hip-hop-news |archive-date=2025-07-11 |access-date=2025-07-11 |website=[[HotNewHipHop]]}}</ref>

==Personal life==
[[File:Missy Elliott, Portsmouth -10 17 22 (52443908096).jpg|thumb|Pusha T at an event dedicated to [[Missy Elliott]]<ref>{{Cite news |date=2022-10-18 |title=Missy Elliott celebrated in Portsmouth, gets key to city |url=https://www.wavy.com/news/local-news/portsmouth/missy-elliott-celebrated-in-portsmouth-gets-key-to-city/ |archive-url=http://web.archive.org/web/20240913014537/https://www.wavy.com/news/local-news/portsmouth/missy-elliott-celebrated-in-portsmouth-gets-key-to-city/ |archive-date=2024-09-13 |access-date=2026-08-06 |work=10 WAVY |language=en-US}}</ref>]]
On July 21, 2018,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Berry |first=Peter A. |date=July 22, 2018 |title=Pusha-T Marries Girlfriend and Makes Pharrell His Best Man |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/pusha-t-marries-virginia-williams-pharrell-best-man/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250420110508/https://www.xxlmag.com/pusha-t-marries-virginia-williams-pharrell-best-man/ |archive-date=April 20, 2025 |access-date=June 30, 2025 |website=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL]]}}</ref> Thornton and Virginia Williams, his girlfriend of eleven years, married in their hometown of Virginia Beach. The wedding was officiated by his brother Gene ([[Malice (rapper)|Malice]]) Thornton and attended by celebrities such as Kim Kardashian, Kanye West, and [[best man]] Pharrell Williams.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Macon |first=Alexandra |date=July 22, 2018 |title=Pusha T Marries His Longtime Love in a Dior Dinner Jacket and Tuxedo Pants by Kim Jones |url=https://www.vogue.com/article/pusha-t-terrence-thornton-wears-dior-kim-jones-suit-wedding |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240513185634/https://www.vogue.com/article/pusha-t-terrence-thornton-wears-dior-kim-jones-suit-wedding |archive-date=May 13, 2024 |access-date=June 30, 2025 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]}}</ref> On June 11, 2020, Thornton and Williams welcomed their first child, Nigel Brixx,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/286992-pusha-t-and-his-wife-welcome-their-first-son-news|title=Pusha T & His Wife Welcome Their First Son|work=[[HotNewHipHop ]]|last=A.|first=Aron|date=June 15, 2020|access-date=June 17, 2020|archive-date=October 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027182537/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/pusha-t-and-his-wife-welcome-their-first-son-news.112594.html|url-status=live}}</ref> whose middle name attracted online reaction for being an apparent slang term for [[cocaine]], a frequent topic of Thornton's music.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/288481-pusha-t-fans-react-to-his-sons-cocaine-referencing-middle-name-news|title=Pusha-T Fans React To His Son's Cocaine-Referencing Middle Name|work=HotNewHipHop|last=Zidel|first=Aled|date=June 16, 2020|access-date=June 17, 2020|archive-date=October 26, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201026191244/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/pusha-t-fans-react-to-his-sons-cocaine-referencing-middle-name-news.112661.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The couple had a second child in April 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 15, 2025 |title=Pusha T, Wife Virginia Williams Pregnant with Second Baby Together |url= https://www.tmz.com/2025/11/15/pusha-t-virginia-williams-pregnant-second-child/ |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20251115194143/https://www.tmz.com/2025/11/15/pusha-t-virginia-williams-pregnant-second-child/ |archive-date=November 15, 2025 |access-date=November 15, 2025 |website=[[TMZ]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bras Nevares |first=Gabriel |date=April 20, 2026 |title=Pusha T &amp; His Wife Virginia Williams Announce Birth Of Their Second Child |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/990424-pusha-t-wife-virginia-williams-birth-of-second-child |access-date=2026-04-24 |website=HotNewHipHop |language=en}}</ref>

Thornton's mother Mildred died in November 2021,<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Hip-Hop Community Sends Condolences to Pusha-T Following News of His Mother's Death |url=https://www.complex.com/music/pusha-t-announces-moms-death-receives-condolences-from-fellow-artists |access-date=2023-02-07 |website=Complex |language=en |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207043653/https://www.complex.com/music/pusha-t-announces-moms-death-receives-condolences-from-fellow-artists |url-status=live }}</ref> followed by his father, Gene Sr., four months later.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pusha-T, No Malice Remember Late Father With Touching Tributes |url=https://www.complex.com/music/pusha-t-no-malice-remember-late-father-touching-tributes |access-date=2023-02-07 |website=Complex |language=en |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207043655/https://www.complex.com/music/pusha-t-no-malice-remember-late-father-touching-tributes |url-status=live }}</ref>

