'''William Leonard Roberts II''' (born January 28, 1976), known professionally as '''Rick Ross''', is an American rapper and music executive.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://djbooth.net/features/2017-03-20-rick-ross-on-mmg-control-of-artists-publishing|title=Rick Ross on control of label artists|access-date=August 4, 2018|date=March 20, 2017|author=Zisook, Brian|newspaper=Djbooth|publisher=DJBooth.com|archive-date=February 25, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210225051158/https://djbooth.net/features/2017-03-20-rick-ross-on-mmg-control-of-artists-publishing|url-status=live}}</ref> Noted for his [[Wiktionary:booming|booming]] vocal presence and vivid lyrical imagery, Ross' music is built upon a [[rags to riches]] narrative.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://singersroom.com/w3/10-best-rick-ross-songs-of-all-time/|title=10 Best Rick Ross Songs of All Time - Singersroom.com|date=April 17, 2023}}</ref>{{Additional citation needed|date=September 2026}}

Prior to releasing his debut single, "[[Hustlin']]" in March 2006, Ross was the subject of a bidding war—receiving offers from [[Sean Combs|Diddy]]'s [[Bad Boy Records|Bad Boy Entertainment]] and [[Irv Gotti]]'s [[Murder Inc. Records|Murder Inc.]]—and ultimately signed a multimillion-dollar deal with [[Jay-Z]] on [[Def Jam Recordings]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1526040/jay-z-gets-behind-rick-ross-to-show-hes-still-in-the-rap-business/|title=Jay-Z Gets Behind Rick Ross To Show He's 'Still In The Rap Business'|website=MTV|access-date=July 15, 2018|archive-date=March 8, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308023508/http://www.mtv.com/news/1526040/jay-z-gets-behind-rick-ross-to-show-hes-still-in-the-rap-business/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://open.spotify.com/artist/1sBkRIssrMs1AbVkOJbc7a|title=Rick Ross|website=Spotify|access-date=July 15, 2018|archive-date=February 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210219084654/https://open.spotify.com/artist/1sBkRIssrMs1AbVkOJbc7a|url-status=live}}</ref> His debut studio album, ''[[Port of Miami (album)|Port of Miami]]'' (2006), peaked atop the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]],<ref name="auto">{{cite news|last=Harris|first=Chris|title=Rick Ross Sails Past Breaking Benjamin, Takes Port Of Miami To #1|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1538669/rick-ross-sails-past-breaking-benjamin-takes-port-of-miami-to-1/|work=MTV News|date=August 16, 2006|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080516223636/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1538669/20060816/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=May 16, 2008}}</ref><ref name="auto1">{{cite web|title=Port of Miami – Charts & Awards|url=https://www.allmusic.com/album/r847767|work=Allmusic|year=2006|access-date=September 21, 2021|archive-date=December 11, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111211083605/http://www.allmusic.com/album/r847767|url-status=live}}</ref> along with his second and third albums, ''[[Trilla]]'' (2008) and ''[[Deeper Than Rap]]'' (2009). His fourth album, ''[[Teflon Don (album)|Teflon Don]]'' (2010) spawned the single "[[Aston Martin Music]]" (featuring [[Drake (musician)|Drake]] and [[Chrisette Michele]])—which received [[Music recording certification|triple platinum]] certification by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] (RIAA)—while his fifth and sixth albums, ''[[God Forgives, I Don't]]'' (2012) and ''[[Mastermind (Rick Ross album)|Mastermind]]'' (2014), peaked the ''Billboard'' 200 twice more.<ref name="auto2">{{cite news|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|title=Rick Ross On #1 Trilla Beating Out Snoop, Fat Joe: 'I Told Them We Bossin' Up'|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1583694/rick-ross-on-1-trilla-beating-out-snoop-fat-joe-i-told-them-we-bossin-up/|publisher=MTV News|date=March 19, 2008|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080424083123/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1583694/20080319/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=April 24, 2008}}</ref>

Outside of his solo career, Ross was a lead member of the hip-hop group [[Triple C's]], alongside fellow Florida rappers [[Gunplay (rapper)|Gunplay]] and [[Torch (American rapper)|Torch]] in 2005. Ross founded the record label [[Maybach Music Group]] (MMG) in 2008, through which he has signed artists including [[Meek Mill]], [[Wale (rapper)|Wale]], [[French Montana]], and [[Omarion]], among others. MMG entered a [[joint venture]] with Def Jam and later [[Epic Records]] to release his subsequent albums: ''[[Hood Billionaire]]'' (2014), ''[[Black Market (Rick Ross album)|Black Market]]'' (2015), ''[[Rather You Than Me]]'' (2017), ''[[Port of Miami 2]]'' (2019), and ''[[Richer Than I Ever Been]]'' (2021). After which, it entered a joint venture with [[Gamma (media company)|Gamma]] to release his collaborative album, ''[[Too Good to Be True (Rick Ross and Meek Mill album)|Too Good to Be True]]'' (2023) with Meek Mill. Ross is the recipient of nine [[Grammy Award]] nominations, and has been named "Man of the Year" by hip-hop magazine ''[[The Source (magazine)|The Source]]'' in 2010 and 2011, as well as "[[Hottest MC in the Game]]" by [[MTV]] in the latter year.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.bet.com/article/bxad4i/rick-ross-covers-25th-anniversary-issue-of-the-source | title=Rick Ross Covers 25th Anniversary Issue of the Source | website=[[BET]] | access-date=September 8, 2023 | archive-date=September 8, 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230908010525/https://www.bet.com/article/bxad4i/rick-ross-covers-25th-anniversary-issue-of-the-source | url-status=live }}</ref>

==Early life==
William Leonard Roberts II was born on January 28, 1976, in [[Clarksdale, Mississippi]],<ref name="birth certificate">[http://www.thesmokinggun.com/file/rick-rosss-jail-time?page=17 Birth certificate] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150705194908/http://www.thesmokinggun.com/file/rick-rosss-jail-time?page=17 |date=July 5, 2015 }} of William Roberts (Rick Ross) as scanned by ''[[The Smoking Gun]]''</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Reid|first1=Shaheem|title=Rick Ross Talks Miami's 'Fast Life,' Southern Heritage, In The Source|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1640385/rick-ross-talks-miamis-fast-life-southern-heritage-in-the-source/|publisher=MTV News|access-date=June 24, 2015|date=May 28, 2010|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150726232415/http://www.mtv.com/news/1640385/rick-ross-talks-miamis-fast-life-southern-heritage-in-the-source/|archive-date=July 26, 2015}}</ref> and raised in [[Carol City, Florida]].<ref name="all music"/> After graduating from [[Miami Carol City Senior High School]], he attended the [[historically black college]] [[Albany State University]] on a football scholarship.<ref>{{cite web|last=Handelsman|first=Jason|title=Rick Ross Spins a New One|url=https://www.miaminewtimes.com/music/rick-ross-spins-a-new-one-6332783|work=Miami New Times|date=January 10, 2008|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080706194243/http://www.miaminewtimes.com/2008-01-10/music/rick-ross-spins-a-new-one/full|archive-date=July 6, 2008}}</ref> Roberts worked as a [[Prison officer|correctional officer]] for 18 months from December 1995, until his resignation in June 1997.<ref name="Rick Ross CO">{{cite news|title=Despite denials, records show rap star worked as corrections officer|url=http://www.thesmokinggun.com/documents/crime/screw-rick-ross|date=July 21, 2008|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121212134528/http://www.thesmokinggun.com/documents/crime/screw-rick-ross|archive-date=December 12, 2012}}</ref>

==Career==
===Career beginnings===
In his early years at Suave House Records, Roberts initially made his debut under the pseudonym Teflon Da Don. He made his recording debut on the song "Ain't Shhh to Discuss" on [[Erick Sermon]]'s lone album for [[DreamWorks Records|DreamWorks]], ''[[Def Squad Presents Erick Onasis]]''.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Burgess|first1=Omar|title=Rick Ross Recalls Drawing Inspiration From EPMD|url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.15174/title.rick-ross-recalls-drawing-inspiration-from-epmd|website=HipHopDX|date=May 18, 2011|publisher=Cheri Media Group|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141210213551/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.15174/title.rick-ross-recalls-drawing-inspiration-from-epmd|archive-date=December 10, 2014}}</ref> In the mid-2000s, he changed his name to Rick Ross. He derived his stage name from the former drug kingpin [["Freeway" Rick Ross]], to whom he has no connection.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/freeway-ricky-ross-taking-battle-430210/ | work=The Hollywood Reporter | first=Eriq | last=Gardner | title='Freeway' Ricky Ross Taking Battle Over Rapper Rick Ross to Appeals Court | date=March 21, 2013 | url-status=live | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131106011404/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/thr-esq/freeway-ricky-ross-taking-battle-430210|access-date=February 28, 2022|archive-date=November 6, 2013 | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

After being signed to [[Suave House Records]], former label for rap duo [[8Ball & MJG]], he eventually signed a deal with Slip-n-Slide Records, which has been under the Def Jam label since 2006. While signed to Slip-n-Slide, Roberts toured with fellow rapper [[Trick Daddy]] and made guest performances on other Slip-n-Slide albums.<ref name="all music">{{cite web|last=Cordor|first=Cyril|title=Rick Ross Biography|url=https://www.allmusic.com/artist/rick-ross-p736150/biography|work=Allmusic|access-date=April 1, 2011|quote=During the early to mid-2000s, he became popular and well known locally through touring with Trick Daddy and guest-appearing on a few Slip-N-Slide releases, but didn't release any solo material until 2006.|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110420141321/http://www.allmusic.com/artist/rick-ross-p736150/biography|archive-date=April 20, 2011}}</ref>

===2006–2008: ''Port of Miami'' and ''Trilla''===
{{Main|Port of Miami (album)|Trilla}}
His debut album ''[[Port of Miami (album)|Port of Miami]]'' was released in August 2006 and debuted at the top spot on the U.S. [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] album chart, with sales at 187,000 units during the first week.<ref name="auto"/><ref name="auto1"/> Christian Hoard of ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' magazine predicted that it would be "the summer's biggest rap record".<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Hoard|first=Christian|title=Rick Ross, Miami's Latest Hip-Hop Phenom|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/rick-ross-miamis-latest-hip-hop-phenom-243034/|magazine=Rolling Stone|date=June 1, 2006|access-date=February 28, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090402121857/http://www.rollingstone.com/news/story/10464338/a_primer_on_rick_ross_miamis_latest_hiphop_phenom|archive-date=April 2, 2009}}</ref> The second single was "Push It", which samples "[[Scarface (Push It to the Limit)]]", the theme song from the gangster film ''[[Scarface (1983 film)|Scarface]]''.<ref>{{cite news|last=Moss|first=Corey|title=Rick Ross Ready to 'Blow' Up When He Docks At Port Of Miami In August|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1536628/rick-ross-ready-to-blow-up-when-he-docks-at-port-of-miami-in-august/|publisher=MTV News|date=July 18, 2006|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080724140402/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1536628/20060718/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=July 24, 2008}}</ref> The music video for "Push It" was modeled after the film.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|title=A Newcomer 10 Years In The Making: Rick Ross Gets Help From Jay-Z, Akon On First LP|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1537187/a-newcomer-10-years-in-the-making-rick-ross-gets-help-from-jay-z-akon-on-first-lp/|publisher=MTV News|date=July 26, 2006|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080504183047/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1537187/20060726/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=May 4, 2008}}</ref> During that time, Ross made guest performances on two singles from [[DJ Khaled]]'s debut ''[[Listennn... the Album]]'': "Born-N-Raised" and "[[Holla at Me]]". ''Port of Miami'' received Gold certification from the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] on November 8, 2006.

In March 2008, his second album ''[[Trilla]]'' was released and, as its predecessor ''Port of Miami'' had, debuted at the top of the ''Billboard'' 200.<ref name="auto2"/> Its lead single "[[Speedin']]" featuring [[R. Kelly]] peaked at number 21 on the U.S. ''Billboard'' Bubbling Under Hot 100 Singles; the next one, "[[The Boss (Rick Ross song)|The Boss]]" featuring [[T-Pain]] peaked at number 17 on the Hot 100. The third single "[[Here I Am (Rick Ross song)|Here I Am]]" featured [[Nelly]] and [[Avery Storm]].<ref name="Tiger">{{cite news|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|title=Rick Ross Talks About Tiger Attack – Seriously! – On The Set Of His 'Here I Am' Video|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1587800/rick-ross-talks-about-tiger-attack-seriously-on-the-set-of-his-here-i-am-video/|publisher=MTV News|date=May 20, 2008|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080919225703/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1587800/20080520/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=September 19, 2008}}</ref> [[MTV News]] ranked Ross on the fourth spot in its 2008 "Hottest MCs in the Game" list among ten rappers.<ref>{{cite web|title='Hottest MCs in the Game': Rick Ross Bosses Up to #4|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1587657/hottest-mcs-in-the-game-rick-ross-bosses-up-to-4/|work=MTV News|date=May 16, 2008|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080726023007/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1587657/20080516/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=July 26, 2008}}</ref> The fourth single "This Is The Life" featured [[Trey Songz]] and was released in July.

===2009: ''Deeper Than Rap''===
{{Main|Deeper Than Rap|}}
<blockquote>I look at the game and the business and all different aspects, it's a lot of great lyricists on the corner that will never properly understand the business and know how to market themselves and get in a position where they can gain capital. I look at all the strategies people use and what made them successful. What made Birdman just as relevant today after selling 50 million records? That intrigues me. To see the class of Jay-Z, his accomplishments and see how he sits backs and accurately makes his moves.<ref>[http://www.mtv.com/news/1608776/rick-ross-is-competing-for-the-throne-with-deeper-than-rap/ Rick Ross Is 'Competing For The Throne' With Deeper Than Rap] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100110043142/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1608776/20090407/story.jhtml |date=January 10, 2010 }}, MTV. Retrieved February 28, 2022</ref></blockquote>

A track from the album called "Valley of Death" was what stood out to MTV reporters. In the song, Ross speaks briefly on his controversial stint as a prison guard. "Keep it trilla, nigga, never had a gun and badge," which he stresses, leaving the word that he was indeed an officer of the law. "Kept a nice watch, smoking on a hundred sack/ Back in the day I sold crack for some nice kicks/ Skippin' school, I saw my friend stabbed with an ice pick/ Can't criticize niggas trying to get jobs/ Better get smart, young brother, live yours." Later, he implies that while he was working as a corrections officer, he was on the streets. "Only lived once and I got two kids/ And for me to feed them, I'll get two gigs," he raps. "I'll shovel shit, I'll C.O./ So we can bow our heads and pray over the meatloaf." The accuracy of some of these lyrics were called into question by reporters.<ref>[http://www.mtv.com/news/1609204/rick-ross-justifies-prison-guard-past-in-new-track-listen-here/ Rick Ross Justifies Prison-Guard Past In New Track – Listen Here] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090417035051/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1609204/20090414/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml |date=April 17, 2009 }}, MTV. Retrieved February 28, 2022</ref><ref>[http://www.mtv.com/news/1610106/rick-ross-valley-of-death-was-intended-for-jay-z/ Rick Ross' 'Valley Of Death' Was Intended For Jay-Z] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090717120151/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1610106/20090427/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml |date=July 17, 2009 }}, MTV. Retrieved February 28, 2022</ref>

The cover of the May 2009 issue of [[XXL magazine|''XXL'' magazine]], titled "Rick Ross Up in Smoke", featured Ross wearing a pair of [[Louis Vuitton]] sunglasses. After publication, a spokesperson for the luxury-goods maker contacted ''XXL'' to inform them that Ross was sporting fake sunglasses in the cover image.<ref>{{cite news|last=Kaufman|first=Gil|title=Rick Ross Called Out For Fake Louis Vuitton Sunglasses On XXL Cover|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1611166/rick-ross-called-out-for-fake-louis-vuitton-sunglasses-on-xxl-cover/|publisher=MTV News|date=May 12, 2009|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090908110327/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1611166/20090512/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=September 8, 2009}}</ref> It was later revealed that the rapper was wearing authentic Louis Vuitton sunglasses which were altered by Jacob Bernstein, known as "The Sunglass Pimp". Bernstein defended his customizations despite Louis Vuitton's insistence that such modification and resale of trademarked property is not legal.<ref>{{cite web|author=J.R.|title=Rick Ross' Louis Vuitton Rumor Slammed By The 'Sunglass Pimp'|url=http://www.hiphoprx.com/2009/05/15/rick-ross-louis-vuitton-rumor-slammed-by-the-sunglass-pimp/|work=HipHopRX|date=May 15, 2009|access-date=November 6, 2009|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090518092054/http://www.hiphoprx.com/2009/05/15/rick-ross-louis-vuitton-rumor-slammed-by-the-sunglass-pimp|archive-date=May 18, 2009}}</ref>

===2010–2013: Prime Rick Ross===
Ross had a number of accomplishments in 2010. In May, he released the critically acclaimed ''Albert Anastasia EP''. In addition to that, he released his fourth album entitled ''Teflon Don'', on July 20, 2010. The lead single was "[[Super High]]" featuring [[Ne-Yo]]. Actress [[Stacey Dash]] made an appearance in the music video. A second official single was released titled "Live Fast, Die Young" which features and is produced by [[Kanye West]]. The album debuted at number two on the ''Billboard'' 200 selling 176,000 copies in its first week.

In November 2010, rapper and frequent collaborator, [[Sean Combs|Diddy]], who in 2009 signed Ross to a management deal with his [[Ciroc Entertainment]], announced from his YouTube account, that he and Ross are working on an EP together. A single was released for the project, called "Another One".<ref>{{cite news |last= Roberts II |first= William L. |title= Bugatti Boyz (Diddy & Rick Ross) – Another One |url= https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dv24FRQ6JvI |date= October 12, 2010 |access-date= October 17, 2010 |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130212113815/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dv24FRQ6JvI |archive-date= February 12, 2013 |df= mdy-all }}</ref> Ross ended 2010 by releasing another mixtape, ''Ashes To Ashes'' on Christmas Eve 2010.

[[File:Rick Ross in 2011.jpg|thumb|right|200px|Rick Ross performing in 2011]]
In early 2011, Rick Ross signed Wale and Meek Mill to his Maybach Music Group imprint. The group released their debut compilation album, ''[[Self Made Vol. 1]]'' in May. Rick Ross performed at [[BET Awards 2011]], which aired on June 26, 2011.<ref>{{cite news|title=Rick Ross Performing at BET Awards 2011 |url=http://www.bet.com/shows/bet-awards/2011/performers/rick-ross.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110630151313/http://www.bet.com/shows/bet-awards/2011/performers/rick-ross.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=June 30, 2011 }}</ref> He also received a nomination for Best Male Hip Hop Artist.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bet.com/shows/bet-awards/2011/nominees/best-male-hip-hop-artist.html |title=Best Male Hip Hop Artist |publisher=BET |access-date=November 21, 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111115180804/http://www.bet.com/shows/bet-awards/2011/nominees/best-male-hip-hop-artist.html |archive-date=November 15, 2011  }}</ref> In early 2012, Ross was named the [[MTV's Annual Hottest MC List#2011|Hottest MC in the Game]].<ref name="rapradar.com">{{cite web |url=http://rapradar.com/2012/02/19/rick-ross-1-hottest-mc-in-the-game/ |title=Rick Ross: #1 Hottest MC in the Game |publisher=Rap Radar |date=February 19, 2012 |access-date=March 30, 2012 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120224032337/http://rapradar.com/2012/02/19/rick-ross-1-hottest-mc-in-the-game/ |archive-date=February 24, 2012  }}</ref>

Big things were happening for Ross's successful career in 2012 as well. He started the year by premiering his ''[[Rich Forever]]'' mixtape to universal acclaim. Ross's MMG camp released their second compilation, [[Self Made Vol. 2]] in June 2012. Less than a month later, Ross's fifth album, ''[[God Forgives, I Don't]]'', was released on July 31, 2012, after a few delays.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rap-up.com/2011/01/04/new-music-rick-ross-f-drake-made-men/ |title=New Music: Rick Ross f/ Drake – 'Made Men' |publisher=Rap-Up.com |access-date=March 2, 2011 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110816165741/http://www.rap-up.com/2011/01/04/new-music-rick-ross-f-drake-made-men |archive-date=August 16, 2011  }}</ref><ref name="hiphopdx.com">{{cite web |last=Jacobs |first=Allen |url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.17650/title.rick-ross-officially-delays-god-forgives-i-dont-to-2012|title=Rick Ross Officially Delays "God Forgives, I Don't" To 2012  |publisher=HipHop DX |date=November 17, 2011 |access-date=February 28, 2022 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120321190958/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.17650/title.rick-ross-officially-delays-god-forgives-i-dont-to-2012 |archive-date=March 21, 2012  }}</ref> Upon its release, the album charted at number eight on the UK Albums Chart and number two on the UK R&B Albums Chart, making it Ross's highest-charting album and first top 10 album in the United Kingdom. The album debuted at number one on the ''Billboard'' 200, with first-week sales of 218,000 copies.<ref>{{cite magazine |url= https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/rick-ross-god-forgives-i-dont-selling-big-heading-for-no-1-480659/ |title= Rick Ross' 'God Forgives, I Don't' Selling Big, Heading for No. 1 |magazine= Billboard |date= August 1, 2012 |access-date=February 28, 2022 |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140917153511/http://www.billboard.com/articles/news/480659/rick-ross-god-forgives-i-dont-selling-big-heading-for-no-1 |archive-date= September 17, 2014 |df= mdy-all }}</ref>

Ross premiered four songs: "So Sophisticated" featuring [[Meek Mill]], "[[Touch'N You]]" featuring [[Usher (entertainer)|Usher]], "Hold Me Back", and "3 Kings" featuring [[Dr. Dre]] and [[Jay-Z]]. The album was certified gold in less than two months. To celebrate his accomplishment and promote his upcoming MMG Tour, Ross released a mixtape, ''The Black Bar Mitzvah'' in October. Rick Ross was nominated by ''[[The Source (magazine)|The Source]]'' as its "Man of the Year".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1653228/rick-ross-named-the-sources-man-of-the-year/|title=Rick Ross Named The Source's Man Of The Year|work=MTV News|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110103061249/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1653228/20101130/ross__rick__rap_.jhtml|archive-date=January 3, 2011}}</ref>

On January 7, 2013, Ross revealed the title of his sixth album to be ''[[Mastermind (Rick Ross album)|Mastermind]]''. The album was set to be released in 2013.<ref>{{cite news|last=Horowitz|first=Steven|title=Rick Ross Announces Title Of Sixth Studio Album|url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.22454/title.rick-ross-announces-title-of-sixth-studio-album|access-date=February 28, 2022|newspaper=HipHopDX|date=January 7, 2013|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130110023455/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.22454/title.rick-ross-announces-title-of-sixth-studio-album|archive-date=January 10, 2013}}</ref> The first promotional single from the album would come in the form of "Box Chevy" which was released to iTunes on February 15, 2013.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://consequence.net/2013/01/new-music-rick-ross-box-chevy/ |title=New Music: Rick Ross – "Box Chevy"|publisher=Consequence |date=January 25, 2013 |access-date=February 28, 2022 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130721020417/http://consequenceofsound.net/2013/01/new-music-rick-ross-box-chevy/ |archive-date=July 21, 2013  }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.djbooth.net/index/tracks/review/rick-ross-box-chevy/ |title=Rick Ross – Box Chevy &#124; Listen & Download |newspaper=Djbooth |publisher=Djbooth.net |access-date=August 6, 2013 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131014004222/http://www.djbooth.net/index/tracks/review/rick-ross-box-chevy/ |archive-date=October 14, 2013  }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/box-chevy-explicit-version/603730029 |title=iTunes – Music – Box Chevy – Single by Rick Ross |publisher=iTunes |date=February 15, 2013 |access-date=August 6, 2013 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131024093333/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/box-chevy-explicit-version/id603730029 |archive-date=October 24, 2013  }}</ref> The music video was filmed on April 1, 2013, and features cameos from MMG members [[Gunplay (rapper)|Gunplay]], [[Stalley]] and [[Rockie Fresh]].<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/04/watch-behind-the-scenes-footage-of-rick-rosss-box-chevy-video-shoot |title=Watch Behind the Scenes Footage of Rick Ross' "Box Chevy" Video Shoot |magazine=Complex |date=April 1, 2013 |access-date=August 6, 2013 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131106181104/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/04/watch-behind-the-scenes-footage-of-rick-rosss-box-chevy-video-shoot |archive-date=November 6, 2013  }}</ref>

During 2008, it was announced that Ross was set to release a collaboration album with his longtime friend [[Birdman (rapper)|Birdman]] titled ''The H'', but after that, no information on the album was released.<ref>{{cite web|last=Reid|first=Shaheem|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1588030/ti-producing-tracks-for-rick-ross-next-lp-tha-dogg-pound-get-gangsta-grizz-ills-in-mixtape-monday/|title=T.I. Tests Himself As A Producer|work=Mixtape Monday|publisher=MTV News|date=May 23, 2008|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100427132103/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1588030/20080523/t_i_.jhtml|archive-date=April 27, 2010}}</ref> On May 16, 2013, Birdman and Rick Ross announced that ''The H'' would be released as a mixtape hosted by [[DJ Khaled]] on May 23, 2013. The project was recorded during a couple of days in 2008.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/birdman-to-drop-lost-album-with-rick-ross-h-on-may-23/ |title=Birdman And Rick Ross To Release Lost Album, 'The H' On May 23|publisher=XXL |date=May 16, 2013 |access-date=February 28, 2022 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130615134943/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2013/05/birdman-to-drop-lost-album-with-rick-ross-h-on-may-23/ |archive-date=June 15, 2013  }}</ref>

On September 5, 2013, Ross premiered the first official single from ''Mastermind'', "No Games" featuring [[Future (rapper)|Future]] produced by [[J.U.S.T.I.C.E. League]].<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/rick-ross-ft-future-no-games |title=Listen: Rick Ross f/ Future "No Games" |magazine=Complex |access-date=March 15, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131106181107/http://www.complex.com/music/2013/09/rick-ross-ft-future-no-games |archive-date=November 6, 2013  }}</ref> The following day, it was released to [[Urban contemporary]] radio.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://frequencynews.com/musiclibrary_detail.cfm?LabelID%3D6%26ArtistID%3D54%26AlbumID%3D1067%26SingleID%3D3703 |title=Frequency News |access-date=October 29, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131013182805/http://frequencynews.com/musiclibrary_detail.cfm?LabelID=6&ArtistID=54&AlbumID=1067&SingleID=3703 |archive-date=October 13, 2013 }}</ref> It was followed by the singles "[[The Devil Is A Lie]]" with Jay-Z and "War Ready" with Jeezy.

===2014: ''Mastermind'' and ''Hood Billionaire''===
Rick Ross released his sixth album, ''Mastermind'', in March 2014, the album received generally positive reviews from critics and debuted at number one on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] chart with first week sales of 179,000 copies.<ref>{{cite web |last=Tardio |first=Andres |url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.27864/title.hip-hop-album-sales-week-ending-392014 |title=Hip Hop Album Sales: Week Ending 3/9/2014 |publisher=HipHop DX |date=April 16, 2014 |access-date=February 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140313001022/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.27864/title.hip-hop-album-sales-week-ending-3-9-2014 |archive-date=March 13, 2014  }}</ref> As of April 8, 2014, the album has sold over 290,000 copies in the United States.<ref>{{cite web |last=Tardio |first=Andres |url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/id.28225/title.hip-hop-album-sales-week-ending-04062014 |title=Hip Hop Album Sales: Week Ending 04/6/2014 |publisher=HipHop DX |date=April 16, 2014 |access-date=February 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140412115143/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.28225/title.hip-hop-album-sales-week-ending-04-06-2014 |archive-date=April 12, 2014  }}</ref> In June 2014, it was announced that Ross would make a guest appearance on the docu-series ''[[Sisterhood of Hip Hop]]''.<ref>{{cite web|last=Corry|first=Kristin|title='Sisterhood of Hip Hop' to Debut on Oxygen This Summer|url=https://www.vibe.com/news/movies-tv/sisterhood-of-hip-hop-debut-oxygen-summer-224519/|work=[[Vibe (magazine)|Vibe]]|publisher=Vibe.com|date=June 9, 2014|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140614220039/http://www.vibe.com/article/sisterhood-of-hip-hop-debut-oxygen-summer|archive-date=June 14, 2014}}</ref>

In October 2014, Ross announced he would be releasing another album, ''Hood Billionaire'', in order to make up for Meek Mill's ''[[Dreams Worth More Than Money]]'' album being shelved due to his incarceration.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1952362/rick-ross-jay-z-feature-hood-billionaire-album/|title=Rick Ross Promises Another Jay Z Feature On His Hood Billionaire Album|first=Nadeska|last=Alexis|publisher=MTV|date=October 3, 2014|access-date=October 6, 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006050417/http://www.mtv.com/news/1952362/rick-ross-jay-z-feature-hood-billionaire-album/|archive-date=October 6, 2014}}</ref> Rick Ross revealed cover art for ''[[Hood Billionaire]]'' in October 2014 and announced the release date to be set for November 24, 2014.<ref>{{cite tweet|user=RickRoss|author=Yung Rénzél 🏁|number=522486992127655937|date=October 15, 2014|title=It's Time!!! #HOODBILLIONAIRE}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Lilah|first1=Rose|title=Rick Ross Reveals Cover Art For "Hood Billionaire"|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/26867-rick-ross-reveals-cover-art-for-hood-billionaire-update-tracklist-with-features-revealed-news|website=HotNewHipHop|access-date=February 28, 2022|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141016210947/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/rick-ross-reveals-cover-art-for-hood-billionaire-news.12472.html|archive-date=October 16, 2014}}</ref> The album received mixed reviews, from fans and critics alike.{{citation needed|date=January 2016}}

===2015–2020: Renzel and Epic Records===
Ross had a mix of both adversity and accomplishments in 2015. Ross was arrested for kidnapping and assault charges and was sentenced to house arrest in his Georgia home. Ross joined the [[Snapchat]] platform and began referring to himself as "Renzel", a play on the name of famous actor [[Denzel Washington]]. In September, Ross released a mixtape, ''Black Dollar'', his third full-length release since March 2014. He soon announced his eighth album, ''[[Black Market (Rick Ross album)|Black Market]]'', for a December 2015 release. To promote his upcoming release, Ross released a remix tape, ''Renzel Remixes'' on Thanksgiving 2015. ''Black Market'' was released on December 4, 2015, and it served as Ross's final album for his original contract with Def Jam. The album's song "Free Enterprise" stirred up controversy because the lyrics included the line "Assassinate Trump like I'm Zimmerman" referring to [[Donald Trump]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://hollywoodlife.com/2015/12/04/rick-ross-assassinate-donald-trump-song-listen-free-enterprise/|title=Rick Ross Raps About 'Assassinating' Donald Trump In Scary New Song — Listen|website=Hollywood Life|date=December 4, 2015|access-date=March 15, 2016|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315101413/https://hollywoodlife.com/2015/12/04/rick-ross-assassinate-donald-trump-song-listen-free-enterprise/|archive-date=March 15, 2016 |last1=Mink |first1=Casey }}</ref>

In January 2016 after much speculation, Rick Ross signed a solo deal with [[Epic Records]], reuniting him with [[L.A. Reid]], who originally played a major role in signing Ross to Def Jam.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Hernandez |first=Victoria |url=http://hiphopdx.com/news/id.37059/title.rick-ross-signs-with-epic-records |title=Rick Ross Signs With Epic Records |magazine=HipHopDX |date=January 15, 2016 |access-date=March 15, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170309014317/http://hiphopdx.com/news/id.37059/title.rick-ross-signs-with-epic-records |archive-date=March 9, 2017  }}</ref>

On February 21, 2016, Rick Ross remixed Kanye West's "Famous".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.spin.com/2016/02/rick-ross-kanye-west-famous-remix/|title=Rick Ross remixes Famous|last=Unterberger|first=Andrew|work=[[Spin (magazine)|Spin]]|date=February 21, 2016|access-date=February 21, 2016|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160222091840/http://www.spin.com/2016/02/rick-ross-kanye-west-famous-remix/|archive-date=February 22, 2016}}</ref> Ross was active in 2016, releasing many songs including "Make It Work" off of Self Made Vol. 4, "[[Purple Lamborghini]]" (with [[Skrillex]]) of ''[[Suicide Squad: The Album]]'', "Supa Cindy", and "Freaky Hoe".

He announced that his ninth album, ''[[Rather You Than Me]]'', was slated for a March 2017 release. The album debuted and peaked number three on the [[Billboard 200|'' Billboard'' 200]].<ref>{{cite tweet|user=MaybachMusic|author=Maybach Music Group|number=800512100141412352|date=November 21, 2016|title=Have you heard the title of @rickyrozay new album coming early 2017? #rytm Rather You Than Me.}}</ref> On February 2, 2018, Rick Ross released "Florida Boy" with T-Pain and Kodak Black as the first single from the album, which was slated for a 2018 release. On June 22, 2018, Ross released "Green Gucci Suit" with Future as the second single from his tenth album ''[[Port of Miami 2]]'', released on August 9, 2019. The album debuted at number two on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]].<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/slipknot-third-no-1-album-billboard-200/|title=Slipknot Ties Up Third No. 1 Album on Billboard 200 Chart With 'We Are Not Your Kind'|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|last=Caulfield|first=Keith|date=August 18, 2019|access-date=February 28, 2022|archive-date=February 28, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220228073653/https://www.billboard.com/pro/slipknot-third-no-1-album-billboard-200/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The album included the single "[[Gold Roses]]" with [[Drake (musician)|Drake]], which was nominated for a Grammy Award for [[Grammy Award for Best Rap Song|Best Rap Song]] at the [[62nd Annual Grammy Awards]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2019/11/20/grammy-award-nominations|title=Here are all the nominations for the 2020 Grammy Awards|work=[[The Fader]]|access-date=July 19, 2020|archive-date=September 23, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200923105053/https://www.thefader.com/2019/11/20/grammy-award-nominations|url-status=live}}</ref>
In July 2020, Ross teamed up with singer [[August Alsina]] for a track titled "Entanglements", a [[diss track]] towards Alsina's ex-lover [[Jada Pinkett Smith]].<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/august-alsina-entanglements-9420661|title=August Alsina Defines His 'Entanglements' to Jada Pinkett Smith on New Record With Rick Ross|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=July 19, 2020|access-date=July 19, 2020|archive-date=July 19, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200719114230/https://www.billboard.com/amp/articles/columns/hip-hop/9420661/august-alsina-entanglements|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2017, Ross was criticized for stating in an interview that he never signed a female rapper because "I would end up fucking [her] and fucking the business up."<ref name=mistake>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/rick-ross-insensitive-comments-about-female-rappers-were-mistake-195084/|title=Rick Ross: 'Insensitive' Comments About Female Rappers Were 'Mistake'|last1=Reed|first1=Ryan|date=July 28, 2017|magazine=Rolling Stone|access-date=May 2, 2019|archive-date=April 25, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190425181334/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/rick-ross-insensitive-comments-about-female-rappers-were-mistake-195084/|url-status=live}}</ref> Ross quickly apologized through a Facebook post where he called his comments "insensitive" and a "mistake [he regrets]."<ref name=mistake />
[[File:Rick Ross at NYL Halftime.jpg|alt=Rick Ross performs with Ellie the Elephant and the Torch Patrol at halftime of a New York Liberty Game during the 2024 WNBA Playoffs. |right|thumb|Rick Ross performs with [[Ellie the Elephant]] and the Torch Patrol at halftime of a [[New York Liberty]] Game during the 2024 [[Women's National Basketball Association|WNBA]] Playoffs. ]]
In August 2020, Ross released the single "Pinned to the Cross", featuring Finn Matthews, following his ''[[Verzuz]]'' battle with [[2 Chainz]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/pinned-to-the-cross-feat-finn-matthews-single/1525731306|title=Pinned to the Cross (feat. Finn Matthews) – Single by Rick Ross|via=[[Apple Music]]|access-date=August 7, 2020|archive-date=August 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200824230632/https://music.apple.com/us/album/pinned-to-the-cross-feat-finn-matthews-single/1525731306|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2021–present: ''Richer Than I Ever Been''===
On August 26, 2020, during an interview with [[Hot 97]], Rick Ross announced his eleventh album, ''[[Richer Than I Ever Been]]'', which was released on December 10, 2021.<ref>{{cite news|title=Rick Ross Announces New Album 'Richer Than I've Ever Been'|url=https://www.rap-up.com/2020/08/26/rick-ross-announces-new-album-richer-than-ive-ever-been/|date=August 26, 2020|access-date=August 2, 2021|website=Rap-Up|archive-date=June 13, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210613144411/https://www.rap-up.com/2020/08/26/rick-ross-announces-new-album-richer-than-ive-ever-been/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On September 7, 2021, Ross' book ''The Perfect Day to Boss Up'' debuted at number 5 on ''The New York Times''{{'}} weekly bestsellers list.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Chan|first=Tim|title=Boss Moves: Rick Ross' New Book Debuts on Multiple Bestsellers Lists|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/rick-ross-book-boss-up-review-9632799/|date=September 20, 2021|access-date=February 28, 2022|magazine=Billboard|archive-date=February 28, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220228073656/https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/rick-ross-book-boss-up-review-9632799/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In January 2025, Ross garnered criticism for, alongside [[Soulja Boy]] and [[Snoop Dogg]], performing at a Trump-sponsored [[cryptocurrency]] event preceding Trump's [[second inauguration of Donald Trump|second inauguration]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jenn |first1=London |title=Fans Drag Snoop Dogg & Rick Ross Over "Crypto Ball" In Honor Of Donald Trump |url=https://allhiphop.com/news/snoop-dogg-rick-ross-backlash-crypto-ball-donald-trump/ |website=All HipHop |access-date=February 11, 2025 |date=January 18, 2025 |archive-date=January 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250120015241/https://allhiphop.com/news/snoop-dogg-rick-ross-backlash-crypto-ball-donald-trump/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Bennett |first1=Jessica |title=Snoop Dogg Loses Over 500K Followers After Performing At Trump's Crypto Ball |url=https://www.vibe.com/news/entertainment/snoop-dogg-loses-500k-followers-trump-crypto-ball-performance-1234984258/ |website=Vibe |access-date=February 10, 2025 |date=January 28, 2025 |archive-date=February 9, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250209154501/https://www.vibe.com/news/entertainment/snoop-dogg-loses-500k-followers-trump-crypto-ball-performance-1234984258/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

