'''Karon Malcolm Vantrees''' (born February 28, 1995), known professionally as '''Veeze''', is an American rapper and singer.<ref>{{cite magazine|url =https://www.xxlmag.com/veeze-interivew-the-break/|last=Kemet|first=High|title =The Break Presents– Veeze|date=June 16, 2023|magazine = XXL Magazine|access-date =January 18, 2024|archive-date = September 22, 2023|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20230922182951/https://www.xxlmag.com/veeze-interivew-the-break/|url-status = live}}</ref> He pursued a recording career in 2017 and released his debut single, "Itself," in 2019. His debut mixtape, ''Navy Wavy'', was released in September of that year. He signed with [[Warner Records]] to release his debut studio album, ''[[Ganger (album)|Ganger]]'' (2023), which peaked at number 97 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]].<ref>{{cite magazine|url =https://www.billboard.com/lists/best-rap-albums-2023/|last1=Damien|first1=Scott|last2=Gail|first2=Mitchell|last3=Heran|first3=Mamo|last4=Carl|first4=Lamarre|last5=Kyle-Brandon|first5=Denis|title=The 20 Best Rap Albums of 2023: Staff Picks|date=December 22, 2023|magazine = Billboard|access-date =January 18, 2024|archive-date = December 29, 2023|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20231229225930/https://www.billboard.com/lists/best-rap-albums-2023/|url-status = live}}</ref> It was supported by the single "GOMD" (remixed featuring [[Lil Uzi Vert]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2228487/stream-detroit-rapper-veezes-new-album-ganger-feat-lil-uzi-vert-lil-yachty-babyface-ray-more/music/|title=Veeze Ganger ft Lil Yachty and More|last=Tom|first=Breihan|website=[[Stereogum]]|date=June 27, 2023|access-date=February 7, 2024}}</ref>

His 2024 single, "[[Sorry Not Sorry (Lil Yachty and Veeze song)|Sorry Not Sorry]]" (with [[Lil Yachty]]), marked his first entry on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]].

== Early life ==
Karon Vantrees, was born in [[Detroit]], Michigan.<ref>{{cite web |last=David |first=Crone |title=Veeze Biography, Albums, Reviews|url=https://www.allmusic.com/artist/veeze-mn0003944557 |publisher=[[AllMusic]]|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> He spent his formative years on the city's [[List of neighborhoods in Detroit|West Side]], specifically on [[7 Mile Road|Seven Mile Road]].<ref>44, Bars. [https://44bars.com/who-is-veeze-where-is-he-from-details/ "Who Is Veeze? Where Is He From? Details"], 44Bars, Accessed January 20, 2024.</ref> Before pursuing a career in music, Veeze harbored aspirations of becoming a professional basketball player, specifically an [[NBA]] guard.<ref>[https://hmc.chartmetric.com/tomorrows-five-that-mexican-ot-anjimile-helena-deland-veeze-saya-gray/ "Rapper Veeze's Transition from Detroit local to Chart Topping Artist"], Chart Metric, June 15, 2023. Accessed January 20, 2024. "Detroit native Veeze's popularity is a result of his whimsical storytelling and slick wordplay, but his approach is far more jocular than his peers."</ref> However, he eventually redirected his focus towards music.<ref>[https://youtube/Yx71gtNeDEo?si=M-hNxk--ogaHKL-L] {{dead link|date=December 2025}}</ref> Veeze has openly discussed his past experiences with substance abuse, including involvement with [[Lean (drug)|lean]] and other [[drug]]s.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://hiphopcanada.com/no-jumper-veeze-interview/|title=The Veeze Interview: The Streets, Babyface Ray, drinking lean, Pooh Sheisty, Lil Yachty & more|last=DJ|first=Bluetec|date=February 22, 2022|website=[[HipHopCanada]]|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> These struggles, which occurred during his youth, have been referenced in his music.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.clashmusic.com/features/word-of-mouth-success-takes-a-shot-at-veeze/|title=Word of Mouth: Success Takes A Shot At Veeze |last=Grant|first=Brydon|date=June 12, 2024|magazine=[[Clash (magazine)|Clash]]|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> Veeze's musical style is heavily influenced by the Detroit hip-hop scene. He has cited various prominent Detroit rappers as inspirations.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ktt2.com/veeze-interview-on-no-jumper-32513519|title=Veeze interview on No Jumper|last=Slice|first=B|date=February 16, 2022|website=KTT|publisher=[[No Jumper]]|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref>

Veeze's upbringing instilled a strong hustle mentality, prompting him to sell apples and pears to make ends meet.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/veeze-detroit-rap-new-music-1234983155/|title=Veeze's Greatness Speaks for Itself — One Croaky Bar at a Time|last=Andre|first=Gee|date=March 21, 2024|magazine=Rolling Stone|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> His musical tastes were shaped by legendary artists such as [[Tupac Shakur]], [[DMX]], [[Jay-Z]], [[Lil Wayne]], [[Cam'ron]], [[Nate Dogg]], [[Bun B]], [[Scarface (rapper)|Scarface]], [[Jadakiss]], and [[Busta Rhymes]].<ref>{{cite magazine|url =https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/detroit-veeze-interview-new-album-1234733662/|last=Andre|first=Gee |title =Detroit's Veeze Is Back To Rapping Like There's Nothing To Lose|date=May 12, 2023|magazine = [[Rolling Stone]]|access-date = January 18, 2024|archive-date = May 23, 2023|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20230523224827/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/detroit-veeze-interview-new-album-1234733662/amp/|url-status = live}}</ref>

== Career ==
===2017–2022: Early works===
Veeze began his music career in the Detroit rap scene. He started rapping in summer 2019, drawing inspiration from local Detroit groups such as Street Lord'z, Doughboyz Cashout, and Team Eastside.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Brandon |first1=Callender |title=Veeze, an All-Star off the bench|url=https://www.thefader.com/2023/06/29/cover-story-veeze-an-all-star-off-the-bench/amp|website=The Fader|access-date=18 January 2024 |archive-date=12 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231212195433/https://www.thefader.com/2023/06/29/cover-story-veeze-an-all-star-off-the-bench/amp|url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Top 21 Detroit Rappers: 2023's Best Rappers from Detroit|url=https://www.kulturevulturez.com/top-detroit-rappers/|website=Kulture Vulturez|access-date=18 January 2024 |archive-date=25 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230625233550/https://www.kulturevulturez.com/top-detroit-rappers/|url-status=live }}</ref> Veeze released his debut single, "Itself", on May 22, 2019. The song, also his first commercial release, featured guest appearances from Wtm Scoob and DT. The song was produced by Top$ide. Additionally, "Itself" garnered moderate attention upon its release.<ref>{{cite web|url =https://www.metrotimes.com/music/the-unflappable-rapper-veeze-talks-detroit-his-ganger-tour-and-new-partnership-with-warner-records-34618252|last=Kahn|first=Davision|title=The unflappable rapper Veeze talks Detroit, his Ganger Tour, and new partnership with Warner Records|date=November 15, 2023|website = [[Metro Times]]|access-date =January 18, 2024|archive-date = November 22, 2023|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20231122121615/https://www.metrotimes.com/music/the-unflappable-rapper-veeze-talks-detroit-his-ganger-tour-and-new-partnership-with-warner-records-34618252|url-status = live}}</ref> Two days following the release of "Itself", Veeze released "Rusty" on May 24, 2019. "Rusty" was produced by Tye Beats. It did not chart on ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]''.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.fakeshoredrive.com/2019/05/veeze-drops-good-heat-in-rusty-video.html/|title=Veeze drops good heat "Rusty"|work=Fake Shore|last=Taylor|first=Greer|date=May 26, 2019|access-date=January 18, 2024}}</ref> Veeze's "Rusty" gained local attention, attracting the notice of [[Babyface Ray]], who subsequently supported Veeze by funding his debut [[music video]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Aidan C |first1=Werder |title=Detroit's Babyface Ray taps fellow hometown favorite Veeze for 'Spending Spree'|date=31 May 2022|url=https://ourgenerationmusic.com/news/detroits-babyface-ray-taps-fellow-hometown-favorite-veeze-for-spending-spree/|website=Generation Music|access-date=19 January 2024 |archive-date=1 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601181122/https://ourgenerationmusic.com/news/detroits-babyface-ray-taps-fellow-hometown-favorite-veeze-for-spending-spree/|url-status=live }}</ref>

Veeze released his debut [[mixtape]], ''Navy Wavy'', on September 27, 2019, through [[Warner Records]]. It included the previously released singles "Itself", "Rusty", and "Heart Insurance". The mixtape featured guest appearances from Babyface Ray, Peezy, and Beno.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.fakeshoredrive.com/2019/10/veeze-drops-navy-wavy-mixtape-featuring-babyface-ray-peezy-and-beno.html/|title=Veeze drops "Navy Wavy" mixtape featuring Babyface Ray, Peezy, and Beno|work=Fake Shore|last=Taylor|first=Greer|date=October 11, 2019|access-date=January 18, 2024}}</ref> Veeze released the single "Law & Order" [[Independent music|independently]] on September 18, 2020.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/thepitch/veeze-law-n-order-new-song-listen/|title=The Ones: Veeze's "Law N Order"|last=Alphonse|first=Pierre|date=September 8, 2020|website=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]|accessdate=January 18, 2024}}</ref> Additionally, Veeze was featured on Nino Number 9's "The Ninth (Remix)", released independently on December 21, 2020.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://music.apple.com/ng/album/the-ninth-remix-single/1546524892|title=Nino Number 9 releases The Ninth (Remix) featuring Veeze|last=Lay|first=PH|date=December 22, 2020|website=M.A|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> Veeze was featured on RoadRunna Wite's "OFF WHITE" on January 3, 2021. He released the single "A and W" on March 11, 2021.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://crackmagazine.net/article/profiles/veeze-issue-155-interview/|title=Veeze: All gas, no breaks|last=Thomas|first=Hobbs|date=March 29, 2024|magazine=[[Crack (magazine)|Crack]]|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> Veeze was featured on Babyface Ray's "Gallery Dept" on May 13, 2021. It was released through [[Empire Distribution]] and produced by Rocaine.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thereelgoats.com/project/babyface-ray-gallery-dept-ft-veeze/|title=BABYFACE RAY "GALLERY DEPT" FT VEEZE|last=Real|first=Mack|date=May 14, 2021|website=Reel Goats|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref>

Veeze released singles: "Free Shiest" on August 16, 2021, "Choppas in Hawaii" on August 20, 2021, "Kurt Angle" on November 5, 2021, "Let It Fly" on March 20, 2022,<ref>{{cite web|author=Alphonse Pierre|url=https://pitchfork.com/thepitch/veeze-let-it-fly-new-song-listen/|title=Listen to Veeze's "Let It Fly": The Ones|website=Pitchfork|date=March 14, 2022|access-date=April 13, 2025}}</ref> and "Talk Prices" in Apr 2022. On June 17, 2022, Veeze featured on [[Lil Baby]]'s track "[[U-Digg]]", alongside [[42 Dugg]], accompanied by an official music video.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://hypebeast.com/2022/6/lil-baby-new-track-video-u-digg-42-dugg-veeze|title=Lil Baby Serves Up New Track and Video "U-Digg" With 42 Dugg and Veeze|last=Dylan|first=Kelly|date=June 17, 2022|website=[[Hypebeast (company)|Hypebeast]]|accessdate=January 18, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Grant |first1=Shawn |title=Lil Baby Teams with 42 Dugg and Veeze for New Single "U-Digg"|access-date=January 19, 2024 |url=https://thesource.com/2022/06/17/lil-baby-teams-with-42-dugg-and-veeze-for-new-single-u-digg/ |website=[[The Source (magazine)|The Source]] |date=June 17, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Powell |first1=Jon |title=Lil Baby recruits 42 Dugg and Veeze for "U-Digg"|access-date=January 19, 2024 |url=https://www.revolt.tv/article/2022-06-20/175352/lil-baby-recruits-42-dugg-and-veeze-for-u-digg/ |website=[[Revolt (TV network)|Revolt]] |date=June 20, 2022}}</ref> "U-Digg" debuted at number 52 on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]], marking Veeze's first appearance on the chart.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/veeze-hot-100-first-timers-lil-baby-42-dugg-u-digg-1235107316/|first=Xander|last=Zellner|title = Hot 100 First-Timers: Veeze Arrives With Lil Baby & 42 Dugg Collab 'U-Digg'|date=June 27, 2023|magazine=Billboard|access-date = January 18, 2024}}</ref>

===2023–present: Rise to mainstream popularity with ''Ganger''===
{{Main|Ganger (album)}}
Veeze released his first single, "4 Kobe", on January 3, 2023. That same year, he gained widespread recognition with the single "GOMD" after going viral on [[social media]], followed by a remix featuring Lil Uzi Vert.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2023/05/10/lil-uzi-vert-veeze-gomd-remix/amp|title=Lil Uzi Vert and Veeze are equally above it all on "GOMD" remix|last=Raphael|first=Helfand|date=May 10, 2023|website=[[The Fader]]|accessdate=January 18, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Admad |first1=David |title=Veeze Enlists Lil Uzi Vert For "GOMD (Remix)"|url=https://www.rap-up.com/2023/05/13/veeze-partners-up-with-lil-uzi-vert-on-gomd-remix/?amp|date=13 May 2023|access-date=19 January 2024 |archive-date=}}</ref> The music video was also released. The video was directed by Lestyn Park, which has garnered 10 million views on [[YouTube]] as of March 21, 2023.<ref>{{cite web |last=Staff |date=21 March 2023 |title=VEEZE shares new video single "GOMD" |url=https://remixdmagazine.com/detroit-rapper-veeze-shares-new-video-single-gomd/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230328225008/https://remixdmagazine.com/detroit-rapper-veeze-shares-new-video-single-gomd/ |archive-date=28 March 2023 |access-date=18 January 2024 |publisher=RD Magazine}}</ref>

On June 27, 2023, he released his debut [[studio album]], ''[[Ganger (album)|Ganger]]''. It features collaborations with [[Lucki]], Babyface Ray, [[Icewear Vezzo]], [[Lil Yachty]], and [[Lil Uzi Vert]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2228487/stream-detroit-rapper-veezes-new-album-ganger-feat-lil-uzi-vert-lil-yachty-babyface-ray-more/music/|title=Detroit Rapper Veeze Releases New Album 'Ganger' Feat. Lil Uzi Vert, Lil Yachty, Babyface Ray|last=Tom|first=Breihan|date=June 27, 2023|website=Stereogum|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> The album received widespread critical acclaim, with several critics ranking it among the best albums of 2023.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://hiphopdx.com/reviews/veeze-ganger-album-review|title=Veeze ''Ganger'' Album Review|website=[[HipHopDX]]|last=Srour|first=Yousef|date=August 11, 2023|access-date=December 18, 2023}}</ref> It debuted at number 97 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]].<ref>{{cite magazine|url =https://www.billboard.com/lists/best-rap-albums-2023/|title=The 20 Best Rap Albums of 2023: Staff Picks|date=December 22, 2023|magazine = Billboard|access-date =January 18, 2024|archive-date = December 29, 2023|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20231229225930/https://www.billboard.com/lists/best-rap-albums-2023/|url-status = live}}</ref> According to ''[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]''{{'}}s Matthew Ritchie, Veeze's style is characterized by "vocal oddities and chuckle-inducing, stream-of-consciousness bars," with a dynamic range that "oscillates between laid-back joints fit for an evening smoke session."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/veeze-ganger/|title=Veeze: ''Ganger'' Album Review|website=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]|last=Ritchie|first=Matthew|date=July 5, 2023|access-date=December 18, 2023}}</ref>

''Ganger'' propelled Veeze to mainstream fame, ranking #16 on ''[[Complex Networks|Complex]]''{{'}}s Best Albums of 2023,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/a/j-mckinney/best-albums-2023|title=Best Albums of 2023: Doja Cat, Gunna, Drake, Bad Bunny|website=[[Complex Networks|Complex]]|last=Rose|first=Jordan|date=December 15, 2023|access-date=December 18, 2023}}</ref> #20 on ''[[The Fader]]''{{'}}s 50 Best Albums of 2023,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2023/12/12/best-albums-of-2023|title=The 50 best albums of 2023|website=[[The Fader]]|last=Helfand|first=Raphael|date=December 12, 2023|access-date=December 18, 2023}}</ref> and #39 on ''[[Rolling Stone]]''{{'}}s 100 Best Albums of 2023.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/best-albums-2023-1234879538/veeze-ganger-1234890245/|title=The 100 Best Albums of 2023|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|last=Reeves|first=Mosi|date=November 30, 2023|access-date=December 18, 2023}}</ref> Another successful album track, "Safe 2", also garnered attention. 

Additionally, Veeze featured on two subsequent releases: BigWorkDog's "Talk To Em" (August 31, 2023) and Desto Dubb's "Dead Homies" (September 29, 2023).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://remixdmagazine.com/new-music-desto-dubb-drops-new-track-dead-homies-ft-veeze/|title=New Music: Desto Dubb drops new track "Dead Homies" ft. Veeze|last=Staff|date=September 29, 2023|website=Remixd|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref>

In September 2023, Veeze launched his record label Navy Wavy in partnership with Warner Records.<ref>{{cite web |last=Andre |first=Ellington |date=16 September 2023 |title=Veeze Launches New Label with Warner Records |url=https://hiphopdx.com/news/veeze-label-warner-records-tour-single-video-safe-2-watch|url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231230052318/https://hiphopdx.com/news/veeze-label-warner-records-tour-single-video-safe-2-watch
 |archive-date=30 December 2023 |access-date=18 January 2024 |publisher=[[HipHopDX]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Aaron |first1=Williams |title=Veeze Celebrates His Warner Records Partnership And Navy Wavy Label Launch With A Video For 'Safe 2'|date=14 September 2023|url=https://uproxx.com/music/veeze-warner-records-partnership-safe-2-video/|website=[[Uproxx]]|access-date=19 January 2024 |archive-date=17 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117192639/https://uproxx.com/music/veeze-warner-records-partnership-safe-2-video/|url-status=live }}</ref> On October 13, 2023, a deluxe edition of ''Ganger'' was released, featuring five additional tracks.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Alexander |first1=Cole |title=Veeze Continues His Dominant Run With "Ganger (Deluxe Edition)"|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/723841-veeze-ganger-deluxe-edition|website=[[HotNewHipHop]]|date=October 13, 2023 |access-date=18 January 2024 |archive-date=19 October 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20231019172352/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/723841-veeze-ganger-deluxe-edition|url-status=live }}</ref> 
On December 1, 2023, Veeze released "ATL Freestyle 1 & 2", a dual single featuring guest appearances from [[Florida]]-based rapper [[Luh Tyler]] and [[Louisiana]]-based rapper [[Rob49]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thefader.com/2023/12/01/veeze-luh-tyler-rob49-atl-freestyle-1-2/amp|title=Veeze trades bars with Luh Tyler and Rob49 on " ATL Freestyle 1 & 2"|last=Raphael|first=Helfand|date=December 1, 2023|website=The Fader|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2244297/veeze-atl-freestyle-1-2-feat-luh-tyler-rob49/music/|title=Veeze – "ATL Freestyle 1 & 2" (Feat. Luh Tyler + Rob49)|last=Danielle|first=Chelosky|date=December 1, 2023|website=Stereogum|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref>

Veeze released singles "Pop Yo Shit" on May 30, 2024,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2265701/veeze-pop-yo-shit/music/|title=Veeze Shares New Single "Pop Yo Shit"|last=Tom|first=Breihan|date=May 30, 2024|website=Stereogum|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> and "F*cked A Fan" featuring [[Rylo Rodriguez]] on July 12, 2024,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://uproxx.com/music/veeze-rylo-rodriguez-fucked-a-fan-video/|title=Veeze & Rylo Rodriguez's 'F*cked A Fan' Is A Flashy Flex|last=Wongo|first=Okon|date=July 12, 2024|website=UPROXX|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> both accompanied by music videos.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/veeze-pop-yo-shit-video-1235696342/|title=Watch Veeze Take Over London in New Video 'Pop Yo Shit'|author=Angel Diaz|date=July 12, 2024|magazine=Billboard|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/veeze-rylo-rodriguez-f-a-f-video-fucked-a-fan-1235730043/|title=Veeze & Rylo Rodriguez Stunt Matching Cybertrucks in New Video 'F.A.F'|last=Angel|first=Diaz|date=July 7, 2024|magazine=Billboard|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> On July 25, 2024, Veeze announced that he will go on a tour in North America, titled ''Worst Tour Ever''. He noted that the tour will have support from [[Karrahbooo]], Wizz Havinn, and [[Star Bandz]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Matthew|first=Strauss |url=https://pitchfork.com/news/veeze-announces-the-worst-tour-ever/|title=Veeze Announces the Worst Tour Ever|website=Pitchfork|date=July 25, 2024|access-date=April 13, 2025}}</ref> Veeze was featured on Tye Beats' debut single "Wocky Sinner" on August 2, 2024,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/831184-veeze-ty-beats-wocky-sinner-stream|title=Veeze Links With Tye Beats For Producer's Debut Single "Wocky Sinner"|author=Zachary Horvath|date=August 5, 2024|website=HotNewHipHop|accessdate=September 29, 2024}}</ref> and Lil Yatchy's "[[Sorry Not Sorry (Lil Yachty and Veeze song)|Sorry Not Sorry]]" on August 16, 2024, released via [[Quality Control Music|Quality Control]] and [[Motown Records]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bernstein |first1=Elaina |title=Lil Yachty Continues His Run with "Sorry Not Sorry" Featuring Veeze |url=https://hypebeast.com/2024/8/lil-yachty-x-veeze-sorry-not-sorry-music-video-stream |website=Hypebeast |access-date=29 September 2024 |date=August 16, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Pointer |first1=Flisadam |title=Lil Yachty And Veeze Are Unapologetic About Their Lifestyles In The 'Sorry Not Sorry' Video |url=https://uproxx.com/music/lil-yachty-veeze-sorry-not-sorry-video/ |website=Uproxx |access-date=29 September 2024 |date=August 16, 2024}}</ref>

==Musical style==
Vantrees is noted for his frequent flows, energetic rapping, and cognizant tones, which he notably employed on his breakout single "GOMD".<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.thefader.com/2023/03/20/song-you-need-veeze-gomd/amp|title=Song You Need: Veeze's triumphant "GOMD"|magazine=[[The Fader]]|last=Jordan|first=Darville|date=March 30, 2023|access-date=February 29, 2024}}</ref> His sound has also been described as "melodic", albeit with a "mutter-croak" style.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2023/07/10/1186710247/veeze-ganger-interview|title=Veeze is just like us — except he's one of the best rappers alive|last=Mano|first=Sundaresan|date=July 10, 2023|website=[[NPR]]|access-date=February 29, 2024}}</ref> Chris Richards of ''[[The Washington Post]]'' newspaper stated that Veeze usually has a "wild and nonchalant" delivery in his verses.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/music/2023/06/30/veeze-ganger-album-review/|title=Wake up. Veeze just dropped the year's best rap album.|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|last=Chris|first=Richards|date=June 30, 2023|access-date=February 29, 2024}}</ref> While pursuing his career, Veeze listened to Street Lord'z and Doughboyz Cashout, two prominent groups from the city of Detroit.{{efn|The Detroit rapper jumped onto the hip-hop landscape in a big way this year and is looking to make himself a permanent fixture. (See [[#Career|career]].)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bet.com/article/qz35s8/veeze-ganger-interview-deluxe-detroit|title=Veeze's 'Ganger' Deluxe is An Extension of His Candidate for 2023's Best Rap Album|work=[[BET]]|last=Paul|first=Meara|date=October 13, 2023|access-date=February 29, 2024}}</ref>}}

