'''Gazzy Fabio Garcia'''<ref>{{Cite web |last=American Society of Composers, Authors and Publishers |date=April 1, 2024 |title=Gucci Gang |url=https://www.ascap.com/repertory#/ace/search/workID/893439571 |website=ASCAP Repertory}}</ref> (born August 17, 2000), known professionally as '''Lil Pump''', is an American rapper. He rose to prominence as part of the [[mumble rap|SoundCloud rap]] scene in the late 2010s, gaining distinction for his [[Minimalism|minimalist]] music and rambunctious public persona. Born and raised in [[Miami]], Florida, he gained mainstream attention following the release of his 2017 single "[[Gucci Gang]]", which peaked at number three on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] and preceded his [[Lil Pump (album)|self-titled debut studio album]], released by [[Warner Records]] in October of that year.

"Gucci Gang", despite being panned by critics, received [[RIAA certification|quintuple platinum]] certification by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] (RIAA),<ref name=platinum>{{cite web |title=Gucci Gang |url=https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=&ti=gucci+gang&lab=&genre=&format=&date_option=release&from=&to=&award=&type=&category=&adv=SEARCH#search_section |publisher=Recording Industry Association of America |access-date=September 28, 2018 |archive-date=September 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210926004729/https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=&ti=gucci+gang&lab=&genre=&format=&date_option=release&from=&to=&award=&type=&category=&adv=SEARCH#search_section |url-status=live }}</ref> while its parent album peaked at number three on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]]. In the following two years, he saw continued success with his singles "[[I Love It (Kanye West and Lil Pump song)|I Love It]]" (with [[Kanye West]] featuring [[Adele Givens]]), "[[Esskeetit]]", "[[Drug Addicts (song)|Drug Addicts]]", "[[Butterfly Doors]]", "[[Be Like Me]]" (featuring [[Lil Wayne]]), and "[[Welcome to the Party (Diplo, French Montana and Lil Pump song)|Welcome to the Party]]" (with [[Diplo]] and [[French Montana]] featuring [[Zhavia Ward]]) for the ''[[Deadpool 2]]'' soundtrack.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Margaritoff |first1=Marco |title=Watch Scenes From 'Deadpool 2' in Diplo and French Montana's "Welcome to the Party" Video |url=https://www.complex.com/music/2018/05/watch-scenes-deadpool-2-diplo-french-montana-welcome-to-the-party-video |access-date=February 3, 2019 |agency=Complex |date=May 21, 2018 |archive-date=February 4, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190204122345/https://www.complex.com/music/2018/05/watch-scenes-deadpool-2-diplo-french-montana-welcome-to-the-party-video |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Hot100">{{cite magazine |url=http://www.billboard.com/music/lil-pump/chart-history |title=Lil Pump – Chart History: Billboard Hot 100 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |access-date=October 30, 2017 |archive-date=January 1, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180101124811/https://www.billboard.com/music/lil-pump/chart-history |url-status=live }}</ref> His second album, ''[[Harverd Dropout]]'' (2019), peaked at number seven on the ''Billboard'' 200 and was panned by critics. His third album, ''[[Lil Pump 2]]'' (2023), was met with mixed critical reception and failed to chart in any known territory.

==Early life==
Gazzy Fabio Garcia was born on August 17, 2000, in Miami, Florida.<ref name="Happy Birthday, Lil Pump! - XXL"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.allmusic.com/artist/lil-pump-mn0003626782/biography|title=Lil Pump {{!}} Biography & History {{!}} AllMusic|website=AllMusic|access-date=November 7, 2018|archive-date=May 25, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190525051414/https://www.allmusic.com/artist/lil-pump-mn0003626782/biography|url-status=live}}</ref> In a 2018 interview with [[J. Cole]], Garcia stated that his parents are from [[Colombia]] and divorced when he was 6 years old.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 26, 2018 |title=J. Cole x Lil Pump Interview at The Sheltuh |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RNlQU-7y9Fo |website=J. Cole |publisher=YouTube |quote=Where your mom from? She from Miami too? (around 13:00) |access-date=November 27, 2022 |archive-date=June 9, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230609045815/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RNlQU-7y9Fo |url-status=live }}</ref>

When Garcia was 13 years old, his cousin, Lil Ominous, introduced him to Omar Pineiro, better known as [[Smokepurpp]]; the two eventually became collaborators. Garcia and Pineiro were expelled from multiple district schools. Garcia, thereafter, enrolled in an [[Opportunity school|opportunity high school]]<ref>[https://www.cde.ca.gov/sp/eo/oe/guide.asp Opportunity School, Class, and Program Guidelines].[[California Department of Education]].  [https://web.archive.org/web/20240718100346/https://www.cde.ca.gov/sp/eo/oe/guide.asp  Archived] from the original on July 18, 2024.</ref> but was expelled in the tenth grade for fighting and inciting a [[riot]].<ref>{{Citation|first=Cole |last=Bennett|title=Smokepurpp & Lil Pump: The Lyrical Lemonade Interview|date=September 18, 2016|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aDq88uhypL4&t=12s| archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211117/aDq88uhypL4| archive-date=November 17, 2021 | url-status=live|access-date=December 30, 2017 |via=[[YouTube]]}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

==Career==

=== Career beginnings, rising popularity, and ''Lil Pump'' (2015–2017) ===
{{Main|Lil Pump (album)}}

Garcia's rap career began when Smokepurpp produced a track and asked him to [[Freestyle rap|freestyle]] over it.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://gulf-times.com/story/578039|title=Lil Pump, hottest American Rapper|date=January 14, 2018|website=Gulf-Times|language=ar|access-date=February 25, 2019|archive-date=February 27, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190227182046/https://www.gulf-times.com/story/578039|url-status=live}}</ref> The single produced was independently released in 2016 on the music streaming website [[SoundCloud]], as his debut single, "Lil Pump".<ref name="Turner 2017">{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/features/look-at-me-the-noisy-soundcloud-revolution-changing-rap-w485101|title=Look At Me!: The Noisy, Blown-Out SoundCloud Revolution Redefining Rap|magazine=Rolling Stone|access-date=October 10, 2017|date=June 1, 2017|first=David|last=Turner|archive-date=August 11, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170811190348/http://www.rollingstone.com/music/features/look-at-me-the-noisy-soundcloud-revolution-changing-rap-w485101|url-status=live}}</ref> Garcia quickly followed the song with singles "Elementary", "Ignorant", "Gang Shit", and "Drum$tick", each garnering over three million streams.<ref name="soundcloud.com">{{cite web|url=https://soundcloud.com/wavey-hefner/tracks|title=Lil Pump's Soundcloud profile|publisher=SoundCloud|access-date=November 19, 2017|archive-date=December 10, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171210234214/https://soundcloud.com/wavey-hefner/tracks|url-status=live}}</ref> The success of his tracks on SoundCloud earned him recognition among the [[South Florida]] [[underground rap]] scene, in a style known as "[[SoundCloud rap]]". He co-headlined the No Jumper tour in 2016, and also performed at the [[Rolling Loud]] Festival.<ref name="HNHH">{{cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/72799-everything-you-need-to-know-about-lil-pump-news|title=Everything You Need To Know About Lil Pump|last=Schwartz|first=Danny|date=April 26, 2017|access-date=July 15, 2017|publisher=HotNewHipHop|archive-date=August 21, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200821034713/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/everything-you-need-to-know-about-lil-pump-news.31687.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="XXLChief">{{cite magazine|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/05/chief-keef-lil-pump-hit-the-studio/|title=Chief Keef and Lil Pump Hit the Studio|last=Berry|first=Peter A.|date=May 18, 2017|access-date=July 15, 2017|magazine=[[XXL (magazine)|XXL Mag]]|archive-date=December 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191204163705/https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/05/chief-keef-lil-pump-hit-the-studio/|url-status=live}}</ref> While rising in popularity and releasing underground hits, Pump would also meet other rappers like [[XXXTentacion]], [[Ski Mask the Slump God]], [[Fat Nick]] and [[Pouya]].

Garcia began gaining attention in 2017 by releasing the singles "[[D Rose (song)|D Rose]]" and "[[Boss (Lil Pump song)|Boss]]", which were major hits on SoundCloud, collecting a combined 70 million streams.<ref name="soundcloud.com"/> The popularity of "D Rose" led to a music video being produced by Chicago-based director [[Cole Bennett]], also known as Lyrical Lemonade.<ref>{{Citation|first=Cole|last=Bennett|title=Lil Pump – D Rose (Shot by @_ColeBennett_)|date=January 30, 2017|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Prfg3vyZkA| archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211117/6Prfg3vyZkA| archive-date=November 17, 2021 | url-status=live|access-date=October 10, 2017|via=[[YouTube]]}}{{cbignore}}</ref> On June 9, 2017, Garcia signed a record deal with Tha Lights Global and [[Warner Records]], just two months before his seventeenth birthday.<ref>{{cite web |last=Schwartz |first=Danny |date=June 9, 2017 |title=Lil Pump Signs To Warner Bros Records Imprint Tha Lights Global |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/lil-pump-signs-to-warner-bros-records-news.33541.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220819113221/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/lil-pump-signs-to-warner-bros-records-news.33541.html |archive-date=August 19, 2022 |access-date=November 19, 2017 |publisher=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> However, in January 2018, his contract with Warner Bros. Records was voided because he had been a minor at the time of signing.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/lil-pump-free-agent-contract-voided-warner-bros/|title=Lil Pump Now Free Agent, Contract Voided With Warner Bros.|magazine=Billboard|date=November 1, 2018|first=Colin|last=Stutz|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=January 12, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180112035939/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8094047/lil-pump-free-agent-contract-voided-warner-bros|url-status=live}}</ref>

In July 2017, Garcia announced on [[Twitter]] that his debut mixtape was in the works and would be released in August.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/07/lil-pump-announces-debut-project-dropping-august/|title=Lil Pump Announces Debut Project Dropping in August – XXL|website=XXL Mag|date=July 22, 2017|access-date=November 19, 2017|archive-date=October 11, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171011223245/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/07/lil-pump-announces-debut-project-dropping-august/|url-status=live}}</ref> However, the album was not released in August and was pushed back. He instead released the song "[[Gucci Gang]]", which became his first ''Billboard'' Hot 100 entry, peaking at number three on November 8, 2017.<ref name="Hot100"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/09/lil-pump-billboard-hot-100-chart/|title=Lil Pump Lands His First Song on the Billboard Hot 100 Chart – XXL|website=XXL Mag|date=September 2017|access-date=November 1, 2017|archive-date=October 9, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171009082510/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/09/lil-pump-billboard-hot-100-chart/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/video/early-release-billboard-hot-100-top-10-november-18th-2017-countdown-official-8023256|title=Early Release! Billboard Hot 100 Top 10 November 18th 2017 Countdown – Official|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=November 6, 2017|access-date=November 19, 2017|archive-date=November 9, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171109094548/http://www.billboard.com/video/early-release-billboard-hot-100-top-10-november-18th-2017-countdown-official-8023256|url-status=live}}</ref> The song was [[Recording Industry Association of America certification|certified gold and platinum]] by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] on January 11, 2018, and as of July 31, 2018, is certified triple platinum.<ref name=platinum/> At age 17, Garcia rose to fame after he released his debut studio album, ''[[Lil Pump (mixtape)|Lil Pump]]'' (released October 6, 2017), featuring [[Smokepurpp]], [[Gucci Mane]], [[Lil Yachty]], [[Chief Keef]], [[Rick Ross]], and [[2 Chainz]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/10/listen-lil-pump-debut-album/|title=Listen to Lil Pump's Self-Titled Debut Album – XXL|website=XXL Mag|date=October 2017|access-date=November 1, 2017|archive-date=May 10, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180510161946/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2017/10/listen-lil-pump-debut-album/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===''Harverd Dropout'' (2018–2019)===
{{Main|Harverd Dropout}}
[[File:Lil Pump Icebox 2018 (cropped).jpg|thumb|Garcia in 2018|249x249px]]

Garcia released "[[I Shyne]]" on January 18, 2018, with producer [[Carnage (DJ)|Carnage]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://pigeonsandplanes.com/music/2018/01/lil-pump-carnage-i-shyne|title=Lil Pump Connects With Carnage for Wild New Song "i Shyne"|work=PigeonsandPlanes|date=January 2018|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=April 8, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180408211531/https://pigeonsandplanes.com/music/2018/01/lil-pump-carnage-i-shyne|url-status=live}}</ref> Following reports that he had left his former labels Tha Lights Global and [[Warner Records]] and the contract had been voided on the grounds of him being a minor when he signed,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/01/lil-pump-out-of-warner-bros-deal/|title=Lil Pump's Warner Bros. Records Contract Voided – XXL|website=XXL Mag|date=January 11, 2018|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=April 20, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180420085510/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/01/lil-pump-out-of-warner-bros-deal/|url-status=live}}</ref> competition rose to sign Garcia with offers reportedly as high as $8 million<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://variety.com/2018/music/news/lil-pumps-warner-bros-contract-voided-gucci-gang-rapper-is-entertaining-other-offers-1202662829/|title=Lil Pump's Warner Bros. Contract Voided, 'Gucci Gang' Rapper Is Entertaining Other Offers|last=Aswad|first=Jem|date=January 12, 2018|work=Variety|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=March 31, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190331122035/https://variety.com/2018/music/news/lil-pumps-warner-bros-contract-voided-gucci-gang-rapper-is-entertaining-other-offers-1202662829/|url-status=live}}</ref> to $12 million or more,<ref name=":1">{{cite news|title=It's Official: 'Gucci Gang' Rapper Lil Pump Re-Signs With Warner Bros. Records|url=https://variety.com/2018/music/news/lil-pump-gucci-gang-rapper-record-contract-warner-bros-records-1202722117/|access-date=March 14, 2018|work=Variety|date=March 13, 2018|archive-date=April 17, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180417040328/http://variety.com/2018/music/news/lil-pump-gucci-gang-rapper-record-contract-warner-bros-records-1202722117/|url-status=live}}</ref> and artists such as [[Gucci Mane]] and [[DJ Khaled]] taking interest in him.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/gucci-mane-lil-pump-1017-records-instagram-comment-8094724/|title=Gucci Mane Wants to Sign Lil Pump: 'U Name the Price'|magazine=Billboard|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412093159/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8094724/gucci-mane-lil-pump-1017-records-instagram-comment|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/01/dj-khaled-sign-lil-pump-we-the-best-music/|title=DJ Khaled Wants to Sign Lil Pump to We the Best Music – XXL|website=XXL Mag|date=January 25, 2018|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=February 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190207135112/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/01/dj-khaled-sign-lil-pump-we-the-best-music/|url-status=live}}</ref> Garcia sparked rumors he had signed with Gucci Mane's imprint, [[1017 Records]], in February 2018.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://hiphop-n-more.com/2018/02/breakout-rapper-lil-pump-signs-with-gucci-manes-1017-label/|title=Breakout Rapper Lil Pump Signs With Gucci Mane's 1017 Label|date=February 10, 2018|work=HipHop-N-More|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=February 17, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190217020524/http://hiphop-n-more.com/2018/02/breakout-rapper-lil-pump-signs-with-gucci-manes-1017-label/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/02/lil-pump-rumors-he-signed-gucci-manes-1017-eskimo-records/|title=Lil Pump Sparks Rumors He Signed to Gucci Mane's Label – XXL|website=XXL Mag|date=February 11, 2018|access-date=February 15, 2018|archive-date=February 17, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190217103725/http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/02/lil-pump-rumors-he-signed-gucci-manes-1017-eskimo-records/|url-status=live}}</ref> Nonetheless, he re-signed another contract with Tha Lights Global and [[Warner Records]] for $8 million on March 12.<ref name=":1" />

On April 13, 2018, Garcia released the single "[[Esskeetit]]", which debuted and peaked at number 24 on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100.<ref name="Hot100" /> On May 24, 2018, Garcia performed "Esskeetit" on ''[[The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon]]'' on May 24.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbc.com/the-tonight-show/guests/lil-pump/385f9ef7a6ceb63abd43ef50a24c03956d7e987d|title=Lil Pump on The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon|website=NBC|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=May 23, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190523165836/https://www.nbc.com/the-tonight-show/guests/lil-pump/385f9ef7a6ceb63abd43ef50a24c03956d7e987d|url-status=live}}</ref> In June, he was featured on [[XXL Freshmen Class]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 12, 2018 |title=2018 XXL Freshmen Class: We got clout |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/2018-xxl-freshman-cover/ |website=XXL |access-date=November 30, 2022 |archive-date=April 27, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200427113213/https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2018/06/2018-xxl-freshman-cover/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In July that year, he released the single "[[Drug Addicts (song)|Drug Addicts]]" alongside a music video which features the actor [[Charlie Sheen]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.nme.com/news/charlie-sheen-appears-in-lil-pumps-latest-video-2350641 |title=Charlie Sheen appears in Lil Pump's latest video |date=July 5, 2018 |website=NME |access-date=July 6, 2018 |archive-date=July 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180706104401/https://www.nme.com/news/charlie-sheen-appears-in-lil-pumps-latest-video-2350641 |url-status=live }}</ref> On September 7, 2018, Garcia collaborated with [[Kanye West]] and comedian [[Adele Givens]] for the track "[[I Love It (Kanye West and Lil Pump song)|I Love It]]".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.complex.com/music/2018/09/kanye-west-lil-pump-i-love-it-video-memes |title=Kanye and Lil Pump's "I Love It" Video Gets the Memes It Was Made to Inspire |first=Joshua |last=Espinoza |date=September 7, 2018 |access-date=September 8, 2018 |work=Complex |archive-date=September 8, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180908054411/https://www.complex.com/music/2018/09/kanye-west-lil-pump-i-love-it-video-memes |url-status=live }}</ref> The track went straight to number 1 on the [[Canadian Hot 100]].<ref>{{cite magazine |title=The week of September 22, 2018 |url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/canadian-hot-100/2018-09-22 |magazine=Billboard |access-date=September 28, 2018 |archive-date=September 18, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180918125118/https://www.billboard.com/charts/canadian-hot-100/2018-09-22 |url-status=live }}</ref> Garcia announced in August 2018 a tour to promote his unreleased album ''[[Harverd Dropout]]'', but it was cancelled a month later due to "unforeseen circumstances".<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Lamarre |first1=Carl |title=Lil Pump Announces Harverd Dropout Tour |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/lil-pump-announces-harverd-dropout-tour-dates-8469997/ |access-date=September 30, 2018 |magazine=Billboard |date=August 14, 2018 |archive-date=December 25, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181225100659/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8469997/lil-pump-announces-harverd-dropout-tour-dates |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Aubrey |first1=Elizabeth |title=Lil Pump cancels his upcoming tour following legal issues |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/lil-pump-cancels-upcoming-tour-amid-legal-issues-2384898 |access-date=September 30, 2018 |work=NME |date=September 30, 2018 |archive-date=December 12, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181212005048/https://www.nme.com/news/music/lil-pump-cancels-upcoming-tour-amid-legal-issues-2384898 |url-status=live }}</ref> He released "[[Multi Millionaire (song)|Multi Millionaire]]" on October 5 as a single featuring [[Lil Uzi Vert]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/lil-pump-lil-uzi-vert-multi-millionaire-732828/ |title=Hear Lil Pump, Lil Uzi Vert Collide on Blistering 'Multi Millionaire' |last=Leight |first=Elias |date=October 5, 2018 |magazine=Rolling Stone |access-date=October 13, 2018 |archive-date=October 13, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181013172340/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/lil-pump-lil-uzi-vert-multi-millionaire-732828/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On October 25, [[dubstep]] producer [[Skrillex]] released the song "[[Arms Around You]]", which is a collaboration he made with Garcia, [[Maluma (singer)|Maluma]], [[Swae Lee]] and posthumous vocals from [[XXXTentacion]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/skrillex-teases-new-track-xxxtentacion-lil-pump-2393317 |title=Skrillex shares new collaborative track 'Arms Around You' with XXXTentacion, Lil Pump and more |work=NME |last=Daly |first=Rhian |date=October 25, 2018 |access-date=October 25, 2018 |archive-date=October 24, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024232003/https://www.nme.com/news/music/skrillex-teases-new-track-xxxtentacion-lil-pump-2393317 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On December 16, 2018, Garcia was accused of being racist towards Asians after previewing a snippet of his new song "[[Butterfly Doors]]"; the lyrics contained Asian stereotypes and slurs including "[[Ching chong]]" and "they call me [[Yao Ming]] cause my eyes real low". While rapping the latter lyric, Garcia mockingly [[epicanthic fold|pulled his eyes back]]. This generated negative press coverage and caused Chinese rappers, including [[CD Rev]] and [[Awkwafina]], to release [[Diss (music)|diss tracks]] against him.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://people.com/music/lil-pump-slammed-anti-asian-racial-slurs-butterfly-doors/|title=Awkwafina Slams Lil Pump for Using Anti-Asian Racial Slurs in New Track, 'Butterfly Doors'|website=PEOPLE.com|access-date=December 18, 2018|archive-date=November 26, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201126160127/https://people.com/music/lil-pump-slammed-anti-asian-racial-slurs-butterfly-doors/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.scmp.com/news/china/society/article/2178577/chinese-rappers-hit-back-lil-pumps-racial-slurs-amid-backlash|title=Chinese rappers hit back at Lil Pump's racial slurs amid backlash|date=December 18, 2018|website=South China Morning Post|access-date=December 18, 2018|archive-date=November 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112011040/https://www.scmp.com/news/china/society/article/2178577/chinese-rappers-hit-back-lil-pumps-racial-slurs-amid-backlash|url-status=live}}</ref> On December 24, he uploaded an apology video on [[Instagram]] regarding the incident and later released the single with the offensive lyrics edited out.<ref>{{cite news |title=Lil Pump Apologizes For Using Racial Slurs: "I Got Asian Homies" |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/177196-lil-pump-apologizes-for-using-racial-slurs-i-got-asian-homies-news |access-date=December 26, 2018 |agency=hotnewhiphop |publisher=Rose Lilah |date=December 25, 2018 |archive-date=November 7, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201107235831/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/lil-pump-apologizes-for-using-racial-slurs-i-got-asian-homies-news.67633.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On February 21, 2019, Garcia released the song "[[Be Like Me]]" featuring [[Lil Wayne]]. A music video featuring both of the artists was also released.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Darville |first1=Jordan |title=Lil Wayne features on Lil Pump's new song "Be Like Me" |url=https://www.thefader.com/2019/02/21/lil-wayne-lil-pump-be-like-me |access-date=February 24, 2019 |agency=FADER |date=February 21, 2019 |archive-date=May 30, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190530091359/https://www.thefader.com/2019/02/21/lil-wayne-lil-pump-be-like-me |url-status=live }}</ref> He performed "Be Like Me" on ''[[Jimmy Kimmel Live!]]'' on February 25.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/jimmy-kimmel-live-lil-pump-performs-be-like-me-8500134/|title=Lil Pump Brings 'Pump University' to 'Kimmel' With Flashy 'Be Like Me' Performance: Watch|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=March 2, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190302214324/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/8500134/jimmy-kimmel-live-lil-pump-performs-be-like-me|url-status=live}}</ref> On February 22, he released his debut studio album ''Harverd Dropout'', featuring [[Kanye West]], [[Lil Wayne]], [[Offset (rapper)|Offset]], [[Quavo]], [[Smokepurpp]], [[Lil Uzi Vert]], [[2 Chainz]], and [[YG (rapper)|YG]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://genius.com/albums/Lil-pump/Harverd-dropout|title=Harverd Dropout by Lil Pump|website=Genius|language=en|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=August 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180819082847/https://genius.com/albums/Lil-pump/Harverd-dropout|url-status=live}}</ref> In the same year, he was featured on ''[[Forbes]]'' 30 under 30 list.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lil Pump |url=https://www.forbes.com/profile/lil-pump/ |website=Forbes |access-date=November 30, 2022 |archive-date=November 30, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221130024508/https://www.forbes.com/profile/lil-pump/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Subsequent ventures, ''No Name'', and ''Lil Pump 2'' (2020–present) ===
{{Main|Lil Pump 2}}

On February 13, 2020, Garcia stated on his Instagram story "I'm done doing music I quit..."; he did not elaborate.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2020/02/lil-pump-retiring-music-quit/|title=Lil Pump Says He's Retiring From Music|last=Centeno|first=Tony M.|website=XXL Mag|date=February 14, 2020|language=en|access-date=February 14, 2020|archive-date=April 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412094504/https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2020/02/lil-pump-retiring-music-quit/|url-status=live}}</ref> However days later he said on the same platform, "Yall Thought I Quit Bitch Im Back", and previewed a new song.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/2020/02/lil-pump-ends-extremely-brief-retirement|title=Lil Pump Ends Extremely Brief Retirement|website=Complex|access-date=February 21, 2020|archive-date=February 21, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200221202438/https://www.complex.com/music/2020/02/lil-pump-ends-extremely-brief-retirement|url-status=live}}</ref> He also announced that ''Lil Pump 2'' was the title of his upcoming album.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/298053-lil-pump-announces-new-album-news|title=Lil Pump Announces New Album|website=HotNewHipHop|date=April 28, 2020|access-date=June 28, 2020|archive-date=July 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200729222228/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/lil-pump-announces-new-album-news.109355.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On September 16, 2020, Garcia released a new single "Life Like Me" on his SoundCloud account. The song was first previewed in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/281255-lil-pump-finally-releases-life-like-me-new-song|title=Lil Pump Finally Releases "Life Like Me"|website=HotNewHipHop|date=September 16, 2020|access-date=September 16, 2020|archive-date=September 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200917000148/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/lil-pump-finally-releases-life-like-me-new-song.1988613.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Later that same year, Garcia released various [[SoundCloud]] exclusives including "Lil Pimp Big MAGA Steppin"<ref name="BillboardLilPimp">{{cite magazine|last1=Aniftos|first1=Rania|date=November 18, 2020|title=Lil Pump Unveils His Pro-Trump Anthem 'Lil Pimp Big MAGA Steppin'|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/lil-pump-lil-pimp-big-maga-steppin-9486234/|magazine=Billboard|access-date=November 30, 2020|archive-date=November 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201124215557/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/hip-hop/9486234/lil-pump-lil-pimp-big-maga-steppin/|url-status=live}}</ref> and "I'monna"''.''

In March 2021, Garcia went into partnership with the [[Non-fungible token|NFT]] marketplace ''Sweet'' to release a special NFT collection that includes digital jewelry and Lil Pump trading cards.<ref name="Cheddar 2021-03-23">{{cite news |url=https://cheddar.com/media/ceo-of-sweet-talks-nft-partnership-with-rapper-lil-pump |title=CEO of Sweet Talks NFT Partnership with Rapper Lil Pump |work=Cheddar |date=March 23, 2021 |access-date=March 29, 2021 |archive-date=November 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211110071812/https://cheddar.com/media/ceo-of-sweet-talks-nft-partnership-with-rapper-lil-pump |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Vulture 2021-03-18">{{cite news |url=https://www.vulture.com/2021/03/music-nft-projects-ranked.html |title=Musician NFT Projects, Ranked by How Many F's I Can Give |work=Vulture |date=March 18, 2021 |access-date=March 29, 2021 |archive-date=October 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008215755/https://www.vulture.com/2021/03/music-nft-projects-ranked.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="XXL Mag 2021-03-23-">{{cite news |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/rappers-nfts-hip-hop-non-fungible-tokens/ |title=Rappers and NFTs – How Hip-Hop Is Cashing In on Non-Fungible Tokens |work=XXL Mag |date=March 23, 2021 |access-date=March 29, 2021 |archive-date=November 20, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211120220727/https://www.xxlmag.com/rappers-nfts-hip-hop-non-fungible-tokens/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On August 12, 2021, "Racks to the Ceiling", featuring Canadian rapper [[Tory Lanez]], was released. On December 14, 2021, Garcia and producer [[Ronny J]] silently released a new album titled ''No Name''.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Blanchet|first1=Brenton|date=December 10, 2021|title=Lil Pump and Ronny J Team Up for 'Lil Pump and Ronny J 1.5' Album|url=https://www.complex.com/music/lil-pump-ronny-j-album|access-date=December 18, 2021|publisher=[[Complex Networks]]|archive-date=December 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211211152536/https://www.complex.com/music/lil-pump-ronny-j-album|url-status=live}}</ref> Unlike Garcia's previous release, ''Harverd Dropout'', the new album was never mentioned on social media, leading it to become a commercial failure.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Neaupane|first=Sushil|title=Lil Pump Dropped a New Album 'No Name' and It Didn't Make a Sound|url=https://hollywoodmask.com/p/lil-pump-new-album.html|access-date=January 30, 2022|website=hollywoodmask|date=December 19, 2021|language=en|archive-date=March 29, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230329004350/https://hollywoodmask.com/p/lil-pump-new-album.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On April 12, 2022, Garcia released the lead single for ''[[Lil Pump 2]]'', "All The Sudden". He released four more singles for the album after this, "Splurgin", "Mosh Pit", "She Know" and "Tesla". In March 2023, Pump would announce the cover and tracklist for his long-awaited second studio album ''Lil Pump 2'', which features [[guest appearance]]s from [[Smokepurpp]], [[YoungBoy Never Broke Again]], [[Ty Dolla Sign]], among others.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Premiere: Lil Pump and YoungBoy Never Broke Again Connect on "I Don't Mind" |url=https://www.complex.com/music/lil-pump-youngboy-i-dont-mind-stream |access-date=March 25, 2023 |website=Complex |language=en |archive-date=March 29, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230329111738/https://www.complex.com/music/lil-pump-youngboy-i-dont-mind-stream |url-status=live }}</ref> The album was released on March 17. Despite receiving more promotion on social media, the album was another commercial failure with the album failing to land on any chart. The deluxe version of ''Lil Pump 2'', was released on September 15, 2023, containing new tracks: "6 Rings", "Glow in the Dark", "I Sell" and "Rick Rubin".

==Personal life==
Garcia has said on social media and in lyrics that he has difficulty reading due to having [[dyslexia]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.vladtv.com/article/246104/lil-pump-says-he-cant-read-gucci-mane-book-will-be-his-first|title=Lil Pump Says He Can't Read, Gucci Mane Book Will Be His First|website=www.vladtv.com|date=June 9, 2018|access-date=May 31, 2019|archive-date=May 31, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190531180553/https://www.vladtv.com/article/246104/lil-pump-says-he-cant-read-gucci-mane-book-will-be-his-first|url-status=live}}</ref> In December 2020 during the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], he was banned from [[JetBlue]] after refusing to wear a mask on a flight from [[FLL|Fort Lauderdale]] to Los Angeles.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://thehill.com/blogs/in-the-know/in-the-know/531908-rapper-lil-pump-banned-from-jet-blue-after-refusing-to-wear-a |date=December 29, 2020 |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |title=Rapper Lil Pump banned from Jet Blue after refusing to wear a mask |access-date=December 29, 2020 |archive-date=December 29, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201229125045/https://thehill.com/blogs/in-the-know/in-the-know/531908-rapper-lil-pump-banned-from-jet-blue-after-refusing-to-wear-a |url-status=live }}</ref> On his [[Instagram]] account, Garcia said that his father Fabio Garcia died on April 8, 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 9, 2022 |title=Lil Pump's Dad Passed Away; Rap Star Mourns Loss |url=https://usdaynews.com/celebrities/sex-relationship/lil-pump-dad-passed-away/ |access-date=April 9, 2022 |website=US day News |language=en-CA |archive-date=April 9, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220409205820/https://usdaynews.com/celebrities/sex-relationship/lil-pump-dad-passed-away/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Politics===
On October 26, 2020, Garcia endorsed the [[Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign|campaign]] of President [[Donald Trump]] in the [[2020 United States presidential election]], citing [[Joe Biden]]'s tax plan as his reasoning.<ref>{{Cite news|date=October 26, 2020|title=The Countdown: Gen Z, why they vote and Amy v Hillary memes|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/election-us-2020-54689293|access-date=November 3, 2020|archive-date=November 3, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201103065415/https://www.bbc.com/news/election-us-2020-54689293|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=October 26, 2020|title=Lil Pump endorses Donald Trump for president, says "f*** sleepy Joe"|url=https://www.newsweek.com/lil-pump-rapper-donald-trump-endorsement-taxes-1542045|access-date=November 3, 2020|website=Newsweek|language=en|archive-date=October 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027103541/https://www.newsweek.com/lil-pump-rapper-donald-trump-endorsement-taxes-1542045|url-status=live}}</ref> Lil Pump has made posts on social media wearing the [[Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign|Trump campaign]]'s [[Make America Great Again]] baseball cap. On November 2, Trump brought Lil Pump out to speak at his rally in [[Michigan]], mistakenly calling him "Little Pimp".<ref>{{Cite web|date=November 2, 2020|title=Trump Invites Rapper Lil Pump to Final Rally, Calls Him 'Little Pimp'|url=https://variety.com/2020/music/news/lil-pump-trump-rally-little-pimp-1234822241/|access-date=November 2, 2020|website=Variety|language=en|archive-date=January 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210119171111/https://variety.com/2020/music/news/lil-pump-trump-rally-little-pimp-1234822241/|url-status=live}}</ref> Despite this endorsement, it was later revealed that Garcia was not a registered voter.<ref>{{Cite web|date=November 14, 2020|title=Trump supporter Lil Pump didn't actually register to vote in 2020 election|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/trump-supporter-lil-pump-didnt-actually-register-to-vote-in-2020-election-2816741|access-date=November 16, 2020|website=NME Music News, Reviews, Videos, Galleries, Tickets and Blogs {{!}} NME.COM|language=en-GB|archive-date=November 15, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201115174015/https://www.nme.com/news/music/trump-supporter-lil-pump-didnt-actually-register-to-vote-in-2020-election-2816741|url-status=live}}</ref> It was revealed that Garcia also deleted a 2016 [[Tweet (social media)|tweet]] of his that read "FUCK DONALD TRUMP" after it went viral on Twitter.<ref>{{Cite web|date=November 3, 2020|title=Lil Pump deletes 2016 tweet about Donald Trump after it resurfaces|url=https://www.dailydot.com/debug/lil-pump-donald-trump-deleted-tweet/|access-date=January 7, 2022|website=The Daily Dot|language=en-US|archive-date=January 7, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220107093945/https://www.dailydot.com/debug/lil-pump-donald-trump-deleted-tweet/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Garcia has continued his support for Trump in 2024. In August of that year, as part of [[Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign|Trump's 2024 presidential campaign]], Garcia announced plans to perform a [[Diss (music)|diss track]] against [[Kamala Harris]] at a Trump rally,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrews |first=Elias |date=August 10, 2024 |title=Lil Pump Will Perform Kamala Harris Diss Track At The Next Trump Rally |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/832795-lil-pump-perform-kamala-harris-diss-track-next-trump-rally-hip-hop-news |access-date=August 11, 2024 |website=HotNewHipHop |language=en}}</ref> although he later backtracked, instead announcing that he would write a song in support of Donald Trump rather than a diss track.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Walker |first=Clay |date=August 14, 2024 |title=MAGA Rapper Nixes His Plan to Record Harris Diss Track |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/maga-rapper-lil-pump-nixes-his-plan-to-record-kamala-harris-diss-track |access-date=August 30, 2024 |work=The Daily Beast |language=en}}</ref> In addition, he stated that he would have left the United States if Harris were to win the [[2024 United States presidential election]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Diaz |first=Angel |date=August 9, 2024 |title=Lil Pump Says He's Leaving America If Kamala Harris Becomes President |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/lil-pump-leaving-america-kamala-harris-beats-donald-trump-1235750587/ |access-date=August 11, 2024 |magazine=Billboard |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=August 8, 2024 |title=Lil Pump Leaving USA If 'Stupid Ass B****' Kamala Harris Wins Election |url=https://www.tmz.com/2024/08/08/lil-pump-leaving-united-states-harris-wins/ |access-date=August 11, 2024 |website=TMZ |language=en}}</ref>

In June 2025, Garcia arrived in [[Russia]], despite the ongoing [[Russian invasion of Ukraine]], and performed at a concert in Moscow with rapper [[Smokepurpp]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Martinez |first1=Laureen |title=Trump supporter Lil Pump finds new fans as he heads to Moscow for performance |url=https://www.irishstar.com/culture/entertainment/trump-supporter-lil-pump-moscow-35402586 |work=Irish Star |date=June 16, 2025}}</ref>

